      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>WARRINGTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE,</p>
        <p>RE-NAMING PEOPLE'S COLLEGE. Beamont School Headmistress. ----- INCREASED SALARY REFUSED.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Education Committee was held at the Town Hall on Monday evening under the presidency of Alderman Bennett. There were also present :—Miss Williams, Revs. Canon Morley Steven- son, N. Wilson. J. Yonge and J. Davies, Alderman Smethurst, Evans and Tinnion, Councillor Fulton Smith, Sankey, Clegg, Barton, Toole, Lord, Pemberton, Ellison, Ball, Wilkinson, Dickenson and Steel, Messrs. C. E. Parker, A. J. Jolley, G. H. K. King,don, C. S. Burgess, R. T. Fairclough, G. Walker, E. Plinston, J. Upson, T. P. Bradshaw, and C. Fl. Unsworth, J. Moore Murray (director of education), and J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer). THE LATE COUNCILLOR BROUGHT.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Mr. Harry Brough acknow- ledging the vote of condolence passed on the death of bis father (Councillor Walter Brough).</p>
        <p>A NEW MEMBER.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRAAN congratutated Mr. T. P. Bradshaw on his election as the representative of the Friendly Societies, and . expressed the hope that his association with the authority would be pleasant and mutually profitable. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>INCREASES OF SALARY.</head>
        <p>INCREASES OF SALARY.</p>
        <p>At a meeting Of the Finance Committee, it was decided to increase the salaries of the under-mentioned officials as follows :—Mr. R. B. Roberts, assistant , director, from £195 to £200; Mr. A. T. Elliott, stores clerk, from 2138 10s. to £143 103. ; Mr. I. C. Pharaoh, -higher education clerk, from £B7 103. to £94; Mr. R. Price, statistical clerk, from £32 10s. to .258 10s. ; Miss M. E. Austin, typist, from £52 to £5B 103. ; Mr. P. W. Smith, committee and minute clerk, from 10s. per week to £39; together with small advances to office. boys. Mr. JOLLEY in moving the adoption of the minutes said the, increases were of a routine character. Mr. Roberts, their very capable assistant director, would on April Ist. reach his maximum. The other increases were all in accordance with the practice which obtained in the various Corporation depattments. Alluding to a statement of the receipts and expenditure of the com- mittee for the nine months ended December 31st, with the estimates for the year, which had been submitted to the committee, Mr. Jolley said the actual receipts and expenditure were very close to the estimates. Councillor WtLicixsoN seconded and the minutes were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>FAIRFIELD SCHOOL. THE HEADMISTRESS OF THE UPPER DEPARTMENT.</head>
        <p>FAIRFIELD SCHOOL. THE HEADMISTRESS OF THE UPPER DEPARTMENT.</p>
        <p>The School Management Committee resolved that consideration of the filling of the vacancy caused by the resignation of the headmistress of the upper girls' department of the Fairfield school be deferred until the effect of the opening of the Beamont Council school upon that school can been seen. Canon STEVENSON formally moved and Councillor Fulton Smith seconded the confirmation of the minutes Mr. R. T. Faractotiox moved an amendment that the above minute be not confirmed. It seemed to him a very serious thing that the committee was going to allow the headmistress to leave the school and not make another appointment immediately. The Rev.J.Davras seconded and said they wanted new schools to supplement and not to supplant the existing schools. The committee, however, was opening a school and at the same time choking another one. He believed the inspector had suggested that the two schools at Fairfield should be formed into one. The returns, however, showed that the accommodation pro- vided at Fairfield was for 571 scholars and he did not think one head teacher should have to control a depart- ment with more than 300 children. Mr. JOLLEY was also in favour of the matter being reconsidered and something definite being arrived at. The inspectors had more than once suggested that the upper girls' school as a separate school should be done away with and combined with the existing Fairfield girls' school, the whole constituting one school which would be worked probably with greater efficiency and undoubtedly more economically. If. the School Management Committee were prepared to recommend that, he thought it would be very desirable that they should do so. It was not, however, fair to adopt a policy of drift which meant starving a department. Let them face the question fairly and arrive at a proper solution. Canon STEVENSON said it had been pressed upon them for some time by the inspectors that it would be ' desirable that the two schools under the same roof should be controlled by one head teacher, and that would no doubt be an economy. The Beamont school was about to be opened and there might, at all events for a time, be some effect upon the attendance at the surrounding schools. That would give the committee some guidance as to what was the wisest thing to do and therefore they thosght it was better not to take a hasty step and, by appointing a head teacher, to close the door to any subsequent reform in that _direction. The minute was withdrawn for further consideration. TEACHERS' SALARIES.</p>
        <p>The School Management Comrnittee recommended increases of salaries to teachers, in accordance with the committee's scale, to the total amount of £1,019. The increases were approved without comment. RE-NAMING PEOPLE'S COLLEGE.</p>
        <p>COMMITTEE'S SUGGESTION NOT APPROVED. No suggestion having been made to the Board of Education by the managers-of the Warrington British school as to the change in the title of the school, the Board have requested the committee to forward them a proposal, and it was resolved by the School Manage- ment Committee, that it is suggested to the Board of Education that the Warrington British school be, in future, called the" Warrington Brewtnall school." The DIRECTOR read the following letter :—People's College, Warrington, 21st January, 1907. It is greatly to be regretted thq through some misconception as to the urgency of the matter,the committee of the People's College have omitted to submit a new name for the one by which the institutionhas been known for half a century. The foundation mangers in their opposition to the name proposed (Brewtnall) as singularly undesirable, have also to express their dislike to any name of a personal character and unanimousily suggest as the best possible substitnto "the Warrington Arpley-street schools," this being a name by which the schools are already well-known and is descriptive of their situation. Yours truly, C. J A.CKSON HOLMES, correspondent. Alderman TINNION said that on behalf of the managers of People's College, he moved that the committee's recommendation be not confirmed. Unfortunately the managers did not take up the matter sufficiently early to deal with it themselves. It was overlooked owing to a meeting of the managers being called for tire same night as a Council meeting, thus preventing some managers attending. The managers, of course would like to retain the old name of People' College but they understood that was • almos impossible. Therefore, while not specially object- ing to the name " Brewtnall " being given to the school, they would rather not have the name of any particular individual attached to the school. They might suggest other names which in the opinion of the managers were more likely to give universal satisfaction than the one proposed by the committee,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>INFLUENZA'S RAVAGES.</head>
        <p>INFLUENZA'S RAVAGES.</p>
        <p>All Quickly Repaired by Using Mother Seigel's Syrup.</p>
        <p>Beware of the after effects of influenza! There is no more weakening disease, and even when you have been " cured" of it your troubles are not, by any means, at an end. The influenza may be gone, truly, but what has it left behind? A broken constitution, a system so weak that it is powerless to resist disease. The fever of influenza has consumed the very material, the nerve and tissues, of your body, and unless that loss be made good, worse, much worse, must follow. The first thing to consider is your digestion. Never forget that all strength comes from food (there is no other way it can come), that your whole body is built up from food, and that this food must be digested or it twill not yield the nourishment it contains. Therefore your stomach, liver and bowels must he restored to full natural activity, so that you may be able to eat and to digest what you have eaten. And for this purpose there is no restorative like Mother Seigel's Syrup. It strengthens every organ of digestion, cleanses your system of all impurities ard gives it a fresh start. As bearing on these facts. we quote the following from Mrs. M. E. T. Beadle, 54, East Hill, Dartford,Kent. "It was after an attack of influenza," she says, "that I began taking Mother Seigel's Syrup. My digestion was very weak and irregular, I was bilious, all out of sorts, and very weak. I had always a bad taste in my mouth, with my tongue much coated, and often I had a dizzy sensation and felt as though I was falling. My appetite was poor, and what I did eat caused me such pain at the chest. 1 had also a violent pain all down my back, velich scarcely ever left me. Finding no relief from the usual remedies I got a bottle of Mother Seigel 's Syrup. I soon found relief then. The pain in my back ceased, and I slept soundly. My appetite became natnral, and altogether the Syrup completely restored my health."</p>
        <p>After influenza or any other wasting disease take Mother Seigel's Syrup. It builds up your strength, cleanses your system and restores the vigour and bloom of heath to the enfeebled betty</p>
