 Brewtnall examination of the children in the public elementary laid the foundations of the school and if it was to be schools in the Boroueh reported to him by the called after any individual he thought the "Brewtnall head teachers as being mentally or physically defective, School" was the most fitting title. - ! was received, and after some discussion, it was Mr. UNSWORTH said that as another old pupil he would prefer to speak of the school in Mr. Hayden's time. But after all, what was in a name? He would like the school to continue to be called People's College, but failing that he was in favour of the com- mittee's recommendation, as Mr. Brewtnall was the first master. Mr. KINGDON said he had no doubt adarge number of gentlemen present had at one time or another received impressions from these headmasters (Laughter.) What the impressions were they need not inquire. (Laughter.) He thought some uniform system of naming schools should be adopted and he preferred to call it by the locality. Mr. UPSON said they did not honour schoolmasters while they were living and it was something gained if they honoured them twenty-five years after their death. (Laughter.) He supported the minutes. Councillor BARTON supported the committee's recom- mendation. Mr. R. T. FAIRCLOUGH was in favour of acceding to the request of the managers as there ought to be give and take between the parties. If the committee took notice of the managers' suggestion the manages would Ibe more likely to fail in with any suggestions the com- mittee made to them. The CHAIRMAN said he was an old People's College boy and it was difficult to describe his feelings when he heard the suggestion that the name should be changed. He was inclined to think that the interference of the Board of Education in these matters wss a wee bit pedantic. (Hear, hear.) If carried to its logical conclusion they would have to do like the I Americans did with their streets,and call the schools by numbers. Logically there Was something to be said against the appellation of "People's College " but the sentimental associations outweighed the arguments on the other side. To him the school would be People's College to the end of the chapter. If they adopted a personal name, Brewtnall was perhaps the most appro- priate. He thought, however, that it would only be bare courtesy to consider the managers in the matter. On a vote being taken, there voted : For the Amendment. Against. Rev. John Yonge The Mayor „ James Davies Councillor Fulton Smith Alderman Evans , 7 Bartcn „ Tinnion 1, Lord Councillor Steel f Pemberton I „ Sankey 22 Elliscn -- Clegg , Toole , Mr. C. te;.Parker ' Mr. A. .T. Jolley ' „ G. Walker „ H. Roberts „ E. Plinston „ .1. Upson „ H. Kingdon „ C. H. Unsworth „ R. T. Fairclough —ll „ T. P. Bradshaw —l3 The amendment was therefore carried. BEAMONT SCHOOL. THE HEADNIISI'RESe'S SALARY., At a meeting of the School Management Committee a letter was submitted from Miss Mary Taylor, in refer- ence to the amount of her commencing salary as head mistress of the Girls' department of the Beamont Council school, which will be less than at present received by her as headmistress of the Upper Girls' department of the Fairfield school. It was resolved that the terms of the advertisement so far as it affects the commencing salary offered for this appointment be adhered to. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY moved that the resolution be not confirmed, because it appeared to him to involve a departure from a principle which"- had governed the actions of the committee up to the present time. When , the authority came into existence it was laid down that, no person employed in any school and who was trans- ferred to another school should suffer financially by the change. He understood the present transfer would involve Miss Taylor in some loss and he hoped the com- mittee would loyally adhere to the principle he had mentioned. Mr. UPSON seconded, and said that by moving to the new school Miss Taylor would lose £lO por annum. It was stated on the scale of salaries that if a teacher was transferred to another school he or she should not suffer financially. It might be said that in the present case, it was not a transfer but a new appointment. It seemed to him that it was an attempt to take advantage to the extent of2lo of a teacher who was apparently helpless:rho head teachers at Fairfield school were aware that it was extremely probable that on the advice of the Inspector some change would take place, and that their tenure was somewhat fragile. Naturally enough, Miss Taylor, seeing the opportunity of making her position secure, applied for the new appointment not because she par4icularly desired to remove from Fairfield but because she knew that in all probability she or her colleague would shortly have to go. A teacher who had served the town as long as Miss Taylor ought not to be asked to suffer to the extent of £lO per annum. It was a mere paltry sum raved at the expense of the teacher and it waa to a certain extent taking advantage of her position. tier school was one of the best, having received the highest reports and grants, and the committee might be asked to stretch, a point. Councillor PEMBERTON pointed out that this was not a transfer but a new appointment, and ifiNliss Taylor's request was grented other applicants would be entitled to the same consideration. He ventured to think that if Mr. Wallington Wad applied he would have obtained the heedinastership and, according to Mr. Upson, the committee would have been asked to pay him £4OO a year. If the committee had requested Miss Taylor to make the change, it would have been a different matter. Mr. ROBERTS said he found .himself ia the unique position of entirely agreeing in word, sense and argument with Councillor Pemberton. (Laughter.) The: committee were asked to do one of the most nnbusiness- like things he ever heard suggested. The proposals i coming as it did from Mr. Jolley, one of the more: logically minded and usually level headed men that the knew, astonished him all the more. (Laughter.)l The committee had properly decided that i head teachers should not suns pscuaiarily by , being transferred to other schools, bat this was not the , case of a transfer. ' Mr. JOLLF:Y : What is it ? Mr. ROBERTS: I arn quite certain you yourself in 'senr innermost mind would not contend for one eo,oooth part of a second that it is a transfer. (Lairehter.) How can it be a transfer when it is a new appointment to a new school, thrown open to the whole teaching profession? Continuing Mr. Roberts repudiated the suggestion that there was any attempt to take advan- tage of a teacher.lle considered it 4 was an attempt on the part of the teacher to take advaertage of the committee. The question was not raised until after the appointment had been advertised and the selection made. Mr. JOLLEY : Mr. Roberts seems to be suggesting that lam inspired by Miss Taylor in the matter. 1 never spoke to her, but move the amendment on my own initiative. . Mr. ROBERTS said he knew Mr. Jolley too well to believe that he was the sort of man to be button-holed on any question that did not commend itself to his judgment. That was what puzzled him all the more. (Laughter.) He objected to the suggestion that there was any departure from correct and honourable business principles on the part of the committee. Presumably Mimi Taylor would fully understand the terms on which she was to apply, but she was seeking in a roundabout way to take advantage of the committee by appealing to sentiment on the question of a rule applying to other circumstances. If the school was anything like the success the committee anticipated it would be, her position in the future would be far better.</p>
        <p>resolved (a) That the head teachers be instructed to furnish the committee with a report upon all the children who have been examined by the medical adviser, showing, as far as possible, to what extent the defects pointed out at that examination have been or are being attended to, and how far the remedies suggested are being used ; (b) that the head teachers be instructed to keep a record of all such defective children in the schools in such a way as to to show from time to time the progress made in the condition of the children; and (c) that the medical adviser of the committee be instructed to pa) periodical visits to the schools (at no greater interval than throe months in the case of each school) for the purpose of examining any eefective children who may have been admitted in the meantime, and of seeing that the efforts made to improve the condition of the children are being maintained. In regard to the mentally defective children, further con- sideration of the matter was deferred to a meeting to be held on the 25th January, in order that the medical adviser may fully consider each case and classify them so that the committee may be better able to determine what further action is needed." Mr. ROBERTS asked when the medical ()Meer was to receive recognition of a financial character for the extra work?</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT: It is under consideration at the present time.</p>
        <p>THE RAIN IN THE WOODS.</p>
        <p>The lamentable effects of the general destruc- tion of forests are now sufficiently recognised in all civilised countries. Such destruction invites devastating floods in mountain streams and causes the surrounding land to dry up. Forests act like vast condensing screens. They preserve a moist atmosphere about them, attenuate the force of torrential 'downfalls, promote a more i gradual melting of the snow n early spring, and. protect the soil against too rapid evaporation. And yet the manner in which forests act their beneficent part i+s not exactly such as might be supposed. They prevent a large ,part of the rain that falls from reaching the soil at all. It is esti- mated that in European climates the forests evaporate directly, or transpire physiologically, four-fifths of the rain that falls upon them. Thus the forest atmosphere is no less important than the forest soil in edualising the . climatic condi- tions of a country.</p>
        <p>oeneves, z gent.rat Walt int tmiaais. store the lustre of a " dead " pearl the outer I tarnished envelopes may be removed with acids.</p>
        <p>ATOMIC WEIGHT OF SILVER.</p>
        <p>One cannot be too often reminded of the fact that in science it is the little things, sometimes the littlest of things, that are frequently of the greatest importance. An instance is furnished by the redetermination, upon the invitation of the International Committee on Atomic Weights, of the atomic weight of silver. The work was done by P. A. Guy() and G. Ter-Gazian, who, after many experiments, have concluded that the atomic weight of silver should be expressed by the number 107'89, instead of 10793, the number heretofore employed. This apparently insignifi- cant change demands a corresponding alteration in the figures representing the atomic weights of many other, substances, and by increasing the exactitude of chemical knowledge it may result in future discoveries of great importance.</p>
        <p>tne lower jaw acing strong aria wen stitpea, rendering the man " underhung," a character frequently remarked in criminals, especially in men, being found, according to Pell, in about thirty-eight out of every 100. The ears are a re- markable feature. They were asymmetrical, the left being normal, but the right was very pithe- coid in appearance, the absence of helix and the general flatness and incomplete development of the cartilaginous folds being very noticeable., The cars were not much separated from the head, and were thus not the "handle ears' so often seen in criminals and lunatics, but they were long and had the Ipbule rather adherent— the so-called "sessile ear'"—which, with the flat- tened concha, is regarded by criminal anthro- pologists as suspicious. The nose was straight„ but very deep at the root and deviated to the side—also characters of the criminal nose, accord- ing to Lombroso.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>NO BETTER PROOF.</head>
        <p>NO BETTER PROOF.</p>
        <p>Time tries all things, and it is wonderfully encourag- ing to find that good news, which we scarcely dared believe, is confirmed after five years, more especially when the news is of great importance to so many of. us. "Five years ago Doan's Backache Kidney Pills cured me of kidney disorders, and I have had no return of the troubles," says Mrs. M. Delooze, 13, Dolman's-lane, Warrington. "I have had a number of people ask me about my cure, and I have always told them what benefit I received from Doan's pills. Mine is indeed a lasting ctre." Here are the facts of Mrs.. Delooze's cure which she gave us some years ago :—For several years I was sub- ject to kidney complaint, and at times my back was so bad that I could hardly walk. There were dreadful pains round my loins, and I used to come over giddy. My limbs ached and swelled, I seemed full of Wind, and my kidneys were disordered. " I could get nothing to do me any good until I used Doan's Backache Kidney Pills. After taking some of these I felt so much better that I was encouraged to keep on with the medicine, and' became all right again. I always keep some of the pills by me, and if I feel the slightest pain I take a dose before going to bed, and the pain is gone by the morning. (Signed), MARY DELOOZg."—Doan's Backache Kidney Pills are two shillings and ninepence per box (six boxes for thirteen shillings and ninepence.) Of all chemists and stores or post free, direct from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells- street, Oxford-street, London, W. Deans are the pills that cured Mrs. Delooze.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>EVILS OF MOUTH BREATHING</p>
        <p>Dr. C. P. LinhaA states that many cases of chronic pharyngitis (throat inflammation) are due to mouth-breathing. Even later in life, when the bones of the mouth and nose are com- pletely ossified, tile openings of the nose 1)&amp;4: and front could be enlarged by proper breathillez exercises. The following rules are recommended for -keeping the nose in a normal condition: Coot living rooms, well ventilated sleeping rooms,i, cold bath, especially for the neck and spine, dry clothing, simple foods, regular habits, moderate daily exercise and breathing through the nose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>SLEEPING OUTDOQRS.</head>
        <p>SLEEPING OUTDOQRS.</p>
        <p>Sleeping out is not the modern thing that many people belie-co it to • be. The out-of-door treat- ment of disease, of whieh it is one feature, ..wr.s practised by the father of medicine, il,lii:pocrntos, and his followers. The Health .Temple of Co,, built over 2,000 years ago to the God of Medi- cine, ./Esculapius, • was admirably designed for the outdoor treatment of disease. Along throe sides of a _square court surrounding the temple Were long porticos where patients :slept; and out- side in the sun were exedra, or raised seats, where they- "took the cure:" by day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>INSECT EGG-EATERS,</head>
        <p>INSECT EGG-EATERS,</p>
        <p>According to Dr. P. Marchal, some. insects ac as fond of eggs for food as human beings are. He has observed that a hytnenopterous insect, Tetrastichus xanthomelanancß, makes a practice of sucking the eggs of the elm-beetle. It first perforates them with a small hole, and then swallows the contents. Afterward the tetrati- e;wis utilises tile emptied egg-shell by depositing its own eggs within. It might be supposed that the primary object of the operation was that im- plied by the last statement, but Doctor Alarchal is confident that the eating of the egg is a no less important motive.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>ORIGIN OF PEARLS</head>
        <p>THE ORIGIN OF PEARLS</p>
        <p>Three principal hypotheses have been offered to account for the origin of pearls. According to one, they arise from secretions caused by the presence of some foreign object, such as a grain of sand, within the shell of the oyster. But it has been shewn that fine pearls are not thus pro- duced, but only pearly concretions. Another theory is that pearls are the result of disease in the• oyster. The third hypothesis, sustained by Monsieur Seurat or the Oceanographic Museum of Monaco, is that the origin of pearls is to -be ascribed to the presence of a parasite. The species of para*e differs with the species of oyster. but.this mode of origin, Monsieur Seurat</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>POISONED WOUNDS. Itching Skin Disease Caught at Work— Cured by Zam-Buk.</head>
        <p>POISONED WOUNDS. Itching Skin Disease Caught at Work— Cured by Zam-Buk.</p>
        <p>Miss Christina Campbell, of 5, Tennant-street, Renfrew, a bright Scottish lassie of sixteen summers, said to a" Paisley Observer " reporter "About the back end of last year I went to learn the polishing trade, and I had not been at it long when my hands broke out into a rash of pimples. I suppose the chemicals had poisoned my skin. The affection Spread, and my hands and arms had, for a time, the appearance of rough sandpaper. They were intolerably itchy, and I applied all kinds of oinments and lotions but these had only the effect of increasing the irritation, without remedying the trouble in the least. After a time the pimples began to dis- charge; and the disorder spread to my face, my lips and nose becoming affected in the same manner. The suppurating pimples developed into sores; I fell sick and could eat nothing, and at last had to keep to my Led. The sores attacked my eyelids, binding them so fast that I could not open my eyes. For four days I was totally blind, and, to add to my suffering, a large boil broke out on my arm. It was then that my mother rind about Zam-Buk, and we anointed all 'the sore parts with the balm. The soothing effect was wonderful, and after one or two applications of Zam-Buk my eyelids unbound, and I could see again. Then the boil on my arm broke ; and from then onward, my improvement was steady and sure. In a few days all the sores, even the big one on my arm, Healed up, and the unhealthy skin scaled off in a sort of scurf, leaving my skin as smooth and fresh as ever. It is now ten months since I was cured by Zarn-Butr, so I may safely say that my recovery is permanent." Keep Zam-Buk handy for injuries and skin diseases. Zam-Buk ensures healing by first intention, and should be every home's first-aid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>WARRINGTON HEADMASTERSHIPS,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON HEADMASTERSHIPS,</p>
        <p>THE AGITATION FOR LOCAL MEN Education Committee Still in Doubt.</p>
        <p>The most important discussion at Monday's meeting of the Education Committee was on Mr. lipson's pro- posal that in the appointment of future headmasters the selection shall be made from among the teachers serving in the schools in the borough. There was a large attendance at the meeting, Aldermen Bennett presiding. Mr. I3PsoN moved "That it be an instruction to the andgers of all Council schools that, in the appoint- ment of head teachers, the selection shall be made from among teachers serving in wilaools within the County Borough of Warrington." 1-1! thought there would be no two opinions as to the advisability of adopting the principle underlying the motion. The question more particularly affected the men as the difference between the maximun salary of an assistant mistress and a headmistress was so slight that the women shrank in very many cases from taking the headship of a school. They had in their midst a num- ber of men approaching middle life who had been assistants in the town for 15 years or more, who were heads of families and who had been ratepayers in the borough for many years and who ought legititnitately to look to the committee for any promotion which was in its power to bestow. It seemed to him that the motion would commend itself to the committee particu- larly as the old school Boards, almost universally, had adopted some such regulation. It had been found that educationally the regulation had worked admirably. If they had a promotion list it was the desire of every assistant master and mistress to get upon that list and there was the continual stinulous to every teacher to do his or her best to get upon the list, and there was a very careful concern shown by the teachers lest they should, by any neglect of duty render their position on the list insecure. The result of that con- stant stimulous was very telling in the work done in the schools. He of course did notmean to say that if the . committee did not aaopt the motion they would not get good service but a man would sometimes do sufficient to escape censure whilst under the stimulous of a legitimate ambition he would do very much more. He knew that teachers were the only people in the world who were supposed to work from noble and self-sacrificing motives but whilst that was so there were many teachers who were encour- aged to do a great deal more under the expectations of being promoted to the foremost rank of their profession. In business firms it had been found best to make the beads of departments accessable to every boy in the office and this had the effect of not only keeping the lad in the firm but of giving him an interest and stimulous to do his work. From what he could gather his motion was extremely popular pith the public of Warrington. He would not like to repeat some of the things he heard said of the aztion of some members of the Committee a month ago. Many parents looked upon the teaching profession as one to which they could send their boys and girls in the hope and expectation that thay would not be compelled, after having passed through their training, to leave Warring- ton, but that by serving the Committee they would secure any promotion which was available. The local was Press unanimous in support of the principle contained in his motion and he thought that, without his saying anything further, the motion would commend itself, at any rate, to the majority of the members of the Committee. Mr. PLINSTON seconded. tl</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. JoLLEy announced his intention of voting against the motion. He felt as strongly as anyone that the assistant teachers of the town had a legitimate cause of complaint with reference to the " Beamont " School, but he doubted if a resolution of the kind suggested was the right 'way to deal with the grievance. The local authority ought not to be asked to tie its hands by such a hard and fast rule. A resolution of the kind proposed would not be in the bests interests of the anthority or even of the teachers themselves, and it would probably alienate a considerable amount of sympathy in the town if it was thought the authority was to be dtctated to by any organization. Mr. R. T. FAIRCLOUGII thought they should deal with the matter as they dealt with contracts and say "all things being equal we give the Warrington people the preference." Councillor ELLISON said he failed to see why the resolution should be confined to headmastership only. Mr. KINGDON did :not think there was any strict analogy in what Mr. Upson had said between large business firms and this particular question. In a large business house a man was promoted because of his special knowledge, but in the case of a teacher a man from outside was as well qualified for a berth as a War- rington man. Councillor FULTON-SMITH said it would he a bad policy to tie one's hands by carrying a motion like that submitted by Mr. Upson. He was eertain that in some schools there would be a difficulty in getting tenchers t,c) take the heads. The resolution was detrimental to the teachers themselves as if the resolution were adopted he thought Warrington teachers_ need never_ apply for situations in outside</p>
        <p>_ town—(Mr. Upson " That is so now )—as he thought the same measure would be dealt out to them as was dealt out to outsiders. The Rev. J. YONGE wished the resolution had been made a little less stringent. He moved as an amend- ment "That it be an instruction to the managers of all Council schools that, in the appointment of head teachers, all things being equal, the preference shall be given to teachers serving in the schools within the county borough of Warrington." Mr. UPSON: I cannot accept a pious opinion and that is what the amendment amounts to. The Rev. N. WILSON seconded the amendment. The motion as it stood was far too sweeping and drastic. The arguments in support of the motion did not seem to pay enough attention to education itself and that ought to be the chief motive. He thought, moreover, that the motion would have a tendency to restrict free trade in teachers. Mr. UNSWORTII said he did not like the motion, but he liked the amendment still less. The words, " other things being equal "would cause a tremendous amount of discussion, and he did not know where it would end. Who was going to judge? He was strongly in favour of the appointment of Warrington's teachers where it was possible, but he did not like a pistol held to his head by the teachers saying" We are to be appointed." In Liverpool and Manchester and all such big cities such a rule might be beneficial but he did not see that it would in Warrington and, he thought they ought not to debar themselves from securing a first-class teacher from outside. They could find teachers in Watrington who could fill the position and Without such a motion being passed he thought the Committeee would require some very grave reasons for the passing over of War- rington teachers in future appointments. Mr. JOLLEY urged that the amendment was not a pious opinion but was a definite instruction. The Rev. Canon STEVENSON supported the amend- ment. He did not think Mr. Unsworth's fears were justified. They wished to see Warrington men appointed, but at the same same they were obliged to see that the appointments were held by the best men they could get. All things being equal theyshould give an appointment to a local candidate. Mr. pson was evidently a strong Protectionist—(laughter)—but his proposition would be a far too stringent regulation. As Mr. Unsworth had pointed out, the system of pro- moting local men might work well in large places like London, Birmingham, Manchester, or Liverpool, but in a place of the size of Warrington the choice was limited. Mr. Ursos did not think it fair for Mr. Unsworth to talk about teachers holding pistols. He wrote his pro- position at a meeting of the School Management Sub- committee, and it had not been seen by anyone until it was in print. He was there as a person interested in education, and not in any special sense as the pleader for the teachers. He believed his proposal to be just, fair, and right. In Ipswich, a town a little larg.r than Warrington, two new schools bad been opened, and teachers employed in the borough were appointed to them. A large number of county boroughs and the county associations themselves were beginning to advertise and say that preference would be given to local teachers. Warrington teachers were shut out from other towns whilst in Warrington outsiders were freely admitted. Sometime ago they recommended the managers of the voluntary schools to cornfine the appointments to teichers working in the borough and the first appointment which the committee had to con- fer went to a man from outside. If they were going to say " all things being equal" the person who wanted an outsider would soon find some in equality which would dam the local teacher. They must have a definite resolution if they were to have any control over the managers. Mr. ROBRRTS said be agreed with Mr. Upson in regard to the amendment. It was not worth the paper it was written upon. He thought, however, that it would give an opportunity for the wobblers on a com- mittee who wanted to give the preference to a local man to the disadvantage of the cade of education and that was why he preferred a definite resolution like Mr. Upson's to a wobbling, weak-minded one like the amendment. He suggested that the time was not quite ripe for the discussion of the whole question and urged that Mr. Upson should withdraw, and re-introduce that question when they had further information before them as to the practice in other towns. He thought some rule could be drafted whereby the managers could be controlled in the appointments in the direction sug- gested by the resolution. He was quite agreed that preference should be given to local teachers if that could be brought within the region of practical politics. Councillor PEMBERTON said he could not accept Mr. Upson's proposition because it was too definite. (Laughter.) It absolutely barred outsiders. At the same time he agreed Warrington men should have pre- ference if they were fit and qualified. Mr. UPSON suggested that he should move that in the event of any vacancies in the hea4masterships of Council schools the teachers in Warrington be circu- larised and invited to apply and that, failing a suitable</p>
        <p>reply ths vacancy should be advertised. That was almost a copy of the Liverpool regulations. Councillor BALL urged the withdrawal of the m,ption and its re-introduction at a future date and this being the evident opinion of the meeting Mr. Upson consented to adopt this course, the nover of the amendment also - withdrawing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>'‘."\:Akii4...1.44 .</head>
        <p>'‘."\:Akii4...1.44 .</p>
        <p>-N.,</p>
        <p>SPIDERS AND MUSIC.</p>
        <p>It has been asserted that spiders possess a sen- sitiveness •to musical sound. Some species seem to respond to the notes of the piano, the harp, the lute, and so on, in a manner suggestive of their ability to recognise these sounds, or the harmonic vibrations on which they are based. But Mons. A. Lecaillon, of the College of France, who has made a special study of the in- stincts and the supposed " psychism' of spiders, thinks that the apparent SensitivenesAs of these animals to music has been misunderstood. It is his opinion that when musical instruments are played near their nests the spiders simply feel the vibrations through their webs, or otherwise, without recognising the musical notes as sounds. The effect upon them is similar to that of the buzzing of an entrapped fly. He does not ascribe much "intelligence" to spiders.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>ORIGIN OF LAKE LIFE.</head>
        <p>ORIGIN OF LAKE LIFE.</p>
        <p>Interesting studies have been made by Miss Rina Monti on the earliest forms of life appear- inn- in Alpine lakes. One of these, the Lake of the Seracs, has been created` within human memory, and it seems to justify the statement that the first living forms inhabiting lakcs are of a vegetable nature. Only five living species are found in the Lake of the Seracs, all of them plants. and four of the order of ,diatoms. In the older lakes of Ong and of Tignaga animal forms begin to appear, feeding- upon the diatoms. The ( cording t Miss Monti's conclusions, are, first, first two eps in the populating of a lake, ac- the appearance of diatoms absorbing carbonic acid dissolved in the water; . and second, the ap- pearance of simple animal organisms, such as rhizopods, whose nourishment depends upon dna pre-existing vegetable forms. Geologists hare supposed that the first living forms in the oceans were vegetable. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>MANUFACTURING NITROGEN</head>
        <p>MANUFACTURING NITROGEN</p>
        <p>A method of fixing atmospheric nitrogGn by means of electricity, and storinc, it in the form of calcium nitrate, which bids fair to be a com- mercial success, has now been carried on for some time at Notodden, in Norway. In this pro- cess air is drawn through an electric furnaces and nitric oxide is formed. The heated gases then pass into oxidising chambers, where " the nitric) oxide is changed into nitrogen peroxide. From thence they pass into towers filled with broken quartz, over which wafer is allowed to trickle. This acting on the nitrogen peroxide forms nitric acid. Finally this passes on to another tower filled with broken bricks, and meets with a stream of milk of lime. Calcium nitrate is thus produced, and finally forms the commercial article. Experiments seem to indicate that this calcium nitrate is as good manure as Chili salt- petre. This process is known as the 13irkelancl- Eyde, from the name of its inventors.</p>
        <p>FACES AND FEATURES OF CRIMINALS. Can the Anarchist, or the man with Anarchist tendencies, be detectCd by a 'study of his face and features? Some scientists think they can, and the researches Of Dr. Antonio Lecha-Marzo, a Spanish authority, are referred to by the British Medical Journal. Taking the man Morral, who committed the outrage at the wedding of the King of Spain and - Prinnss Ena, he thus sum- marises his characteristics: The brows overhung the face markedly, giving a somewhat sinister, look, a feature which Loin. broso points out as common in criminals, occur- ring more than twice as frequently as in the nor mal person (25.7 per cent. as against 11 per cent.). Sefior Juan Marro places the figure-some- what lower, but Mirabella, in a recently-pub- lished record of the physical peculiarities of eighty-four criminals, assigns it a proportion of as much as 48 per cent. It is, however, much less ,marked in wni-nnn The chin was nreminent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <p>- • •</p>
        <p>INFLUENZA IS RAGING. Take Bile Beans arid Make Yourself Proof Against its Attack</p>
        <p>"Influenza is very prevalent in London, and many' society functions have had to be abandoned:" "There is a fashion in influenza as well as in dress, and just now the fashionable forin affects the liver." These extracts from the daily papers emphasise the great necessity for keeping your body, and especially your liver and stomach, in a thoroughly healthy con- dition, if yon wish to be proof against influenza's insidious attacks. Bile Beans increase your vitality and fortify you against disease by stimulating your liver .and strengthening your digestive organs. After an attack of intlaanza, also, during the trying arid danger- ous stage of convalescence, Bile .Beans are of great value in restoring health and strength to weakened bodies.</p>
        <p>Miss Emily Walker, of 6, Tenham-avenne, Streatham. bill, London' S.W., says :—" I was attacked by gastric influenza, as the doctor called it. I had severe pains in the side after the fever left me, and I thought it was ulceration of the stomach, which 1 had had some years. previously. I could not lie down in bed, nor could I sneeze, without enduring great pain. As for sleep or- rest, that was impossible. "One day I noticed the report of a cure by Bile Beans of one who hal been suffaring like myself. I made up my mind at once to give Bile Beans a trial, and am glad I did so, for I found great relief. I kept on with Bile Beans until I was quite better. Since I have taken Bile Beans my general health has remined very good. As a precaution I always keep ,a box of Bile Beans in the house."</p>
        <p>Keep your liver and stomach healthy by taking Bile Beans, a safe, sound, and sure medicine ; safe, because they contain no drastic drugs and no minerals; sound, because they are prepared with th. 3 utmost care and are al gays c3nstAnt in value; and sure, because their action is purely natural, gentle, and genial, and the good results lasting. Of all chemists, at Is. 11d. a box, or 2s. 9cl. for box containing three times the quantity.. Refuse all substitutes, and accept only the genuine.</p>
