      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>UNEMPLOYED QUESTION. Mr. J. Morton Proposes a Solution.</head>
        <p>THE UNEMPLOYED QUESTION. Mr. J. Morton Proposes a Solution.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Progressive Labour League was held at the Liberal Club on Tuesday even-P ing under the presidency of Mr. T. Barnes. There was a good attendance. Mr. John Morton gave an interest- ing paper on "The unemployed question" which of all social problems was, in his opinion, the one of first importance, containing in its solution the key either directly or indirectly of most other social problems- whether moral, physical. or intellectual. He pointed out that a conference of London administrative) authorities on lack of employment, in whose proceed- ings the Right Hon. John Burns took part, came to the conclusion that " want of employment is due to two main.causes, fluctuations of trade and fluctuations of individual character. The former lie for the most part beyond the control of society as at present constituted." Mr. Morton contended that such schemes as reclaiming the Wash were only a form of charity and that labour colonies would have the eff4ct of displacing work else- where. His proposal was that the unemployed should be put to is ork producing commodities which they themselves required and not work for which there was no immediate demand such as was now usually resorted to He would give them the opportanity of taking their places in general industry producing their own food and clothing and other necessities under State direction with the means of production provided by the State. It was no way out of the dilemma to throw the produce of the workers on the open market, but the State wourti have to supply for its workers not only the meaos of production, but the machinery of exchange by which each worker received his share of the produce. He suggested that paper money should be used to pay the workers, and thus the demands for labour would coma automatically and regularly into the hands of-the State. At present the issue of tickets for presentation to tradesmen was a common method of alleviating distress. Under his scheme the tickets could all be made presentable at one shop or store, an d if this was run by the State or municipality the public authority would have concentrated in its hands a means for providing work, altogether different from any that was suggested now. If these tickets were only given in exchange for work done and all the towns wore linked up into one national system it would be plain that the State would have under its control a vast amount of industry which it would be its duty to organise in the best interests of the country. The wages paid to commence with would be the same for each worker and it would he the aim of the authorities to determine the relative values of the different classes of labour. The State industry would be open to all who wished to take advantage of it at tbe wage being paid and this wage would form a national minimum below which none need sell their, labour, but if more remunerative work could be found in private industry by any State employee he would be at liberty to go. The minimum wage he suggested was on economic one based on the actual productive power of those employed and would increase with the greater eliciency of the workers and of the organization. An interesting discussion followed, Mr. Ockel con- tending that if they freed industries of the taxes levied upon them at present and put a "single tax" on the rental value of the land, it would prevent land being kept back for speculative purposes, and unemployment would be remedied.</p>
        <p>The debate was also taken part in by the chairman, who said Mr. Morton's scheme was Socialism for the unemployed, Mr. J. Elwell, Mr. T. Osborne and others. Mr. MoitToNtsaid they did not want to raise bitter- ness against one section of soc!ety at the expense ef the other and expressed his disagreement with the principle of the" single tax."</p>
        <p>Death of Viscountess Cross. We very much regret to record the death, from heart failure, after influenza, of Lady Cross, which took Place at her residence Redo Riggs, Broughton- in-Furness, Lancashire, on Sunday. Lady Cross, who was in her 80th year, was Georgian], third daughter of the late Mr. Thomas Lyon, D.L., of Appleton Hall Cheshire, by his wife Eliza, daughter of Mr. George Clayton, of Lostock Hall, Lady Cross who married Lord Cross in 1852 was a lady of the Imperial Order of the Crown of India. She was for many years closely identified with Vitarrington, owing to her connection with Appleton Hall. Her father was the Chairman of the bench of magistrates for Warrington, Newton and Daresbury, and was, we are informed, approached by Hr. Beatnont, with a view to becoming the first Mayor of Warrington. It was, however, an honour he declined, and Mr. Beamont took the office. Lord and Lady Cross lived for several years at Hill Cliffe, his Lordship taking an active interest in the manage- ment of Parr's Bank. Lady Cross, was a sister of Mr. T. 11. Lyon, of Appleton Hall, and is still remembered by a few of the older generations. Her son, the Hon. C. F. Cross, is vicar of St. Matthew's Church, Stretton, while another son, the [lon. John E. Cross, is the estate agent for Colonel Legh, of High Letzh. The funeral took place on Wednesday afternoon at Broughton-in-Furness. A specially appreciated hymn by the late Viscountess "The King of Love my Shepherd i,," was rendered at the graveside. Canon Monnington performed the last rites, and the magnificent floral tributes decorating the grave were ample testimony Of the popularity of the deceased lady. Viscount Cross received numerous messages of s)mpathy, including telegrams from the Prince and Princess .of Wales, the Right Hon. A. J. Balfour, Viscountess L'an Life Lord and Lady C..tdog In, and Lord and Lady Derby.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>CRIPPLED FROM PARALYSIS AND NERVE WEAKNESS.</head>
        <p>CRIPPLED FROM PARALYSIS AND NERVE WEAKNESS.</p>
        <p>Cured by Dr. Cassell's Tablets. ANOTHER EXTRAORDINARY CASE.</p>
        <p>Mrs. WEL UNSON, 4t Flax-road, B3lgrave, Leicestsr, writes :—"My little girl, aged 3, suffered from Infantile Paralysis and nerve and muscular weakness. She could not walk alone, or even stand, or turn over in bed. I was recommended by Mr. Abbott, Police Court Missionary in Leicester, -whom you cured, to try DR. CASSELL'S TABLETS, and after three months' triat- ment my little girl can now walk by herself ; she i&amp; much stronger and 31bs. heavier. Everybody marvels, at the result, arid lam delighted to give you these facts." DR. CASSELL'S TABLETS will cure you as they- cured Mrs. Wilkinson's little girl. They create healthy flesh, strength and vitality, as no other medicine ever. did ; sod they cure the most pronounced cases of LEANNESS OF BODY WASTING DISEASES GENERAL WEAKNESS AN.EIIIA WRECKED NERVES INDIGESTION PARALYSIS OF NERVES MAL-NU TRITION LOSS OF FLESH KIDNEY WEAKNESS' and all diseases arising from physical and nerve exhaus- tion. We could no more explain the marvellous action of this extraordinary tablet than you can explain th&amp;. chemical phenomena which produces electricity. It is the tablet everybody requires. 10A., and 2s. 91. at all. Chemists and Drug Stores everywhere,or post free from the famous Dr. Cassell's Co., Ltd.. King Street West,. Manchester. Advice free by letter.</p>
