      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>DIZEf:S-FASHION-HONI2.</head>
        <p>DIZEf:S-FASHION-HONI2.</p>
        <p>WOMEN AS DOCTORS.</p>
        <p>Women have had now at least a quarter of a -century in which to shew their value to the com- munity in the- Art of Healing, and in my humble judgment, says a "Man Doctor" in the Reader, they have not been able to compete successfully with men doctors. lam quite aware that one -or two women have attained a certain eminence as consultants, but these are .the exceptions that prove the rule. Let women who believe they have a special talent for dealing with disease strive to become efficient and trustworthy nurses. I say that the intelligent fume, by doing the right thing at the right moment, has over and over again .saved patients, the credit going, of course, to the doctor. It is in those little details of nursing. by which she enables the patient to escape death by a hair's 'breadth, that woman stands pre-eminent. In all earnestness, then, I would say, let woman pursue that line of the healing art to which she is by Nature adapted, ;and in which she must always excel, leaving to man the burden of ultimate responsibility which he alone is fitted to bear. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>CLOTH DRESS FOR THE HOUSE.</head>
        <p>CLOTH DRESS FOR THE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>The dress shewn in _our sketch is one that would make a useful house-dress for present wear, and yet could be worn later on as a walk- ing dress, if made of some light but warm woollen material. It would look well carried out in rather a deep shade of amethyst-coloured -cloth, and trimmed with velvet a shade darker. The bodice has a slightly rounded opening at he neck, which is filled in with a little vest of -tucked white silk and lace insertion, finished at</p>
        <p>'the base with a short circular strap, the fasten. ing being effected beneath the loft hand rever sand down the, left side, beneath the double box- pleat. The revers are faced with velvet and edged with a narrow kilted frill of silk to match. The sleeves are full at the top, but fit closely to .the lower part of the arm, and are trimmed at the elbow and wrist with small pointed bands of velvet, edged with kilted silk frilling to corre- spond with the revers. The skirt has a narrow panel-shaped pleat down the front, and is trimmed at the hem with two shaped bands. THE WOMEN OF JAMAICA.</p>
        <p>The only point which seems to present itself as an obvious truism to the minds of those Anglo- West Indians with whom I have discussed the Jamaica disaster, writes a London correspond- ent, is that so long as the native feminine popu- lation of Kingston is not destroyed the mortality among the males is of little consequence. The matter seems to be simply one of commercial value. Though I have net heard that the suf- frage question has been raised in the West Indies, the ladies of those islands have obtained even more than their rights, particularly in the •directiod of the right to labour. Kingston, in particular, is run by women. Judging from talk in the City, the only other inhabitants who count are buggymen. The bug-gyman is the most ob- vious inhabitant of Kingston—the pet which ,most nearly corresponds to the guide in Italy. The prayer of those who know and have suffered -is that the women of the town may be preserved, and the buggymen less mercifully dealt with. I am assured that compared with the black buggy- man the London cabby is an angel of mercy, and 'his vocabulary that of a clergyman.,</p>
