      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>NORTHERN CROSS-COUNTRY ASSOCIATION</head>
        <p>NORTHERN CROSS-COUNTRY ASSOCIATION</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Association was held on Wednesday evening in Manchester. It was unanimously decided that Haydock Park be again the venue for the Northern Junior and Senior Championships, to be held on Satur- day, February 16, with a recommendation that either Stockton or Doncaster be considered for next year. Six new clubs were elected to membership—viz., Swinton (Yorkshire), Warrington Rylands, Preston, Dukinileld, Armley and Worton, and Whaley Bridgp. The latter club will be admitted subject to the club coming into the Northern radius.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>RUNCORN WOMAN'S REMARKABLE DELUSION.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN WOMAN'S REMARKABLE DELUSION.</p>
        <p>A strange statement was made at Runcorn on Monday night, at an inquest on Elizabeth Done. aged 62, the wife of a Ship Canal waterrnan.—Her husband said that a few years ago she ate some watercress for tea and must have eaten something alive with it, for ever since she bad said there was a bira in her inside continually gnawing.—The Coroner: What did she take for it ? Witness: Sometimes a glass of ale and sometimes a drop of whisky. It was her wish when she died that she r.hould be opened to see if anything could be found. —Dr. Brunton. who made the post-mortem examination, said the internal organs were loaded with fat. She suffered from chronic valvular disease of the heart. The flutterings of the heart would be responsible for deceased's making such an extraordinary statement.—A verdict of " Death from heart disease '5. was returned.</p>
        <p>NFLUENZA'S original foe. Is still repelling INFLUENZA</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 26, 1907.</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.]</p>
        <p>It was Vernon Salisbury's turn to grow pals. He looked round with apprehension. Had any- one heard? Jack had screamed out the words, utterly reckless of the consequences they might bring upon himself. However, seemingly, no one had heard. He drew a breath of relief.</p>
        <p>"You should be careful, my friend," he said. "You should remember my liberty and your life are bound up together; for I swear upon my— well, I swear on the day I am taken the world shall know what you are, and I swear also if you do net give me your sister the world shall know what you are. I know she has another suitor—what of that? Your wife had another suitor, and you dealt with him. I've no intention of dealing with Paul Clifford in the same way, I value my life too highly; besides, there is no necessity, your sister will do as you tell her, particularly if you point out the conse- quences likely to come if she does not. And you will tell her to marry me ? " , Jack drew himself up; out of his pale face his dark eyes blazed. Give his sister, his pretty sister, to this man—never! He would take the consequences of his crime first! - "You are mistaken," he answered, "I will do no such thing.' Do your worst, and I will do my worst! Money I am willing to give you c flesh and blood, no. If that is all you have come for, you have come on a fool's errand." Vernon Salisbury looked what he felt, surprised. It had seemed to him that Jack would have no other course open to him but to give in to his dictates; the inan's defiance frightened him. He was fond of impressing upon Jack that he had the whip-band of -him, but, after all, Jack, too, had him in his power; he was quite as much afraid of being sent back to Dartmoor as -Jack was of the hangman's rope. It took. him a minute or two to recover himself. "You mean you refuse ? "</p>
        <p>anif absolutely! Give my sister to you? I'd sooner see her dead! If you want money, you shall have it; though, mind you, I'm not going to be bled for ever. And I prefer that you should not go back to my home; I was a fool ever to let you into it. After all, Pve no more need to be afraid of you than you have of Inc."</p>
        <p>Vernon Salisbury's face fell perceptibly. That was, of course, true. He wondered Jack had not realised it before. He was, however, not going to admit it. "Alt, but I think you have," he answered. "The joys of my life are at present summed up in the one word liberty; the joys of your life are manifold. Misfortune coming to me would strike one individual only—myself; misfortune coming to you would deal almost a deathblow to tw" women—your wife and your sister; would brand them with disgrace; might even go further than that—brand some child-to-be as the offspring of a murderer—a modern Cain! At worst, I can go back whence I came, perhaps to escape again. As for you, there is no doubt whatever as to what your fate would be. I saw the murder committed, I would give evidence." -"And prove yourself only guilty in a lesser degree; an accessory after the fact does not get off scot-free."</p>
        <p>"No, but there is such_a thing as King's evidence.. Well, I don't wait to rush you; think _the matter over, and take this into consideration you have committed a worse crime than I have, and you are making the woman you love happy;. there is nothing terrible about you that even, hints at your sin, and frightens her. Well, it would be the same with me. By birth lam what is called a gentleman; all my instincts are decent. I'm not a good onve at talking of such things, but I love her utterly. I would make her a good husband, on my honour; she should be a happy wife. Think it over, it will be better for us all that you should fall in with my views. And now, for money matters. The police are still active, it is best I should not be unprovided mith eash. Will you let m) have a hundred pounds? I should prefer notes and gold to a cheque. You will admit lam modest in lily requirements—most men in ray position would have demanded a thousand."</p>
        <p>"And would not have • got it," Jack Brandon said; "as for the hundred, you must take a cheque for it, or not have it at all. I don't keer a hundred pounds in ready money about me. And quite understand, I will not have my sister annoyed in any way. Heaven knows, I have fallen low enough, but not quite so low as that ! " Vernon Salisbury laughed.. "Once we begin falling," he said, we never know how low we may drop. We'll leave the subject now. Later on I shall return to it. If you are foolish, 1 must lay the case before your sister; she, at all events, will not wish her brother to be hanged!"</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXVII. "WHY DOES HE WEAR A WIG ? "</p>
        <p>The letters telling Jack Brandon of the engage- ment of Paul Clifford and Olive had been posted. Olive felt more comfortable. He would write *back, say how pleased he was, and she would be in no way further troubled with the madness of Vernon Salisbury. Vernon Salisbury had not returned ; he had been gone for two days. Olive and Mrs. Cole did not know, of course, but on arriving at a big town, the attraction of it had been too much for him ; he had plenty of money in his pocket, he would take his chance of capture—of which, as a matter of fact, he was not much afraid—and have a bit of a fling. When he returned to Jack Brandon's house, he would have a serious talk with Olive; if he did not succeed in frightening the girl into marrying him, he would be very much surprised. Paul Clifford and Olive Brandon had been sitting in the garden, talking the usual charming nonsense lovers indulge in, when Mrs. Cole joined them. The handsome widow looked less radiant than usual; her face was pale, even sallow, her eyes dull. Anyone looking at her would have said at once that she was not happy. And they would have been quite right. She had fallen in love with a man about half her own age—always a very serious matter—she 1.1 171:ka -hal;‘,C Shot' aks</p>
        <p>.' , ....,..7,., •st: .- • '):,w..-V.,1,1'..7...' • '..-``,. :. ' ' 2 - - ,--. •.::, :.:,.--„. -.-- '_ , .'''-'-:':',,h-'-r'-:-'"` • .''''...';:'-'::' "::::_--,!'-''''::c',474,-,;--"4.0?"--F.--_`',,,X- 14,4-7. ... ' . ?.—. - ' I::: '•.: -- ",;',:-. ~' ". -•-,..". ,- -- :‘ . . '.„ 1:-;.?' *!.:01"....„m.&gt;.,:5-g; : , ,i,----...k - . ... ~,..,..:„„ ~_-_-;:-.,-..,;.,. -- , , --- ~,,54..-.4.w,A-4. 01/4 i....t,.... .. ....•• - , ~-"f : --,--..----4-- -,43- •45, ,errti. Tsr:- . '., ' "'..- . • /,'" : ::: .--,,:- •:,—. ''' - ^ -.....,A to ~„.• , - , - , . ~. ":i,.....5t- • ' k-.' ..,,' • •.. t.• 4...-- ...• • - , ~ , _- , . - ...,;,$,. : lift. . :., ' !?;';,;,., ~. ,' . - ' ... ' ''''' % • ' ."': s: '?..: ~.' 1 . . . . : . ~ - " s ~,V.i,&amp;:' N 4,.. , _.- - - '," F 1 .:. . • f ;47' q ~,,,,, ' - ' '''. ' ''' '. . • :::. ''.:7.:ii ,` : ..4, 1,-',:.4.;,,,,• ' -- , ' ''•• if -,;:".-.•-• '). ' • ;',.?:31 '',.... '':',.., . , . ~ . ____ • r _</p>
