      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>WRITTEN IN SCARLET.</head>
        <p>WRITTEN IN SCARLET.</p>
        <p>Dv ALICE MAUD MEADOWS, author of "One Life Between," "A Million of Money," "The Eztreute Penalty," "For Love 44 a Lady," 'II Charge You Both," Sco.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXVI. "I WANP YOUR SISTER FOR MY WIFE.'</p>
        <p>John Brandon had written Vernon Salisbury with regard to the letter his wife had received; had told him to pretend to make inquiries, and to write back that the letter was most certainly a emel hoax; that nothing had been seen or heard o: Dick Brandon in the villa,re • nothing had bappencd to point in any way, to his being alive. Celia had been relieved when the letter came back. She liked her husband now, even if she did not love him. She was quite happy, and cortainly did not wish the even tenor of her way disturbed. She was quite sweet_ to her husband after she had finished the letter.</p>
        <p>"You think me a little goeso, I suppose ?" sne said. "But I did want my heart set at rest.. Of course, I should have been glad if poor Dick bad been alive but I didn't like the idea of his having written to me not knowing what was going to happen; and it seemed strange if he had written that he did not turn up." "And now," he said, fondly, heart is at rest ? "</p>
        <p>"your little</p>
        <p>She -nodded her pretty head. answered.</p>
        <p>"Yes," she</p>
        <p>"And you—are quite happy ? " "I'm happy any way," she ieturned. "I don't know- about quite happy—perhaps it's difficult to be quite happy. I wonder whether anyone ever is? Are you, for instance ? " He thought of the convict masquerading as his friend in his home, mixing daily with his sister.- He remembered the hold this man had over him, and he could not truthfully say he was quite happy. “So far as my marriage is concerned," he said, "I am utterly, utterly happy. I don't think we poor mortals must expect perfect happiness in more than one direction. When I put my arms round you, and kiss you sweetheart, I forget any worries I may have; but, of course, lam not free from them."</p>
        <p>She peeped round at him, looking at him gravely. "We are supposed to share everything now," she said. "You must tell me what your worries are, and perhaps I can make them lighter. You may be sure I shall if I can. Once or twice I've seen a line across your forehead here "—she traced it with her finger—" and wondered why it was there. Now, you must tell me."</p>
        <p>He shook his head. "Worries are not for little fairies to bear," he said. "Besides, dearest, they aro really nothing; perhaps "—he smiled grimly—"l may overrate them to call ' them worries; responsibilities would perhaps be a better word. You see, I have a big estate to manage and now I must manage it more care- fully than ever because I have a sweet wife, and there may be other blessings that will come to me in time; blessings "—he smiled tenderly— "that will run me up bills for schooling, and shoe leather, and a hundred other things. She made a grimace but she looked at him rather shyly. "I don't think those responsibilities need trouble you at present," she answered. "There really is nothing bothering you—nothing definite, I mean ? " • "Nothing," he answered. "No, i really consider myself the most fortunate man in the whole wide world. I had a long time to wait for you, sweetheart, but since I have you now I am utterly happy." Someone turning the handle of the door made them move apart. The next moment a man- servant entered the room.</p>
        <p>"Mr. Vernon Salisbury to see you, sir," he said. "He preferred to wait downstairs." John Brandon turned pale. Vernon Salisbury here! What had he come for? Money? He could have written for that. To say, perhaps, that he was in danger, in fear of being recaptured. If so, why had he not tried to get out of the country? He turned to his wife.</p>
        <p>"Do you mind-my leaving you for a minute or two ? " he said. "It's a most extraordinary thing that my old friend should have followed me here, but since he has,l suppose I must see him." "Of course you must," she answered. "And why not ask him to dinner? I rather liked the look of him when I saw him at our wedding. I daresay, too, you'd be glad of a chat with a man, though, of course, I do not expect you to acknowledge it." She gave his arm a little pinch, and looked up saucily. He smiled back moodily, all the joy gone out of his face. "No, I won't ask him," he answered. "I want no one but you. It's an unheard of thing that he should intrude upon us now." She shrugged her shoulders. Though she had begun to care for her husband, she would not have been in the least displeased with the society of a handsome young man—someone fresh to admire her and her pretty frocks. "Just as you like, of course," she answered, "only, so far as I am concerned, you might ask him to dinner. I should have thought you would have liked to. I'm afraid "—she laughed again—"you are not as proud of your wife as I thought yoil were." For a wonder Jack seemed hardly to hear her wo—fds. He walked across the room, turning as he reached the door.'</p>
        <p>"I shan't be ten minutes," he said, and then she was alone.</p>
        <p>She crossed to the glass and looked at herself. "Certainly I am out of the way pretty," she said, complacently, "but I don't, see why he should be so horribly jealous. Of course, it was jealousy that would not let him bring the man up here. He'll have to get over that. I'm a married woman, not a nun.' Jack Brandon went slowly downstairs. A servant threw open the door of a room into which Vernon Salisbury had been shewn, and shut it again. Vernon Salisbury was lounging in a chair, looking extremely handsome, and having still about him that look of perfect health. He nodded carelessly to Jack, and did not rise. Jack was the first to speak. "What's the meaning of this intrusion ? " he said. "I can call it nothing else."</p>
        <p>„"But you will forgive a-when you know the cause,” Vernon said with a laugh. "You've just been through the mill yourself, so you'll sympathisealth me. I've fallen in love, and, being a nor al man, I naturally want to marry. To do so I shall require your help —your moral persuasion and financial help." , Jack Brandon grew, if possible, paler than ever. "You want to marry ? " he repeated. "The thing's preposterous! Do you remember what you are ? Vernon Salisbury smiled. "Certainly," he answered, "and I remember what you are also. Come, come-, Jack, what's the good of the pot calling the kettle black? \l've done you'a good turn, now you are going to return the obligation, or "—his face darkened—"l'll know the reason why. I don't want to threaten, but please to remember you are utterly in my power. I'm your master. You'll do as I bid you, or you'll be dragged from the arms of your young wife, clapped in prison, and ultimately hanged for a brother's murder. Ah! that frightens you. Well, it doesn't sound pretty, does it ? " "What do you want," Jack said, harshly,. " money/ " "Money eventually, of course, but not primarily. I want, as every man wants, the woman I love. She does not care for me now; like your bride, she fancies another man, but we'll cure her of that. I daresay you've found it possible for a husband to wipe out the remembrance of a lover. I daresay your pretty wife is quite fond of you now ? " Jack's eyes blazed. "Don't dare to speak of my wife!' Ile said. "You're not fit to mention her! "</p>
        <p>"Yet you are fit to marry her? That seems a little strange, doesn't it? Well, well, we won't quarrel; and since you objectr-to my speaking of your wife, suppose we speak of your sister—in fact, it was your sister I came here to speak abeut. Now, don't say anything violent. If you're fit to be her brother, I'm fit to speak of her. I told you I had fallen in love. Well, it is with your sister Olive that I have fallen in tote. I want you to settle an income upon me, and give hr to me for my wife." For an instant rage rendered Jack Brandon dumb, then he strode across .the room, and if Vernon Salisbury had not dodged the blow he wo,Ald have struck him.</p>
        <p>"Bow dare you!" he said. ,"Good Heavens! Yen,a eonvie:, to mention my sister. in such a connection! I wonder I don't kill vi</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <p>greatly as she.aliiiired hix, he.admired her. Sne had fully believed he would ask her to become his wife. Olive's news had come to her like a thunder clap. Olive rose as Mrs. Cole sat down. "I'll fetch my work," she said; "it does not seem right to sit idle for so long. You two can keep one another company, can you not ? " • She nodded to them, and walked off towards the house. Paul would have liked to have gone with her; politeness however, forced him to remain. Mrs. Cole looked at him and smiled.</p>
        <p>"And so you two are really engaged ? " she said. "I'm sure I congratulate you both; I don't know which of you is the more fortunate." He laughed boyishly, brightly; he flushed with pride and pleasure. "Oh, I am," he answered—"far, far more fortunate than Olive. She will only get an ordinary sort of fellow for a husband—l shall have the sweetest, most beautiful, most clever and charming woman in the world for a wife. But I'm going to make her so frightfully happy, she shall never regret throwing herself away upon me. The best fellow in the world could do no more than worship her, and I'm sure I do that." _ _</p>
        <p>Mrs. Cole smiled very sympathetically. "I believe you do, you foolish boy ! " she said. "Do you know that's not at all a wise thing to do ? " He put his hands behind his head, resting on them. The action took his thoughts to Vernon Salisbury. "Oh, but I don't want to be wise—only happy," he returned. "And, after all, isn't that the best kind of wisdom ? "</p>
        <p>She sighed softly. Her next words shewed where her thoughts had travelled. "I wonder when Mr. Salisbury will come back?" she said. "I had no idea he would be away more than one night." Something of the brightness left Paul Clifford's face. "So far as lam concerned, he's welcome to stay away for ever!" he said. "I know you rather like him' Mrs. Cole' and I hope you won't be offended, but I don't, and I don't trust him."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Cole did not look offended, but she difa look interested.</p>
        <p>"I wonder why not ? " she asked. He hesitated a minute. Should he say why he did not trust the friend of Jack Brandon? After all, was it not only jealousy that made him dislike him? He did not want to be unfair to anyone. "Instinct, perhaps," he answered, "or perhaps I'm unreasonably jealous. i don't like the way he looks at Olive. The fellow is undeniably handsome, and, although I'm sure my little girl would never give a thought to him, it's natural, perhaps, that I should not like it." Mrs. Cole nodded in a meditative way. "You have no definite reason, then ? " she said. "I thought, perhaps, you had." Again he hesitated, then he burst out with the truth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <p>"I don't understand why the fellow wears a wig." he said. "and that's the truth." Mrs. Cole looked, as she felt, astounded. "A wig ? " she repeated. Yes, a wig. There is no reason, so far as I can see, that he should, unless, of course, it's a disguise of some sort. I noticed as he leant down the other day that his real hair is quite thick—very short, but quite thick. Why should he cover it with a wig, unless for some reason he does not wish to be known ? " Mrs. Cole did not answer. Some of Paul Clifford's words remained in her mind; "Very short, but quite thick." Like a flash the description of the escaped convict which Warder Clark had given her returned to her memory. Was it possible? If so, why was John Brandon harbouring this man ?" He had come on the night previously to the wedding—the night the convict had escaped. Ho had told Olive Brandon he knew something ix he past life of her brother, something discredit- ible. Had. he escaped from Dartmoor, and, iSecause of the knowledge he had of some secret of John Brandon's, had he forced himself into the house? It looked very much like it. "I'll make suspicion certainty," she said to herself. "I'll go to the police-station, there is sure to be a rough photograph or drawing of the escaped convict there. If my suspicions are correct, I hold him in the hollow of my hand ! " She closed her fingers as the thought came, and they shut with remarkable closeness. She - smiled grimly to herself. She was not horrified as most women would have been.</p>
        <p>"You are sure ? " she asked. "I did not notice."</p>
        <p>"Absolutely sure," he answered. "I have looked at him differently from the way in which everyone else has looked, I've been jealous of his handsome features, his curly hair. Wlten I found out that was a wig—l only suspected it at first, but looked and looked until I was sure-- you may guess I was glad. If the man's a fraud in one thing he may be in another; that's why I distrust him."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Cole's lips were shut rather tightly. Was her suspicion true? Had she fallen in love with a convict? It was horrible! But if this man was the man who had escaped from the prison it was a fact, and having fallen in lova with him this terrible suspicion did not make her fall out. "He may have been falsely condemned. Such things have happened," she said to herself. "He may be innocent. Any way, he has the most amazing nerve of any man I ever knew if this thing is true. He did not turn a hair that day Warder Clark called, though, to be sure, he sent me to interview him."</p>
        <p>"I wonder if you are right ? "she said, quietly. "Any way, I hope you have not mentioned this to anyone; it is better not to." "I have not mentioned it, and shall not," he answered. "I don't know really what made me speak of it to you. but I am perfectly sure of the fact. When he comes back you can see for yourself."</p>
        <p>(To be continued).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Warrington Board ot Guardians.</head>
        <p>Warrington Board ot Guardians.</p>
        <p>An interim meting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday. Mr.J. Billinge presided and there were also present : Mrs. Wilkinson,l4,iss Broadbent, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, W. C. Brooks, G. Mather, R. Bolton, J. Thompson, A. Brooks, M. W.Seton, J.Cook,R. Forghaw, E.L. Bent,J. C. Sutton (clerk), J. F. Awbery (master), and A. Wright (superintendent of Padgate schools).—The clerk was instructed to write to the secretary of the Warrington Infirmary inviting Lord Lilford and the other guests to visit the workhouse on the occasion-of laying the foundation stone for the extension to the Infirmary.—On the motion of Mr. Mather, the Chair- man, Deputy Chairman and Clerk were appointed delegates to attend the Centrai Poor Law Conference at London.—Mr. Bolton gave notice that he would ask , for a return of the number and cost of deputations in 100 G.—The Clerk suggested that Mr. Bolton could obtain the information by,an application to the officers of dae Board without a special motion. He knew Mr. Bolton had no rersonal feeling, and Merely wanted the information. If after be had the information he had any cause for complaint he could then bring the matter before the Board.—Mr. \Bolton said he would con- sider the Course -mggested by the Clerk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>LOCAL MUSICAL SUCCESSES.</head>
        <p>LOCAL MUSICAL SUCCESSES.</p>
        <p>At the examination recently held in connection with Trinity College, London (Liverpool Centre), Miss Esther Bothwell, of Fairclongh-street, and Miss Florence Bolton, Market-street, Earlestown, successfully passed in pianoforte playing in the junior and primary divi- sions, respectively. Miss Lillian Sims, Leigh-street, also passed successfully the primarydivisions in musical knowledge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>HUSBAND, WIFE AND 2 CHILDREN</head>
        <p>HUSBAND, WIFE AND 2 CHILDREN</p>
        <p>Covered with a Red Pimply Rash Itching- Most Distressing—Tried Every Kind of Ointment It Was Possible to Get Without Relief.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>COMPLETELY CURED BY CUTICURA REMEDIES</head>
        <p>COMPLETELY CURED BY CUTICURA REMEDIES</p>
        <p>-cMyself and two little children were covered with a red pimply rash and it was so itchy. I had tried every ointment it was possible to get. Until I tried the Cuticura Ointment, it was the first relief we got. I was afraid to go near a doctor for fear of him making a fuss as of course I knew it must be infectious, for I took it first; then one little one and then the other, and finally, my husband. "I used about twelve or thirteen boxes Cuticura Ointment, but that wasn't much considering all I had to do with it. My own arms and between my fingers, on front of shins and between my toes were completely covered with an ugly itchy rash, and my youngest little girl was covered from crown ta sole. I. thought she would have died. She was only a year and two months at the time. My eldest wasn't so bad, but bad enough. Thanks to the Cuticura Ointment, we are completely cured and you are welcome to take extracts from this if you wish, as I shall recommend the Cuticura Remedies to my friends. Mrs. D. M. Grieve, Donaghmore, Co. Tyrone, Feb. 12 and Feb. 19, 1906."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
