      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>WYCLIFFE CHURCH.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>1 Lessons of the Kingston Calamity. 1 Preaching at Wycliffe Church on Sunday evening the I Rev. john Yonge took for his subject, "After the I earthquake, a fire ; and after the flre,a still small voice." - . The very convulsions of nature,he said,riaight and should speak to men of the Infinite and Almighty Being who was behind, in, and over nature. When the uproar and the fury, and devastation had passed, there came a silence, and a calm, wherein the ear that was sensitive and susceptible might catch the deep and intense cadence of the still small voice of God—" after the wind an earthquake, after the earthquake a fire, and after the fire a still small voice." Recently the world had been witnessing a series of signal catastrophes He did not remember any period of similar brevity which had been so crowded both at home and abroad with calamaties on land and sea. IVhether it had been the tidings of ciuel persecutions of Armenians or Jews, or of sudden political assassinatione or earthquakes, railway catastrophies, a long continued' and multiplying series of disasters had destroyed the i 1 security of human life and the tranquility of mankind. Many of the calamities had perhaps been due to causes 1 which might later be not only minimised but almost entirely prevented. The vengeful hand of the political assassin would be robbed of its weapon when rulers of nations ceased their tyrannies. Anger and malignant Ihatred would die out when equity and liberty were established. Other calamities, as science was applied to industry and labour was guarded by precautions, would also be found to be largely preventable and the immediate significance of such calamities pointed to augmented fore-thought and more vigilant precision and care. But man would never be able to con- trol or prevent the phenomena of nature. Whilst they in England might find an assurrance of security in the fact that they lived in islands which hardly ever experienced any calamity from earthquakes they must not be deaf to the counsels and the warnings given by calamities which occurred in various parts of the earth. The preacher gave a graphic description of the earthquake, quoting largely i also from Mr. Henniker Heaton's message to the i" Tines," and went on to say that it often seemed to him that they allowed themselves to be sadly callous land unconcerned about the calamities which befel their fellow-men. They were keen and quick enough in the feeling of their own misfortunes or- sorrows or suffer- ings ;they were very hungry for sympathy when such experiences betel them, but they heard of the distresses and calamities which betel their fellow men without any very deep sense of pity. It ought not to be so. , They should never read reports of occurrences of calamity 1 without concern and pity for those involved in pain. ; The agonies of the world should humanize mankind. : Was it not one of the teachings c.onveyed by "the still i small Voice" which now followed the earthquake and the fire, the calamity and catastrope which had caused I , havoc and distress? They could appreciate the prompt ; generosity of the King and Queen, the Prince and , Princess of Wales, and the possessors of wealth in the i metropolis and the country which the event in Jamaica ; had already evoked, and if the movement should spread ; through the kingdom it was to be hoped that Warring- ton would not be unwilling to give a helping hand. , Wird did these catastrophes, these awful and resistless • i upheavals of even nature itself say to them of counsel , arid of warning—" Be ye also ready, for in such an hour as ye think not the Son of Man cometh." Did not the ' . "still small Voice "say to all of them " Be still and know that lam God ? " When they witnessed the I Almighty power of the Creator shattering the fabric of His own creation they should be seeking to hide •i themselves in His saving and forgiving love. His power I was infinite and they might find a refuge in Him. 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>SUTTON HARRIERS' INTERNATIONAL VICTORY.</head>
        <p>SUTTON HARRIERS' INTERNATIONAL VICTORY.</p>
        <p>On Sunday the Sutton Harriers achieved a splendid victory at St. Cloud, Paris, winning the international cross-country match with 35 points. The race was watched by over 5,000 spectators. The course was about 7 miles, and the first half dozen home were :- 1. Wilding, Sutton 47 45 2. Joe Bailey, Sutton 3. Drouet, Stade Francais 4. Cousin, Stade Francais 5. whittle, Sutton 4B 24 G. Chapman, Sutton The club placings were :—Sutton Harriers, 3458 p2o7ints; PARISH Couscrf, AfEETING.—At the meeting of the Stade Francais, 55 ; Club Arnica' Sportif de Saint Parish Council after discussing the question of sewer- Mande, Si. ing the district it was decided to take the ratepayers in On their return home to St. Helens on Tuesday night, their confidence at the meeting held for the purpose of the victors were accorded a splendid welcome by an electing the Parish Councillor, to be held in March enormous crowd who awaited their arrival at the and in order to insure a good representative meeting station, and they were enthusiastically cheered. Headed the Clerk was instructed to circularise the parishioners, by tho Sutton Brass Band and several torch-bearers, the It was also decided that the Cheshire Lines Committee champions were driven along the main thoroughfares should be asked to erect a booking office on the south to their headquarters at Sutton where they were enter- side of Padgate Station to meet the convenience of tamed and congratulated on 'their victory. Captain passengers, and in order to avoid the danger of crossing Michael' Hughes, president of Sutton Harriers, who the line. I attended the banquet, returned thanks for the welcome</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>Prostrated by Influenza.</head>
        <p>Prostrated by Influenza.</p>
        <p>Its Dangerous After-Effects. Weak. Melancholy, Unable to Work. This Man Now Strong and Well. Curea by</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>DR.WILLIAMT PINK PILLS</head>
        <p>DR.WILLIAMT PINK PILLS</p>
        <p>"My health was so uncertain at one time that no medicine did anything for me beyond patching me up for a time," said Mr. Thomas Gamble, of 1, Bay-street, Leicester. "Then my system became so undermined that when I was attacked by Influenza the effects on my weak frame were quite alarming. In Dr. Williams' Pink Pills I finally found new strength and a sound cure, but before that happened my state was indeed deplorable. The first sign of things being wrong with me was that I could not do my work quickly or well. I felt worn and weary, and out of sorts. THESE WERE I was at the mercy of any change HIS SYMPTOMS. in the weather. Then came</p>
        <p>_ a violent attack of influenza, with splitting headaches, intense weakness, and a feeling as though I had been beaten all over. I felt utterly powerless ; indeed, on attempting to get out of bed I could not keep myself up, but would fall back helpless. The lightest food caused burning pains to shoot through my chest and stomach. At night sleep deserted me' and my brain reeled with a dull, heavy pain. Sometimes I lost con- sciousness, and remained in that state for hours. Pains flew all over me. I could not control myself, but shivered from head to foot.</p>
        <p>I had been taking medicine all this time, but it did I little for me except to upset my digestion and injure ' my stomach. Even liquid foods upset me. When I did insist on getting about again it was only with the greatest difficulty that I could go upstairs. The palpitation of my heart was so violent that every beat made me flinch with pain. At best I was only able to sit about, utterly despondent, and as weak as a babe. This pitiable state one doator told me was due to acute debility following severe influenza. My whole body remained cold as ice, and it seemed as if my veins were filled with a watery liquid instead of blood. "I had lost all faith in medicinen. and was resigned to my fate, when Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People were recommended to me. I bought a box, and began taking the pills. First my appetite returned, and I enjoyed food without violent pains. I began to feel a '"low of new, warm blood through my whole system. I could sleep well at night and awake refreshed. Before long I was able to take outdoor exercise, and I felt stronger. After six boxes of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills I felt so fit and well that I returned to work. From that day to this I have never known what it is I to be out of sorts, and altogether I am as sound as a I bell."</p>
        <p>For all bodily weaknesses and influenza with its depresiing. dangerous after-effects, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People are most valuable ; they actually make Good, Red Blood that builds up the system anew and gives strength to the vital organs. They have also cured Anemia, Eczema, Rheumatism, Sciatica, Neuralgia, St. Vitus' Dance, Rickets, Paralysis, Locomotor Ataxy, and Ladies' Ailments. Sold by all dealers (look for full name, seven words, on every pack- age), or direct from the British Depot, 49, Hol- born - viaduct, London, post free 2s. 9d. a box, or six for 13s. 9d.</p>
      </div>
