      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>BROKEN-DOWN sysrm‘t.</head>
        <p>A BROKEN-DOWN sysrm‘t.</p>
        <p>This is a condition (or disease) to which doctors give many names, but which few of them really understand. It is simply weaknees—a breakdown, es it were, of the vital forces that sustain the system. No matter what may hi its causes (for they are almpst numberless), its symptoms are much the same ; the more prominent being sleeplessness, sense of prostration or weariness, depression of spirits and want of ' energy for all the ordinary affairs of life Now, what alone 'is absolutely essential in all such cases is INOREASED VITALITY—vigour—VITAL S TRENGTH AND ENERGY to throw off these morbid feelings, and experience proves that as night succeeds the day this may be more certainly secured by a course of the celebrated life-reviving tonic. THERAPION NO 3. than by any other known combination. So surely as it is taken in aocordance with the printed directions eccom panying it, will the shattered health be restored. the EX- PIRING LAMP OF LIFE LIGHTED UP AFREJII, and a new existence imparted in place of what had so lately seemed worn-out, " used up," and valueless. This wonderful re- storative is purely vegetable and innocuous, is agreeable to to the taste—suitable for all constitutions and conditions, in either sex ; and it is difficult to imagine a cite of disease or derangement. whose main features are those of debility, that will not be speedily and permanently benefited by this never-falling recuperative essence, which is destined to cast into oblivion everything the had preceded it for this wile, spread and numerous class of human ailments. THERAPION may be procured in England at 2s. 9d. and 4. 6, post free, for P. 0., from Mr. R. JOHNSON, 43, HOLFORD SQUARE, LONDON, W.C. Purchasers tamed see that the word ' THERAPION ' appears on British Government Stamp tin white letters on a red ground) !affixed on every package by order of His Majesty's lion. Comissioners and without which it is a forgery.</p>
        <p>THE removal of Mr. BIRRELL</p>
        <p>THE MINISTRY. to the Irish Office and Mr. REGINA LD MCKENNA to the Board of Education is only the beginning of the Ministerial changes that will probably be com- pleted before Parliment resumes. It is likely enough that Mr. McKENNA will he succeeded at the Treasury by Mr. WALTER RUNCIMAN, who has also been mentioned in connection with the vacant Under-Secretaryship for India. Mr. RUNCI- MAN is a keen and able man of business, as well as an effective Parliamentary speaker, and his path upwards will probibly be rapid. These changes, and others by which they must be followed, will amount almost to a recon- struction of the Ministry, and will be accom- panied by the introduction of some new blood. Sir HENRY CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN formed his Government, it will be rernr;mbered, mainly from the House of Commons, when the Liberal party was in an ineffective minority, awl when men like Mr. St. JOHN BRODRICK,Mr.GERALD BALFoup.,and Mr. ArtNonn-Fonsrmt were wont to pride them- selves on their intellectual superiority, and their proficiency as statesmen, as compared with, lot us say, Mr. HALDANE, Mr. ASQUITH, Mr. Lr.oYD. GEORGE, and Mr. JOHN MoRnEY. Thero were actually some persons who took the BRODRICK- BALFoUR view of the subject, and really believed that the Liberal party did not contain the materials from which a capable Ministry could be formed.</p>
        <p>It is a little more than a year since the new Government was formed, and a little less than that since it met the new Parliament. During that time several reputations have been made find none have been lost. There have been no failures on the Treasury Bench among the principal members,</p>
        <p>hose who have had responsibility. Sir HENRY 1 CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN has turned out to be, as was expected by those who knew him best, a man of the • clearest political foresight, inflexible in principle, genial in manner, sound in judgment. Mr. ASQUITH has already shewn great capacity as a financier, and continues to be one of the soundest and readiest and most decisive of debaters. Mr. HALDANE has been something of a surprise. His year's work at the War Office .hae revealed him as a statesman and administrator of quite unusual competence. Mr. MORLEY is not much heard as Secretary for India; but those who know what is going on behind the scenes recognise in him • a strong influence, which is being most beneficially exerted in Indian air Airs. Mr. LLOYD GEORGE'S eulogy has just been pronounced at Birmingham by a gentleman who says that there has been no . such man at the Board of Trade since Mr. CHAMBER- ' LAIN. It would be possible, without injustice, to go much further than this. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN failed to carry his Shipping Bi!l ; but Mr. LL-YD GEORGE carried his with apparent ease, and amid the applause of both sides of the House of Commons. At the Local Government Bard Mr. JOHN BURNS has had, we believe, a rather troublesome and worried year. He has had to contend on one hand with the ways of permanent officials who like to keep to the ruts, and on the other with tendencies to extravagance, and a desire to kick over the traces, on the part of public bodies which expected to find him much less practical, and much less firmly disposed to economic administra- tion, than he has proved to be. Mr. BURNS'S next sphere of labour will not improbably be the Home Office, though the change may not be made for some time to come. In his speeches Mr. BIRRELL complains that he has laboured in vain ; but, as a matter of fact, he is far from having done so. Within the Board of Education itself he has produced groat and beneficial changes, which will continue to bear fruit in the years to come. These, we may confidently expect, will be added to by Mr. KENNA who, also, will have to introduce to the House almost immediately the measures through which the Government proposes to deal with the Educa- tion question. We should be glad to hear that one of these will deal with the case of the Passive Resister, whose grievance is one tat an exeeed- ingly short measure would remove.</p>
        <p>There is a strong agitation in Sr. ELPHIN'S the east end of the town for PARK. the purchase by the Corpor- ation of the land fronting St. Elphin's park in order to preserve at least one green oasis in the Manchester-road district. Most of the land belongs to the Training College, but there is a strip along the road, which will pass into the hands of the private speculator unless the Corporation steps in. It would be a thousand pities were this view of the Church and College, and the pleasing glimpse of Cheshire, to 'be obstructed by villa residences. The land would be a good investment for the Corporation. At £3,000 it is not very dear, and it would make an admirable open space where public bowling greens and the like might be provided. Obtained on loan, as the purchase money would have to be, the cost to the rates would not be more than a ninth of a penny in the pound. We hope the General Purposes Committee will, on Monday, consider the proposal in the most favourable light and not be too mercenary in its estimate of the pros and cons.</p>
        <p>"That which we call a rose, "WHAT'S IN A by any other name would NAME ?"'" smell as sweet." Thus</p>
        <p>quoted Mr. Unsworth at the Education Committee meeting on Monday when the question of re-naming People's College was discussed at length and finally leferred back. Apparently there is a good deal in a name at times, and in regard to this particular matter many people have a pet name which they think would do excellently for the People's College, whose time-honoured title has given offence to the susceptibilities of the Board of Education. The school managers were only just in time with the;r suggestion, for the School Management Committee had resolved to call the school after its first headmaster, Mr. Brewtnall. This, the managers considered " singularly undesirable " and offered "The Warrington Arpley-street Schools" as an alternative. We should magine tbis stands the best chance of bong- selected, for so many pupils of different head masters were present at the meeting that it got embarrassed with names and accepted an amend- ment to refer the question back. After further cogitation in private a solution to the problem should be found. If not, the Education Com- mittee had better lay it before Mr. Wallingt )n and his pupils who have a more up-to-date acquaintance with problems and their solution.</p>
        <p>The new street behind TIT fc NEW STREET Bridge•street is already BLOCKED. pretty well blocked at</p>
        <p>both ends, but as the result of the Local Government Board inquiry on Wednesday the scheme is blocked in its entirety. So strong was the opposition to the proposal to carry the street through into Sankey-street and make it a uniform width of 40 feet that this clause was abandoned by the Corporation and with the consent of the Board it will be withdrawn. The tradesmen,property-owners and Non-Traders corn bined to prove that a new street was not required in this position, and to protest against any further expenditure upon it. It seems to us that the Corporation went beyond its powers and acted against the best interests of the ratepayers when it departed from the original idea of a 20 feet passage. This is all that was required, and in fact all that was practical on a site so close to Bridge. street itself. But in order to secure a bargain in land the Corporation has widened the passage to 40 ft. and in order to he consistent would,of course, have to make the two entrances to the street a similar width. This would involve considerable expenditure. and it is this expenditure for some- what flimsy reasons that has been so successfully objected to. A future generation may possibly decide that a street is required here, but at Present it certainly is not and the matter had better remain in status quo rather than that the rates should be burdened any further by completing a scheme .if very questionable utility.</p>
        <p>The proposals of the above TEMPERANCE League for helping forward LEGISLATION LEAGUE. real and material temper-</p>
        <p>ance reform are arousing a great deal of interest all over the country. Tne League ki m b races members of all denominations and schools of political thought, and at the same time it aims at consolidating the efforts for temperance of the teetotaller and the moderate drinker. There can be no question that if the calse of temperance is to make headway at any- thing like a satisfactory rate, the brunt of the task will not have to continue resting upon the shoulders of the total abstainers. The latter only number about one-tenth of the electors and the difficulty of one-tenth securing special legislation in the face of opposition from several of the other tenths is obvi- ous. It is necessary that the services of the temperate man as well as the teetotaller should be enlisted in the cause, and if the new League can succeed in bringing the two upon one platform it will have performed an immeasureable service to the community. Apparently the League is going to succeed to a considerable extent in doing this, and the question is the general adoption or other- wise of those proposals which have already been formulated. That there will be objection</p>
        <p>to some of these, even from inside temberance circles, is evident. The suggested dis- interested management is a Case in point. Many strong teetotallers and members of the U.K.A. are opposed to this idea on principle and the quettion will have to be thoroughly threshed out. Warrington Nonla wi;l have an opportunity of hearing the proposals of the League expounded on *Monday, February 4-.11 at a meeting in Parr Hall to he addressed by the Bishop of Liverpool and Mr. Henry Vivian, M. P. for Birk snhead. We hope that all temperance workers in Warrington will make a point of being present acd familiarising themselves with the detads of the movement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Alacbmerp, etc.</head>
