      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>"Can you speak English ? " asked Judge Strachan of an Italian at the Warrington County Court on Thursday. "No,' was the prompt reply. The laughter which greeted the witness was, however, a little premature, as it transpired that he could not speak any more English and the services of an interpreter had to be requisitioned.</p>
        <p>Earl Grey, the Governor-General of Canada, • has written to Mr. Frederick Litchfield, organizing secre- tary of the Garden City Tenants, Limited, congratu- lating him and Mr. Henry Vivian, M.P., on the book " Garden Suburbs, Villages, and Homes." Lord Grey says : " I think Mr. Vivian is entitled to the grati- tude of all who have the well-being of England at heart for the good work he has done." _ _ _ * * * _</p>
        <p>"Man and Woman were Created equal. Very well, my dear, sttid Man. We've only got one pAir of shoes. You shine them and I'll put them on." This little epitome of the English experience of Mr. Shaparji Sakletvala, of Bombay, convulsed the gathering of men and women who crowded the Con- ference held at the National Liberal Club, under the Rev. Stewart Headlam's presidency, in order to con- sider the question of women's suffrage.</p>
        <p>The verse below was not written at West Ham, but it should have been to have the fullest application. A pauper in West Bromwich Workhouse penned the following lines after objecting to face the 9 hours wood-cutting per day that the Guardians had provided. " The Lord made the bees ; The bees make the honey ; The tramps do the work; And the Guardians get the money."</p>
        <p>As we remarked last week, " The Heathens are nothing if not original." One day this week the resi- dents of Gaskele-street were alarmed at bearing the tramp of many feet. On proceeding to investigate they were entertained by the spectacle of the senior lads attending St. Thomas school, bareheaded, pro- ceeding at a quick trot along the thoroughfare. Acco- mpanied by the masters, they made a circuit by way of Pike-street and Ellison-street, and their afternoon's exercise over,trotted back to school. * * *</p>
        <p>Local Government Board inquiries at Warrington are not the perfunctory affairs they used to be and the Council Chamber was on Wednesday filled with ratepayers anxious to hear the application with refer- ence to the proposed new street. The Inspector must have been surprised when on inquiring if anyone wished to oppose the scheme, he was occupied for several minutes in recording the names of objectors who stood up in quick succession. The proceedings were followed with the greatest interest and occasion- ally there were outbursts of applause. * * * . .</p>
        <p>The penny popular con-certs held in Bold-street schools hare undoubtedly filled a great need. If any evidence of the fact were needed, it was afforded on Saturday when the room was crowded to overflowing. To provide pleasant and wholesome entertainments at a charge within the reach of all is one of the best ways of counteracting the attractions of the streets and the public-houses and we are glad this effort has been taken up with so much enthusiasm. The committee have generously decided to devote to-night's proceeds to the Jamaica Relief Fund and there should be a cordial response to the appeal for the sufferers by the great disaster. *</p>
        <p>The weather has been playing pranks this week, but the enthusiastic skater w ill not mind so long as opportunities are afforded for his favourite winter pastime. It is remarkable, however, that there have been 41 degrees of frost in Berlin, 17 in Paris, and that Constantinople has been snowed up, while Iceland has been basking in warm airs, the Hebrides have been untouched by frost, and St. Petersburg has experienced the mildest spell of winter since 1836. The climatic conditions certainly seem topsy-turvy in many parts of the world, though they are not strikingly abnormal in this country. The vacant seat on the Town Council caused by the death of Councillor Brough, one of the representatives of Whitecross Ward, will be filled on Monday, February 4th. Only one candidate is in the field, i.e., Mr. Joseph Hawthorn, the Liberal nominee, and it is hoped that he will have a walk-over, especially as he will have to appeal to the electors again in No vember next. Mr. Hawthorn is a well-known local journalist and until the end of 1904 he was on the staff of this paper. Since then he has joined the " Liverpool Post and Mercury," but still retains his home and many interests in Warrington. He is closely identified with Wycliffe Church, and is also chairman of the Junior Liberal Association. We have no doubt that Mr. Hawthorn will make an excellent representative of the ratepayers and of the Ward in which he has lived for so many years. *</p>
        <p>The question of selecting- headmasters for the new Warrington Council schools from the ranks of the local teachers was fully debated on Monday without any decision being come to by the Education Com- mittee. Mr. Upson made out a strong case for the adoption of a resolution which would mare local appointments compulsory in q.ll cases, but the balance of opinion was against so drastic an instruction. It appears to us that the Committee has succumbed to the popular demand, but wishes to climb down gracefully, and so the question has been adjourned for a month in order that some acceptable basis of action may be formulated in Committee. The teachers have apparently fought the battle and won the victory. They are entitled to more than a sentimental preference " all things being equal" and we hope no such equivocal clause will be permitted to come into force. Promotion by merit ought to obtain here as it does in most educational centres.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>RAILWAY TRAGEDY. Warrington Man Found Shot.</head>
        <p>RAILWAY TRAGEDY. Warrington Man Found Shot.</p>
        <p>_ - - - A considerable sensation was caused in Warrington yesterday by the news of the tragic death of Mr. Prediarick Plinston (24), Bloomfield Cottage, Appleton, who was well known in local athletic circles. On Thursday evening, about half-past eleven, a ticket- collector at Edge-hill Station, Liverpool, found deceased in a second-class compartment of a train which had just arrived from Manchester, lying dead with a bullet wound in his head. It was considered desirable to allow the body to remain in the carriage during the rest of the journey to the terminus at Lime-street, information having been meantime conveyed to the police. The body was speedily conveyed in the ambu- lance from Lime-street to the Royal Infirmary. Beside the body in the railway carriage was a Volunteer rifle, recently discharged. Deceased, who was a plumber by trade, was a Volunteer, being a corporal in the 3rd Cheshires, stationed at Lymm, and was also connected with the Warrington Hockey Club and the Warrington Cricket Club. Prior to his mother's death some months ago he, along with two other brothers assisted in carry- ing on business at the -King's Arms Hotel, Buttermarket- street, Warrington.</p>
