      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>toming Events. At Monday's meeting of the Junior Liberal Associa- tion Mr. C. E. Parker will give a paper on -" Socialism." The annual ball in connection with the Warrington Market Tenant's Association will be held in the Parr Hall on February 14th. The annual congregational gathering in connectioy with Cairo-street Unitarian Church will be held on 'Monday evening in the schools. A meeting in connection with the anniversary of • the Bewsey-road Wesley Guild will be held on Monday evening at which a lecture on Charles Kingsley will be delivered by the Rev. Bevan Shepherd, M.A., Principal of the Frodsham Chilireii's Home. The annual ball in connection with the Warrington Butchers' Association will be held in the Parr Hall on Thursday next. Mr. Starkey's band will be in attendance. The annual tea party in connection with St. Paul's Church will be held in the Parr Hall on Monday evening. On Tuesday the annual dance 'will be held in the Parr Hall. The annual dinner in connection with the Grammar School Old Boys' 'Union, will be held at the Li on Hotel on Thursday evening next. It is expected' that Lord Lilford and the Mayor will be present. Under the auspices of the Temperance Legislation League a mass meeting will be held in the Parr Hall on February 4th, at which addresses will be delivered by the Bishop of Liverpool, and Mr. Henry Vivian, M.P. An open meeting of the Warrington.P.S.A., will be held in the Sankey-street Co-operative Hall to-morrow, Councillor Steel will preside and solos will be given by Miss V. Brown and Miss E. Barnes. UNDER THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS. Advt.— Sermons were preached on Sunday last at Sankey 'Church in the morning and evening in aid of the funds of the Diocesan C.E.T.S. by the Rev. J. Hirst. He also gave an address at the Penketh Mission in the afternoon. The offertories smounted to £4 7s. 11d. Local Dog Fancier's Success. At the annual show of the Northern Collie and Fox Terrier Clubs at Blackpool on Saturday, in the class for Sehipperkes Mr. Richard Daniels Warrington, won the second prize for a dog or bitch under 111 b. The New Theatre. We understand that the Palace Theatre and Hippodrome which is to be erected on the site off Barbauld-street will be the property of the Macnaghten Vaudeville Circuit, and that preparations for the work of construction are well advanced. The company is one of the leading concerns of its kind in the country, owning theatres in London, Blackburn, Bradford, Bath, Halifax, West Hartlepool, Lincoln, Leicester, Sheffield, Nottingham, Leeds, Rotherham, Chester- field, Attercliffe and Southampton. St. James' Cricket Club. The annual meeting of the above club was held on Saturday evening, the Rev. H. B. Firth presiding. The Hon. Treasurer submitted a satisfactory -report and the Hon. Secretary gave a report of last season's matches, Br.c. The election of officers :—President, Rev. H. B. Firth; vice-president, Rev. H. H. Corsbie; eaptain, J. L. Forster; vice-captain, C. Holt:' hon. secretary, J. Grounds; hon. treasurer, E. Jackson Mee • committee, F. It. C. Tole, W. H. Minards, and A. B. Todd. It was proposed to ask a number of gentlemen to act as vice-presidents. LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Adtv. Lancashire's Higher School Rate. The Lancashire Education Committee on Monday decided to ask the County Council to apply to the Local Government Board for a continuance Of the orders granted last year authorising that a rate of 23d. in the pound he levied -for higher education.—Sir Henry Hibbett thought it had been shown tO the satisfaction of everybody that in a great industrial comity like Lancashire higher education was of such great importance that secondary education work could not be financed at the limit imposed by the 1902 Act. Visit to Gasworks. On Saturday last the Warrington Technical Students' Science Association paid a visit to the Longford Gas works. About thirty members assembled at the Central depot, where they were joined by Haddock, who kindly conducted the visitors round the works, with the assistance of Mr. Mac Nicol and Mr. Robinson. Great interest was taken in the various processes through which the gas passes before it is fit for use, and also in the by-products, which were explained by the guides. After going round the works, the members left, after having spent a most enjoyable and interesting afternoon. ,PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCISHIRE.—AdVt. Taxation of Land Values. At the seventh fortnightly meeting of the Warring- ton Branch of the E.L.T.L.V., Mr. A. Hulme in the chair, it was decided in view of the fact that the Postmaster General is coming to Warrington on February Ist, to postpone the Co-operative Hall meeting from that night to February 15th,and to have on February Bth another rehearsal of the six chapters of "The story of my Dictatorship"which are to be per- formed a week later in the Cooperative Hall. Mr. Hulme's report that Mr. Carnegie had published in -the" Review of Reviews "a remarkable article in which he proved that all the earnings of a millionaire came from the land and were rightly earned by the whole of the nation and therefore proposed that all the great fortunes ought to be taken by the State at the death of the owner, caused Mr. Ockel to express the -hope that Mr. Carnegie might still develop like the millionaire Tom L. Johnson. who once asked why he was spending so much on the Single Tax, answered: " I would prefer to leave my children penniless, but in a state of society in which they always could com- mand the full fruits of their labonr, than to leave them my millions in the present state of society, which enabled me to earn them." It is interesting to learn how Mr. Johnson got converted. He wanted to prove the fallacies of , Henry George, and therefore , studied his book "Progress and Poverty." But he! seemed to be enchanted! The more he studied it, the more he was forced to agree with Henry George. Sol he went to his lawyer and asked him,whether he could disprove the fallacies of "Progress and Poverty."—. " Nothing easier than that !" said the lawyer.—Then do it—" I have no time for that "—Well do it as an I order from me, as your client said the millionaire.— ' After five weeks the lawyer came to him and asked : 1 -4' Do you acknowledge George's premises as right, that every child born has a right to life, liberty and pursuit of happiness ?" " Yes I do "—was the answer. "Then George is right and cannot be con- -tradicted !" said the lawyer. Consequently Tom L. Johnson visited Henra,George and became one of the staunchest supporters of the Single Tax. He it was who brightened the last years of Henry George, who gave him the means necessary for the leisure to write his last great work ; "Science of Political Economy" 'The German Single Tax paper:"Deutsche Volksstimme" contains in the last number an article by Damaschke, who is putting up for the Reichstag Election showing how the troubles in German South-West Africa are also due to land-monopoly. More than one third of the territory belongs to six companies which -stand under the influence of English speculators. How 'little these companies add to the colonisation of the country is shown by the mightiest of all, the South- West African Company. which holds the most valin - able mining rights and which has brought into the country in every four employees,one white one and three coloured ones. How this company secured for itself the sole mining right in 0 vamboland is very significant. Once upon a time this company had managed to get from the German Colonial Office the sole right of building railways. Remember the right but not the duty! Ten years ago in 1897-revolts started and a railway from the port to the capital become necessary. The company flatly refused to build the railway. When the German Government laid the railway itself, the English Company forbade the us of steam and the mighty German Empire was forced to pull the cars by donkeys! Englishmen and natives watched it laughingly. At last the South-West African Com- pany allowed the use of locomotives after having sipeezed out of the Colonial Office the sole mining Tight in the whole of the Ovambo country. TABLE, FLOOR, AND HANGING LAMPS in large variety. Latest patterns.—J. S. GARLAND, Iron- monger Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Suicide Follows Trouble. Mr. S. Brighouse and a jury incjuired into a sad case of suicide at Warrington yesterday. On Wed- nesday Joseph Lawless, 47, Orford-street, Warrington was found hanging in the back kitchen of his house and when cut down was found to be dead. It appeared that the deceased had been out of work and that recently his daughter was killed by being run over by a motor car. Depression followed these troubles—a depression which was increased later by the illness of his wife which necessitated her removal to the hospital. —Commenting on the case Mr. Brighouse said trouble always seemed to visit those who did not deserve it. He supposed, however, that it was the inscrutable way of Providence and they must not complain.—A verdict of "Suicide whilst of unsound mind "was returned. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—IINDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Wesleyan Bible Classes. A scheme is on foot to amalgamate all the Bible Classes attached to the Wesleyan Churches in War- rington and district and a meeting of the whole cf the members of the classcs will be held in the Bold-street Schoolroom in the early part of next month for the purpose of inaugurating some addresses to be given by the various Presidents of the classes.—A. General Committee consisting of representatives for all the classes has already been formed to carry the object out. The Rev. John Gunnell, of Penketh, gave an amusing lecture entitled " The spice and life of , letters," at the meeting of the Wesley Guild on ' Monday. During his address Mr. Gunnell quoted ' many different kinds of humour and gave reading4' from Johnson, Lamb, Artemus Ward, Mark Twain,&amp;c. He also gave some very good exaples of the uncon- scious humour of children. " HERALD " KITCHEN RANGES and " Peveril " Fire Grates are hard to beat.—Sole agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. Dr. Markel. We are asked to correct a statement which has appeared in the press that Dr. Markel has been appointed by the Cheshire County Council as its representative on the governing body of the Warring- ton Grammar School. Dr. Markel was approached for the purpose but chiefly on the ground of health he was compelled to decline the honour. Dr. Markel has just returned from Switzerland, where he has been spending a ftw weeks in the Bemese Oberland. It may interest their friends to know that Dr. Markel and Miss Markel, both of whom are most enthusiastic lagers, were successful in a numbers of races, winning the second prize in the race for the Montreux Cup and creating a new record for sleighs steered by ladies. The bobsleigh steered by Miss Markel, with Dr. Markel at the brake, covered the famous course from the Col de Sonloup to Les Avants, in something over two miles, at an average speed of about 42 miles an hour. As the course has a number of sinuosities with very sudden returns which have to be taken with great caution, some idea may be formed of the speeds attained on the straight and incidentally of the risks that are run. The courses are remarkable quick this year throughout Switzerland.</p>
        <p>_ - STRANGE'S A.l. Crystal LIMP OIL in barrels or drums delivered free.—Sole agent, J. S. OARLA_Np, 51 to 55, Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Popular Penny Concerts. The patrons of these concerts held weekly in the Bold-street school were on Saturday afforded an exceptionally pleasant evening, a special musical pro- gramme arranged by Mrs. Flubi-Newcombe being submitted. The large attendance must have been exceedingly gratifying to the promoters, the room being filled to the doors. _ The programme opened with a quartette " Anchored " given in pleasing style by Miss Brown, Miss Houghton, Mr. Martley and Mr. Fred Stanley. Miss Florence Brown, who posseses a sweet and flexible voica, sang "April Morn" and "The Nightingale's trill" most effectively. Miss Marie Houghton had a very cordial reception and,i her singing delighted the audience. Her first item " Tyrolienne " was artistically given and called forth an encore which was responded to with the ever- popular "Kilarney." Later Miss Houghton inter- preted two charming little songs by Mrs. Habi- Newcombe " Allan-a-dale " and " Posies ' which„were well received and her vocalisation of " Vainka's song" was alao highly creditable. Miss Dorothy Truslove, a promising singer, gave a praiseworthy rendering of The Nightingale. Mr. George Stanley sang "The storm fiend" and "Three for ,Tack" with fine spirit, and Mr. Fred Marltey's • feeling interpretations of " Alice " and "Goodbye "met with much favour. Me. Joseph Forrester thoroughly pleased the assembly with Two bhoys from Cork'," "Simon the Cellarer ' and "Jack's Sweethearts," the latter being the com- position of Mrs. Hubi-Newcombe. Other enjoyable features were duets by Miss Brown and Mr. Forrester, and Mr. Fred Martley and Mr. George Stanley. Miss Lily Rocks was an able accompanist and also cleverly executed two pianoforte solos. The Rev. W. Mellor presided and during the evening made an excellent sugc,rgestion which will be acted upon by the com- mittee - that the proceeds of to-night's concert be devoted to the Jamaica Relief Fund. WOOD CHIMNEY PacEs, Fire' Grates and Tiles. • unrivalled soletion, inspection invited. —J. S. GARLAND Builders' Ironmonger, &amp;c., 51 to 55, Bridge at., Warringtog.—Advt. The City Beautiful. A meeting was held at the Town Hall, Liverpool, on Monday, for the purpose of making arrangements for the second conference of those interested in the beautifying of cities. Among the speakers was the Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson who was very heartily received. The Canon said that some of them who had been interested in various kinds of work in beautify- ing.their towns had been struck by the number of societies scattered throughout the land whose objects were to enfleavour to beautify the cities and brighten the lives of the poor people who lived in them. As an example of the excellent work done in that direction he instanced the securing of that beauty spot, Gow- barrow Fell, which was opened to the public last year, and also spoke of the garden city and garden suburbs movement. There were also societies like the Selborne- Society, who interested themselves in the natural history of the country and endeavoured to protect the birds. Some little time ago it occurred to some of those whose work lay in trying to beautify their cities that it would be a useful thing if the representatives of these societies in all parts of the country gathered together in mutual conference, and by exchanging views see if they could help one another in the work. The first conference was held in Manchester last June, and they received such encouragement that it was suggested the conference should be made a permanent annual institution under the title of "The City Beautiful Conference." ;Now they came to ask if Liverpool would extend to &amp;Ina gut hospitality for which it was so famous as they wanted to hold the second conference in fiiverpool. There were many societies,includiug the Kyrie Society i and the Ruskin Society,that had done good work in that I city, and lately a branch of the City Beautiful Society I had been established there, so the seed was sown. I (Applause.) Addresses were delivered by Sir Edward IRussell, Sir Edward Hampson and others, and it was , decided to hold the second annual City Beautiful 1 Conference in Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>WHITECROSS WARD VACANCY. The Liberal Candidate.</head>
        <p>WHITECROSS WARD VACANCY. The Liberal Candidate.</p>
        <p>The election to fill the casual vacancy in Whitscross Ward created by the death of Councillor Walter Brough will take place on Monday, February 4th, and 5 p.m. on Monday next is the latest hour for receiving nomina- tions. Mr. Joseph Hawthorn, a well-known local journalist, employed in Liverpucl, but residing at 27, Lovely-lane, will be the Liberal candidate and every effort will be put forth to secure his return. A few days ago a deputation consisting of repre- sentatives of the Liberal Ward Committee waited upon Mr. El. Roberts asking him to contest the Whitecross Ward in the Liberal interest to supply the vacancy caused by the death of Councillor Walter Brorigh. He replied to the effect that, whilst he warmly appreciated the desire of the committee, he considered it necessary there should be some indications as to the real feeling of the electors before giving his final decision. He could only look favourably upon such a request if i backed up n some substantial form by evidence from the electors of the wars i indepfindently of party. The committee were unable to obtain that evidence, and Mr. Roberta would not, therefore, allow himself to be nominated.</p>
        <p>PRINTING OF ALL DESCRIPTIONS, IN ALI , ITS BR \NCB MS. PROSPECTUSES. PROGRAMMES. RULES BOOKS. AND ALL KINDS OF LETTERPRESS AND LITHOGRAPHIC PRINTING. JOHN WALKER &amp; Co.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, January 26, 1907. YISIT OF MR. SYDNEY BUXTON, M.P. ; "DR. WAKE'S PATIENT." Next Friday's Liberal Denwistration. DRAMATIC PERFORMANCES IN PARR HALL</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 26, 1907. YISIT OF MR. SYDNEY BUXTON, M.P. ; "DR. WAKE'S PATIENT." Next Friday's Liberal Denwistration. DRAMATIC PERFORMANCES IN PARR HALL</p>
        <p>Great interest is being taken in the demonstration promoted by the Lancashire and Cheshire Young British Liberals, and the Warring,ton Junior Liberal Associations, which will be held in the Parr.liall on. Friday evening next. A. crowded attendance is antici- pated as the principal speaker will be a popular Cabinet Minister in the person of the Right Hon. Sydney Buxton, M.P., Postmaster- General. Daring the short period in which he has held the offica Mr. Buxton has done a great deal of useful work which is apprecigted by the workers in the postal service. He is a man of great experience in many branches of public work and has represented Poplar since 1886. He held a seat on the London School Board from 1876 to 1882 and other important duties which he has undertaken include services on the Conciliation Committee in connection with the Dock strike of 1889, the Royal Commission on Education, 1886-89, and Income Tax Committee 1901. He was Under Secretary for the Colonies from 1892 to 1895 and among his other Parliamentary work may be mentioned that he was author of the "Fair Wages" resolution. Mr. Buxton possesses considerable literary ability and has written a number of valuable books on political questions as well as on fishing and shooting which are his favourite recreations. We are sure Mr. Buxton will have a very cordial reception from War- rington progressives. Other prominent politicians are expected to be present and Mr. C.-E. Parker will preslde. -Ara</p>
        <p>BOATING FATALITY IN THE SHIP CANAL. On Monday, Percy Taylor, aged fifteen years, of Weston, was drowned under pathetic circumstances. He was a telephone boy on the Weston Marsh Lock of the Ship Canal, at the entrance to Weaver Canal, and I was crossing the waterway to his work in a small boat. The public of Warrington owes much gratitude to One moment he was seen sculling all right, and the next societies like the Warrington Dramatic Society for ,he was struggling in the water. Walter Hough. captain their annual efforts in the cause of charity and their of a fiat, threw the drowning boy a rope;:but he miseed success in providing that high-class entertainment it and sank. His body was recovered in ten minutes, but all attempts at resuscitation failed. Nobody saw which is often lacking in the town. We maker bold to say that there are no more popular features of the how Taylor got into the water. winter season locally than the performances of the ! THE ALLEGED SILVER THEFTS. amateur operatic and dramatic societies. This week ' At the Widnes Police Court, on Tuesday, Michael has seen the members of the Dramatic Society very Riley, Esther-street, a foreman silver refiner at Messrs. - Dennis's Cornubia Capper Smelting Works, was again much to the fore. With the chief local theatre burnt out they have had the boards to themselves in Parr brought up on remand, charged with stealing 61b. 10oz. of metallic silver value £2l the property of the coin- Hall and attracted large audiences to witness the palsy. After hearing evidence Mr. Riley asked the production of "Dr. Wake's Patient," a comedy in bench if they considered the case proved, to which three acts by W. Gayor Macay and Robert Ord. It Major Waring (chairman) said: We consider it a case affords us much gratification to be able to say that the that should go for trial. Upon being cautioned, Riley , best histrionic traditions of this old-established society said: All I have to say is what I have said before— havethat lam not guilty. My calls at the Bradley Hotel , been fully maintained during the week, and that and at Carrol's shop door were perfectly innocent calls. : the performances have been marked by a uniform I have never stolen any silver, nor yet asked anyone excellence and display of real talent for the actor's to sell any silver for me. I had too good a place, and art. We cannot say that we are much enamoured of was too proud of my masters, to be tempted to do any- the play itself. "Dr. "Wake's Patient "is not a great thing. I feel sure they do not believe me guilty, which comedy, nor does it present many Pinero-like sitria- helps me to bear up through the trial. —Riley was com- tions, the genius and delicacy of which are so mitted for trial at the Liverpool Assizes,which are to be held nextm(th, bail being allowed in one sum of £5O a' palatable to the present-day audience. Perhaps it is two sureties of £5 each. I unfair to look for the mastery of Pinero in a play ' which is not from his pen, but the fact remains that ' ALLEGED THEFTS OF LEAD AT WIDNES, in constructive detail "Dr. Wake's Patient" is some- ' At Widnes, on Monday, Peter Grady, thirty-seven, of what ordinary. It tells the story of an accident in the Elizabeth-street, was charged with stealing 741 b. of lead, the property of the Widnes Alkali Company, cbuntry which throws togethtr the beautiful daughter between the Bth and 12th inst.—From complaints of an earl and the brilliant son of hunshle parents in received Sergeant Davidson and Police-constable Jones the person of the distinguished Dr. Wake. They are secreted themselves in an entry, and at 6 a.m. on the thus made acquainted in the first act, and love at first 12th inst., they saw Grady enter the yard of Michaal sight is a noticeable symptom on both sides. Before M'Conville, a marine store dealer, and throw the lead they are aware of each other's name and pedigree against a wall. On being questioned he said he had. the Fates separate the two for