      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>MEETING OF MAGISTRATF.3.</head>
        <p>MEETING OF MAGISTRATF.3.</p>
        <p>Big Changes in the Licensing Committee</p>
        <p>A. meeting of the Borough Bench was held yester- day afternoon for the purpose of appointing the Licensing Committee, in view of the fact that the Brewster Sessions will be -held on February &amp;h. The Mayor (Alderman Smethurst) presided over a good attendance.—The retiring .committee consisted of Mr. JohnFairclough(chairman),Alderm an Francomb, Alder- man Tinnion, Dr. E. A. Fox, Messrs. E. J. Guest, J. Geddes, J. W. Hayward, 11. Roberts 4ud Charles White. There was a long and lively discussion, Mr. S. Wright contending that it was wrong that certain magistrates should be debarred from deciding licen- sing matters. He moved that the whole Bench should constitute the Licensing Committee. Councillor Wilkinson seconded. An alternative suggestion was made by Alderman Tinnion that a committee of twelve should be appointed. On a• vote being taken the resolution that the committee should consist of the whole of the magistrates was carried by ten votes to nine. It was farther decided that seven members should form a quorum, although Peveral magistrates expressed the opinion that that number was too small. Under the old system of delegating the duties to a committee, the whole Bench had if they chose to exercise it, the power to veto the granting of new licences even if the committee were in favour of it. They could not, however, interfere if the Committee refused to grant a new licence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>GENERAL BULLER'S VISIT.</head>
        <p>GENERAL BULLER'S VISIT.</p>
        <p>The visit of Sir Redvers B tiller, V.C., to Warring- ton on Thursday, February 21st, to unveil the soldiers' memorial in Queen's Gardens, is being looked forward to with considerable interest and pleasure. We understand that the General has acceded to the request of the Executive Committee to perform the ceremony at ree o'clock in the after- noon instead of at mid-day as previously pro- posed. In all probability the distinguished visitor will be invited to lunch at the Parr Hall prior to the ceremony in Queen's Gardens. Sir Redvers will be escorted from Bank Quay station to the Town Hall where he will be received by the Mayor. Either before or after the luncheon a pro- cession will take place_, the route being Sankey-street, Bridge-street, round the Arpley Cautions to the Parr Hall or Queen's Gardens. It is expected that about 1,000 soldiers from Aldershot and other centres will be in attendance. In another column the Committee appeal for sub- scriptions to defray the cost of erecting the memorial, providing granite steps, &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>WARRINCTON FREE CHURCH COUNCIL Annual Meeting.</head>
        <p>WARRINCTON FREE CHURCH COUNCIL Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>---- Mr. C. E. Parker pr3sided over the annual meeting of the Warrington Free Church Cou;lca which was held at Bold-street Church Parlour last evening. There was a good attendance. The financial statement showed an income of £2O 9s. Bd., and that the year closed with a debit bllance of £5 Os. led. Mr. Ormiston presented the accounts of the Winwick Free Church, showing that the income had exceeded the expenditure by £l2. There was a debt of £377 on the new building and he appealed for financial assistance from the Free Churches of the town.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS. Rev. J. YONGE moved the re-election_ of Mr. C. E. Parker as president; the Rev. F. VAUGHAN seconded, and the resolution was unanimously and heartily approved. The Revs. J. Yonge, J. L. Milne, J. S. Hughes, Mr. E. England, and Mr. E. J. Guest were elected vice- presidents. The Rev. J. F. Matthews and Mr. Pearce Williams were re-elected secretaries. Mr. Winstanley was re-appointed treasurer. The executive was con- stituted as follows : Messrs. R. Wyllie, T. Dale, J. Griffiths, E. Sanders, W. Winstanley, J. Walters, C. Wilcockson, R. Pearce Williams, T. Deakin, NV. Clat- worthy, R. Hazledene, J. Shaw, Evan E. Hughes, NV. Brown, E. England, E. J. Guest, J. Longland, R. Ormiston, and J. Holland, together with the ministers of the various churches. It was felt that in the erection of the Winwick Free Church the Free Church Council had accepted responsi- bility for its future and the question of assisting the Church in the liquidation of the debt was left to the Executive Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH SUPPLIES A MINISTER FOR GOLBORNE.—Mr. William Arnold, monumental mason, 4&amp;c., Old London-road, Stockton Ifeath, has received a unanimous and hearty call to undertake the pastoral charge of the Baptist Church, Golborne, and has felt impelled to accept the same and enter upon the full work of the ministry. By his departure a well known firm and name that has been in the district for four or five generations will be removed. From childhood Mr. Arnold has been connected with Hill Cliff Sunday school and then afterwards with the Church as Sunday school teacher and lay preacher of a very acceptable character. Some years ago he spent a year or two in America, and on his return became a manager of a firm in Liverpool. Here he allied himself with the Kensington Baptist Church (Rev. H. Dunnico's) and was practically placed in charge of a Mission Church in Old Swan where he greatly endeared himself to the people. On the death of his' father four years ago, he returned to his native ' village' and continued hi a late father's business with success at the same time entering with a full heart into Christian work, both amongst all the Free Churches and at Hill Cliffe where his presence and help will be keenly missed. In entering upon the work of the ministry, he sacrificed atuccessful business' career, but is realizing the cherished hope of his earlier manhood and undertakingwork for which he has special gifts and qualifications, and all who known him wish him every success in his sacred calling.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>!RESIGNATION OF THE REV. J. S. HUGHES.</head>
        <p>!RESIGNATION OF THE REV. J. S. HUGHES.</p>
        <p>At the annual business meeting of the Hill Cliffe Baptist Church held on Tuesday evening, the Pastor, the Rev. J. S. Hughes, submitted his resignation after having teld the position for over thirteen years. The Church however, refused to accept it and after speak- ing in the highest terms of his services; hoped he would continue. The question is now under recon- sideration by Mr. Hughes, who will give his final answer in a short time: Wo are informed that even if Mr. Hughes resigns his charge of the church, it is not his intention to remove from the district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>CRAWFORD'S</head>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
