      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Tree-Planting in Church-street.</p>
        <p>, An important part of the scheme of the Beautiful Warrington Society is to relieve the daluess of the streets by the planting of trees. The Corporation have I given forty trees for this purpose and on Thursday I afternoon the ceremony of planting six plane trees in Church-street was witnessed. Despite the bitterly cold weather there was a good ;attendance, amongst those present being the Mayor and Mayoress, Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson (chairman of the Beautiful Warring- ton Society), Rev. C. Harvey Cook (chairman of the Tree-Planting Committee), Alderman Bennett, Coun- cillors Sankey, Clegg, Dickenson, Wilding, Cunnington, and Chantler, Captain Crosileld, Messrs. C. E. Parker, J. Longland, J. Fairhurst, C.Plinston, L. Bennett, William Houghton, A. Mounfield, J. F. Whittle, J. Gandy. W. Bibby, G. T. Rushton and J. Ryan (Secretary of the Tree-Planting Committee.) Canon STEVENSON cordially welcomed the Mayor and Mayoress and said the Society felt very grateful to the I Corporation, especially to the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee, for having granted their request to Ido something in the way of tree planting. They were beginning with Church-street and they all hoped that, Ihaving made a good beginning, they would carry on the work in successive streets and have the pleasure of seeing street after street in their good old town planted I with trees. They had in the Mayor a very warm ; supporter of the Society and they appreciated his I kindness in advocating the claims of the movement on ' every possible occasion. (Applause). • The Rev. C. HARVEY CsaoK said that by his patronage of that movement the Mayor was not only immortalising I his Mayoralty but was doing work which would perhaps I outlast some of that done in the Council Chamber. (Hear, hear). There was a Sanitary Committee which I had to do with the health of the public and which kept I its movements underground, but the Beautiful Warring- ton Society believed in the open air treatment and wanted to carry on the sanitation of the atmosphere. There was no reason why they should shorten their lives by making their surroundings ugly. From some motive or other, they did make then ugly and then because people did not love the beautiful they turned round and condemned them. They believed that environment and education had a great moulding influence on the lives of the people, and by the work of that Society they hoped to do a little to mould the sentiment of the love of the beautiful. (Applause). The Mayoress then planted the first tree, and others were planted by Mrs. Sankey, Mrs. Harvey Cook, the Mayor, Alderman Bennett and Councillor Clegg. Mr. C. E. PARKER expressed the indebtedness of the Society to those who had assisted in the cere)nony, and said he hoped they would live long to watch the growth and development of the trees. They were all glad to have taken part in introducing a feature of beauty and a green spot in Church-street. (Applause). The MAYOR thanked Canon Stevenson, Mr. Ryan and the chief workers in the movement, and urged the inhabitants of the street to take care of the trees.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Services for Sunday. Wycliffe Congregational Church, preacher .10.30 and 6.30, Rev. J. Willcock, Southport. Cairo-street Unitarian Church, preacher 10.45 and 6.30 Rev. Lawrence Schroeder, M.A. Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church (Guild Anniversary), preacher 10.30 and 6.30 Rev. Bevan Shepherd, M.A., principal of the Frodsham Children's Home. Bold-street Wesleyan Church, preachers 10.30 Rev. J. Manning; 6.30 Rev. E. J. Gawne. Latchford Baptist Church, preacher Rev. J. F. Matthews. S übjects, 10.30 "Privileged spectators," 6.0 " A daring theology." Golborne-street Baptist Church, preacher 10.30 ani 6.30 Rev. A. Hare, Little Leigh.</p>
        <p>Workingmen's Mission. The annual congregational gathering in connection with the above Chapel was held on Monday. About 180 of the members and friends sat down to tea in the schoolroom at six o'clock. After tea a concert was held, presided over by Mr. William Roberts, J.P. —The Chairman congratulated the Mission workers on the successful year they had experienced. He was very much astonished to know of the great number of scholars they had at the Mission. He used to take a great pride in collecting statistics relating to the various schools of the town, and he believed he was right in saying that theirs was the biggest school in the district. He expressed the hope that the success which had attended their efforts in the past would be with them in the future. An excellent programme was submitted by the Warrington Juvenile Harmonic Prize Choir (conductor, Mr. T. Brough) •' the choruses "Wake, Wake, Wake," "Gipsies Laughing Trio," and "Don't forget the old folks," being exceedingly well rendered. Miss Emily Dunn was heard to advantage in the song "In Zanzibar," and a duet" Pollywolky Doddle " by two of the youngest members of the choir (Misses Brough and Barton) received an encore. An action song by the choir, entitled "Soldiers in the Park" was well rendered.—Master R. Lawton was the humorist of the evening, his amusing song,: and stories causing much merriment, he being recalled time after time. Mr. C. Morgan, elocutionist, of Manchester, was heartily, applauded for his recitations.—A vote of thanks, pro- posed by Mr. T. Dale, to all those who had contributed to the success of the evening, brought a very enjoyable entertainment to an end.</p>
        <p>In his annual repOrt. the HON. SECRETARY Mr. A. Tilling, spoke of the improvement of the Mission in the early part of the year by the beautiful stained glass window erected by Mrs. Crosfield in memory of the founder of the Mission, the late Mr. John Crosfield. (Applause.) For some months they had been contem- plating the introduction of a new hymn book but up to the present no choice had been made. It was felt to be desirable that as they were an undenominational Mission they should use an undenomination.al hymn book, but it was rather a difficult matter to get hold of a book exactly suited to their requirements. The chapel accounts showed an income of £10.5 and the year closed with a balance in hand of £5 14s. (Applause.) On the Poor Fund account they had a balance in hand of £1 4s. They also had in hand the sum of .C 6 16s. subscribed by the young ladies' class towards the decoration of the organ. It had not been thought advisable up to the present to have that work done, but perhaps daring the ensuing year the decoration might be carried out. (Hear, hear.) The individual Communion cups, pur- chased for the Mission by Mr. J. J. Crosfield were used for the first time on the first Sunday in 1907. The gift was highly appreciated and the cups would undoubtedly add to the solemnity of the sacramental service. (Applause.) The success which bad attended the work of the past year was very encouraging to the Mission workers and they trusted that in the future all the workers and friends, as in the past, would labour harmoniously together. (Applause). The officers for the present year are :—President, Mr. N. Hough; vice-president, Mr. G. Dale; treasurer, Mr. T. Taylor; secretary, Mr. A. Tilling, organist and choirmaster, Mr. F. Atkinson, committee, Messrs. T. Dale, D. Hill, T. Taylor, F. Atkinson, W. Worrall, H. Edwards, R. C. Tanner and T. Edmudds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>WYCLIFFE CHURCH TEA MEETING.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE CHURCH TEA MEETING.</p>
        <p>Successful Gathering.</p>
        <p>The congregational tea meeting in connection with Wycliffe Congregational Church which took place in the Memorial Buildings on Thursday everting was one of the most successful and enjoyable gatherings of the kind held for many years. Admirable arrangements had been made and all present were highly pleased with the evening's entertainment. About 160 sat down to an excellent tea; the catering which was in the hands of Atkinson's Cafes Limited, giving the utmost satisfac- tion. The following ladies presided at the tables :—Mrs. Garland, Mrs. Bateman' Mrs. Corney, Mrs. Longland, Mrs. Edelsten, Miss Ellison, Mrs. Porritt, Mrs. Molyneux, Mrs. Yeadon, Mrs' Farrar, Mrs. Eccles, and Mrs. Winskill. The hall was ; most tastefully decorated and presented a very pleasing I scene, a collection of choice plants being arranged on the platform. Before the concert began, the pastor (Rev. John Yonge) delivered a short address in which he emphasised the value of such gatherings as promot- ing social intercourse amongst the members of the congregation. He also commented upon the improved facilities for these functions which the new buildings provided. An exceptionally interest- ing programme was submitted arid all the items were well received, several encores being accorded. The choral sections "When the budding Ibloom of May" (Sullivan) and "Strange Adventure " (Sullivan) were very creditably interpreted by Misses Knowles, Armstrong, A. Griffiths, and E. Hibbert, Messrs. . N. Cook, B. Hibbert, A. Griffiths and W. H. Wall iagton. Songs were given in excellent taste by Miss Avice Beswick, Miss A. Griffiths, Miss Armstrong, Mr. W.V. Barnes, and Mr. %V. H. Wallington, and an instrumental trio• for violin, violincello and piano by Messrs. T. F. Wallington, H. Wallington, and W. H. Payton proved a popular feature. The pianoforte solo La Fontaine" was very well played by Miss Jessie Farrar. Miss Beswick delighted the audiimice with a recitation, and the duets "The white rose" by Miss E. and Mr. B. Hibbert, and "The, spider and the fly" by Miss A. Griffiths ' and Mr. W. J. Barnes were given with fine effect. Mr. T. F. , Wallington was warmly applauded for a cleverly-executed violin solo "Romance." Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac., accompanied with his usual taste and skill. Daring an interval refreshments which had been kindly -provided by one of the deacons, were served. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to all who had contributed to the success cf the evening on the proposition of Mr. J. Motms, seconded by Mr. J. WATSON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <p>" 4 --</p>
        <p>gave much pleasure with their selections. Mr. J. C j Atkinsen and Mr. Dan Stokes introduced their wax- works with due ceremony. The characters were "Bubbles " (Miss Williams), "La Belle France" (Miss Maisy Ambrose), " Grace Darling " (Miss D. Burgess), " Shylock " (J. H. Williams), " Portia (Hiss D. Cawley), "Adelina Patti" (Miss IC. Alderson) Auguste Van Biene (Mr. J. Atkinson)," The better laud" (Misses E. Teece and V. Cutler), "Uncle Tom and Eva" (Hr. Ward and Miss E. Briscoe), "Old Folks at home" (Mr. A. Houghton and Miss M. Watkins) "Pears' Soan "(Mr. F. N. Clare), " M.P. for Birming- ham" (Air. J. Williams). The subjects were excellently staged and attired, assistance behind having been given by Mr. George Newall. The final scene, with coloured fire, excursions and alarums cul lib will not easily be forgotten. In the sketch," The old bachelor," the parts were well sustained by Alessrs. D. Stokes, J. Williams, Adam French, and Master W. J. Scott. The arrange- were supervised by Messrs. A. Lamb and W. E. Goslin (hon. secretaries).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>UNNATURAL ST. HELENS SON. Walter Woodward, a young man, of 56, Thompson- street, as at St. Helens on Monday bound over for six months for riotous conduct and for beating his father on Saturday night. Woodward's father appeared in court with two black eyes, and gave evidence against his son.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS LICENSING COMMITTEE. The St. Helens magistrates held a meeting on Wednesday to arrange for the general annual licensing meeting. It was decided to lazy the maximum rate of charges under the Licensing Act for compensation purposes. This is the same rate as last year, when the charges produced over £3,000. The Licensing Committee was appointed, and will consist of the Mayor (Alderman C. J. Bishop), Colonel W. W. Pilkington, Colonel R. Pilkington, Aldermen J. C. Gamble, J. C. Forstrr, J. Green J. C. Massey, and • Messrs. H. Martin, J. Crooks, J. Collins, and W. J. Cook • ALLEGED SHOPBREAKING AT Sr. HELENS. George Fox, a Bolton man was at St. Helens, on Saturday, committed for trial at the assizes on a charge of breaking into the shop 34, Broad Oak-road, and steal- ing articles valued at 10s. 2d. as well as doing damage amounting to 22s.—Ernest Martin of Derby- shire Hill-road, stated that late on the night of the 10th inst., the prisoner came into Rainford rick and -Tile Works, and ho gave him permission to stay until his clothes were dried. About ten o'clock prisoner went to him and asked him to awaken him at four o'clock in the morning, because he had had a crib to crack.. Martin did not pay attention to the man's statement, but during the night he left the place, and returned in about half an hour with a quantity of tobacco. Later in the day he was arrested in a drunken state in the main street of the town by Police-constable Bate, when the tobacco was found on him. ST. HELENS DR-OWNING MYSTERY. About noon on Sunday a boy of fourteen, named William Gilheeney, of Mill-lane, St. Helens, saw the ! body of a man floating in the Sutton Milldam. He informed Police-Constable Chorlton, who conveyed the body to the mortuary at the Town Hall. The body had been in the water several weeks, and is believed to be that of John Green' who had worked at Ashton's Green , Colliery. A pay ticket and club card booked up to November 23rd were found on the body. I Mr. Brighouse, county Coroner, held the inquest at • St. Helens on Tuesday, on John Green, aged forty-two, of 52 Ormskirk-street. Joseph Isherwood, a collier, ' said he met deceased coming out of the sale yard at the ; Fleece Hotel on the 11th ult.—They went for a walk ; round the town. Witness pawned deceased's jacket. The Coroner: Did you have a drink on the proceeds ? Yes, we had only three glasses of beer apiece. Pre- ; ceeding, witness said they went to Parr, where they ; parted about half past ten. Deceased said he was going ' to his brother's, in Berry's-lane.—Mary Green, wife of • Robert Green, 130 Berry's-lane, sister-in-law, said ; deceased came to her house on this night and stayed there. He seemed a bit upset about his work, but did not say anything about doing away with himself. The I place where he was found was over a mile from their I house.—A verdict of" Found drowned "was returned.</p>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODIST CENTENARY. A Week of Meetings. The centenary of Primitive Methodism has been cele- , btated at Warrington this week by the holding of a series of meetings for addresses and prayer. With one exception, all the gatherings have taken place in Legh- . street Church and have been fairly well attended, the/ speakers dealing with various aspects of the history. of 1 the denomination and its work.—On Monday evening the Rev. C. L. Stowe presided, and Mr. T. 13. Parkes was the speaker. His subject was " Primitive Methodism : Its origin." He gave a I concise sketch of the foundation of the denomination as the result of the labours of Hugh Bourne and William Clowes. He described the first camp meeting h3ld at Mow Cop on May 31st, 1807, pointing out that crowds of visitors from Macclesfield, Congleton, Knuts- ford, Wheelock, and Warrington attended. The success of the meeting gave great encouragement, and in order to avert opposition the ground was licensed for preach- ing, and Bourne himself obtained a minister's licence. At the Conference of the Methodist Society at Liver- pool, however, the subject of camp meetings was intro- duced, and great disfavour was thrown upon the scheme, the meetings being described as" highly improper," and desired preachers to declare against them, as a result of which Clowes was not present at one for 15 months. Hugh Bourne still continued his work, and his brother James, who had wavered for a time on account of the Liverpool decision at last joined him, and they were then expelled from the Methodist Society. James Crawford was engaged as a travelling preacher at 10s. a week, to devote his time wholly to preaching. in March, 1810, a separate society was formed apart from the old Methodist Society. In 1820, the first confer- ence of the Primitive Methodist Church was held, show ing the possession of eight circuits, 48 travelling preachers, 272 local preachers, 7,842 members, with a monthly magazine with Hugh Bourne as editor. Today the statistics were 210,174 members of the church, 1,153 ministers, 16,209 local preachers, 10,997 class rooms, 61,275 Sunday school teachers, 477,144 scholars, 4,413 chapels, 4,239 schools, 492 other rooms, whilst they had 96 chapels in New Zealand and West Africa. On Tuesdag evening Mr. J. Griffiths presided and Mr. W. HIGGINSON spoke on "Our Founders" He referred to the personal characteristics of the founders, showing how each went to the making of Primitive Methodism. Hugh Bourne was the legislator, Clowes was the evange- list, and Crawfoot was the mystic. Wednesday evening's meeting was held at Knutsford- road under the presidency of Mr. Massey, and a highly inte,resting paper on "Our institutions" was given Grr— . .</p>
