      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Conseuvative Whist Drive.</head>
        <p>Conseuvative Whist Drive.</p>
        <p>About 150 ladies and gentlemen were present at a successful and enjoyable whist drive held on Friday night in the Assembly Room at the Conservative Club. No fewer than 32 tables were requisitioned. The proceedings, which commenced at eight o'c!ock, were continued until about two o'clock on the following morning, the result of the competitions for prizes being as follows : —Ladies : 1, Mrs. Sudlow, lady's gold bangle ; 2, Mrs. Hollingworth, silver scent bottle ; 3, Mrs. Thomas Atherton, gold neck pendant. Gentlemen : 1, Councillor Wilson, silver cigarette case ; 2, Mr. John Rigby, silver-mounted pipe, and 3, Mr. James Houghton, toy drum. Substantial refresh- ments were provided.</p>
        <p>„,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>COUNTY COURT ACHONS.</head>
        <p>COUNTY COURT ACHONS.</p>
        <p>Injured Workman's Claim.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before His Elonour,Deputy Judge Strachar,K.C.,Leonard George Bogg, Lower Walton, brought an action under the Employers" Liability Act, against Messrs. Richard Evans and C0.,t0 recover damages in respect to injuries suatained by him when working for the defendants. Mr. Cuthbert Smith, instructed by Mr. T. S. Steel, ' appeared for the plaintiff, defendants being represented .by Mr. Rigby Swift, instructed by Messrs Peace and 'Darlington, Liverpool. Mr. Cuthbert Smith stated that on the sth July last, the plaintiff was employed at the Acton Grange lay-bye I loading coal into a vessel on the Manchester Ship Canal by means of skips. To do this he -had to attach to the skips two chains so that they could be lifted by means of a crane. He had fastened one of the chains and in order to fasten the other one he had to raise skip a I ittle.This was done when the chain which was fastened broke and the skip fell on the plaintiff's foot causing such injuries that two of his toes had to be amputated. Plaintiff alleged that the accident was due to the negligence ef a man named Bodle whose duty it was to see that the chains were safe, or of the defendants themselves. During the whole of the correspondence which had passed between the parties no denial of liability had been made by the defendants. Evidence was called in support of Mr. Smith's 'statement. His Honour said he could not see that the plaintiff had produced any evidence to show that either the man Bodle or the defendants' had been guilty of negligence in not observing the defect which bad resulted in the breaking of the chain. The application under the Employers' Liability Act could not therefore be sus- tained. Opon the application of the plaintiff, his Honour con- sented to amend the claim to one under the Workmen's Compensation Act, and awarded the plaintiff 12s. per week during the period of incapacity. A DISPUTED WARRANTY.</p>
        <p>Robert Connor, farmer, brought an action against Mr, Rowbotham, Agden Bridge, to recover £6 Bs.for alleged breach of warranty in respect of a cow purchased from defendant by the plaintiff.—Mr. E. A. Connor appeared for the plaintiff, defendant being represented by Mr. T. S. Steel. Plaintiff's case was that in October of last year he purchased the cow from Rowhotham for £6.At the time of the transbction he noticed the cow was coughing and asked the defendant if she was straight,defendant reply ing"perfectly straight in every way.'?The cow died a few days later and a post-mortem examination established the fact that death was due to tuberculosis and bronchial pneumonia. Defeadant's case was that no warrantymas given with the cow. After hearing the evidence his Honour gave judgment for the defendant with costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>AN UNDISPUTED CLAINT.</head>
        <p>AN UNDISPUTED CLAINT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel on behalf of Patrick McLoughlin, 7, Robinson's-yard, applied for an award, under the Work- men's Compensation Act, a gainst Mrs. Chappel, Stockton Heath. The plaintiff was engaged as a plasterer in some houses in St. Mary's-street, Latchford. on the 20th November, when a scaffold pole fell and injured his head. Mrs. Chappel admitted liability. His Honour gave judgment for the plaintiff for 14s. a week with posts on scale B.</p>
        <p>The bridegroom at a wedding at Port Stewart Church co. Derry, was a youth of seventy-five, and the bride had reached the mature age of sixteen. A flake of burning soot, cirried by the strong east wind from the chimney of o house at Copford, near Colchester, on Wednesday afternoon lodged in a sparrow's nest under the eaves of an adjacent building, with the result that two houses were burnt to the ground. The original manuscript order for the massacre of Glencoe, signed by Major Robert Dnncanson, Argyle Regiment, and directed to Captain Campbell, of Glen- lyon, will be incln led by Messrs. Puttink and Simpson, London, in an early sale of autograph munuscripts.</p>
      </div>
