      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>MARRIAGE OF A PRESCOT LADY.</head>
        <p>MARRIAGE OF A PRESCOT LADY.</p>
        <p>A marriage was quietly celebrated on Saturday, at Christ Church, Eccleston, the contracting parties being Miss Lucy Lea Leyland, second daughter, of Mr. J Leyland, M.R.C.V.S., of Atherton House, Prescot, and Mr. Fred R. Rose, second son of the late Mr. J. B. Rose of Clifton-terrace, St. Helens. The ceremony was per formed by the Rev. B. S. Clarke. vicar of Eccleston- The bride was attended by her sister, Miss Constanc. Leyland, and Mr. Harold Leyland acted as best mane Mr. H. Foreman, organist, rendered appropriate music.</p>
        <p>The hearing conclnded at Carlisle Assizes on Wednes- day, of the uction brought by Thomas Routledge, iron- worker, of Workington, to recover damages from William James Rogers; of the Duke-street Mission at Workington, for the alleged seduction of the plaintiff's daughter Hannah. The jury found for the plaintiff, and awarded £l4O damages. The death is announced trom New York of Mrs. Louise Beauchamp Hughes, who was intimately acquainted with Oaribaldi, Krupp, and King Humbert of Italy.</p>
        <p>Woman Chancellor</p>
        <p>looking.</p>
        <p>of the</p>
        <p>xthequer</p>
        <p>FELS-NAPTHA 39 Wilson Street London EC</p>
        <p>All housewives are Chancellors. They have not so much money to spend as the Nation's Chancellor and that makes their job more difficult. They must save pennies and half-pennies where they can.</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha soap used the right way—no boiling—will save them some money each week. And, don't forget that clothes when washed "the Fels-Naptha way" are cleaner, whiter and better</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha used the Fels-Naptha way" does it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <p>' )</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY F33TBALL. [By " Ovalist."]</p>
        <p>On Saturday Warrington had one of those matches which was just sufficiently well contested to be exciting, but as to the issue of which there was no doubt locally. In times past Bradford have been powerful enough to beat Warrington• on their own ground, but this season—though they are the Northern Union Cup-holders —much of their glory seems to have departed. They have the remarkable record of having lost every match away from home during the season, and only won seven at Park Avenue. On the contrary Warrington have won all home engnements and only been beaten five times elsewhere. Yet on Saturday there was not a great difference between the teams, and Warrington had to fight hard' to win.</p>
        <p>Lack of.interest in the game,and the black shadow of Association football which hangs over Park Avenue, must have a lot to do with Bradford's poor perform- ances this season. They have a good team yet, and if the men put their hearts into the game they would soon alter, the position of affairs so far as League football is concerned. A finer body of men has not stepped into the Warrington enclosure this season and they had ability too, but it seems misdirected. Had the visitors taken all the ckances their vigorous play provided them with they might have spoiled Warring- ton's home record, but some easy things went begging, whereas the locals snapped up every good thing that came their way and won by 11 points to 5.</p>
        <p>I rather fancy Bradford had some hopes of avenging the defeat sustained against Warrington on December 29th for they started off in very determined fashion and early obtained the lead through a weak kick by Brooks which resulted in a mark and a goal. It took Warrington some time to rub off the arrears and meanwhile Bradford should have got a try as the result of fine dribbling which actually beat the War- rington defence and carried the ball over the line. While the Bradford men were tumbling over one another to drop on the ball Naylor came up and cleared with a lusty kick. Profiting by the escape the home men attyked with determination and some fine combined play was witnessed. The Bradford defence was penetrated close on half-time, Lees throwing to Brooks on the blind side of the scrum and the wing man rushing over. With Preston and Fish both absent the team was hard up for a goal- kicker but Thomas came to the rescue and kicked a beauty from the touch-line.</p>
        <p>The second half was much like the first, with Warrington showing the smarter combination and Bradford making several individual efforts which should have resulted in points but did not. Warring- ton's second try came after a hot attack and after Thomas had had a good effort nullified. 4. Bradford back tried to relirwe but his kick was charged and Harmer dropped on the ball over the line, if the same thing had happened at the other end of the field it would have given Warrington some anxious moments. However, Bradford did get a try eventu- ally, a cross-kick being well taken by the burly Dechan who ran in at the corner, despite Hockenhall's attempt to stop him. From Warrington's drop-out there was a lightning transformation. The fowards followed the ball speedily and Heath raced in from the 25 line with a capital try, and one which neither side expected. Bradford, though they had had as much of the game as Warringtm were thus well-beaten on points.</p>
        <p>In one respect the Warrington team was very weak on Saturday, the threequarters being very much disorganised by the poor form of_ Tilly and Hocken- hall, and if Bradford had not also been let down at centre the _locals might easily have had the worst of the encounter. It has become quite evident that Tilley is no use to the team as a threequarter. His methods have too much of the full-back's deliberation and, moreover, he is a poor runner. Brooks should have had three or four tries in the last two matches if his centre had only been quicker. Hockenhall was on the wing, but he shaped feebly and was several times tackled with the ball whan he should have been running away with it. The saviour of the side was Isherwood, whose cleverness and rp/iability had much to do with Warrington's victory, It will be a cause for rejoicing when some of the injured men ara fit again. In any case the commlitee should drop experimenting with Tilley as a threequarter. Either Cartwright or Jordan would make much better use of the opportunities.</p>
        <p>-The Warrington halves, Lees and Jenkins, were brilliart behind a pack of forwards who heeled out much more smartly than usual. Jenkins keeps on improving and bids fair to become a really first- class half. Considering his size he is a glutton for work, and never appears to tire. Berry and Gunn both gave a fine display at full back, with the Brad- ford man perhaps the better of the two. He kicked with more power than Berry, who does not give his leg enough swing. The Bradford forwards, though beaten for possession, dribbled with speed and skill and nothing but sheer bad luck prevented them scoring more than once. Dechan was always dangerous, but the play of the visiting centres pre- vented him having much opportunity. With his speed and 131 stones he fairly scattered the Warrington men once or twice.</p>
        <p>Fish is improving so rapidly that he wants to turn out at Tidnes next Satuyday. It would be taking ii. grave sks to let him play. Two months is only just about t e minimum for a broken arm, and Fish would be bett . advised to wait a little longer and do some running in the meantime. What a disaster it would be were he laid up again ! And there will be no kid-glove tactics at Widnes,though Warrington should have a comfortable win.</p>
        <p>The League Table up-to-date is as follows : For Agst. Per-</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P.centage. Halifax 23 20 2 1 89 80 420 26 36 160 89.13 Leeds 2016 4 0 59 71 340 24 37 159 -.80110 Warrington 22 16 5 1 56 91 394 31 39 185 7500 Runcorn 2O 15 5 0 57 93 393 27 31 147 7500 Oldham 94 17 6 1 55 72 326 35 39 187 72•91 Hunslet 23 16 7 0 65 82 376 48 57 267 69.56 Wigan 93 15 7 1 69105 453 36 46 210 6739 Broughton 21 13 7 1' 56 80 352 29 32 151 6412 Keighley —lB 11 6 1 45 61 273 31 40 182 6389 Barrow 00 10 9 1 38 65 271 38 55 241 5250 Salford 23 12 11 0 46 59 269 55 52 266 5217 Dewsbury 22 10 11 1 45 75 315 49 68 302 4772 Batley lB 8 9 1 39 38 192 35 48 214 47.22 Widnes l4 6 7 1 25 26 128 32 54 226 4642 Leigh 91 911 1 32 64 256 38 50 226 4523 Hull K.R.91 912 0 31 48 206 37 45 209 42.85 Rochdale 00 811 1 26 53 211 31 56 230 4250 Swinton —.21 10 14 0 45 62 276 50 61 283 4P66 Hull 91 814 0 35 51 223 60 56 378 3636 Bradford 2l 713 1 49 66 296 44 58 262 35.71 Hud'sfield 2O 713 0 32 60 244 64 71 350 3500 Walefild T lB 512 1 20 42 178 39 74 300 3055 York l7 512 0 25 40 172 41 62 268 2941 St. Helens...l9 514 0 37 47 215 40 65 275 2631 Bramley 17 115 1 716 62 44 83 337 8•82 Liverpl.C 21 020 1 10 17 71 102 199 801 2'38</p>
        <p>Warrington still hold the third position, but Runcorn have reached the same percentage with two matches in hand. There is evidently g-,ing to be a very keen fight for final honours, and the positions of the first four clubs are interesting. Leeds have the worst chance of maintaining their place, for they have eight matches all away from home and some of them very stiff engagements. Only Broughton Rangers and Hunslet have yet to visit the Leeds ground. Halifax have five at home and six away.- Warrington have six at home and six away, and Runcorn have six at home and only four away. The Linnets are obviously Warrington's most serious rivals, and yell much will depend on the return match at Runcorn. Oldham are not out of the running either, but they have six matches away to four at home. The Pontefract Club having disbanded are struck from the League table, but as they did not meet any of the top clubs the percentages are not seriously affected.</p>
        <p>Warrington to-day enter upon the first of four successive away matches, viz., Oldham, Widnes, Huddersfield and Halifax. As a rule they do not visit Watersheddings with much confiderce. It has always been an unlucky ground for thorn, but there is so much at stake to-day that their supporters are hopeful of at least one point from the encounter. If they so far slimed I shall be very pleased. Oldham are playing in such style at present that there does not seem much hope for visiting teams. The result largely depends on whether Warrington ein reproduce their best form and get a reasonable share of the luck.</p>
        <p>Tbe selected team will not give satisfaction locally,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>NEW THEOLOGY.</head>
        <p>THE NEW THEOLOGY.</p>
        <p>THE REV J. F. MATTHEWS' VIEWS.</p>
        <p>There was a crowded attendance at Toft's Assembly Room on Thursday evening, when the Rev. J. F. Matthews resumed his series of weekly addresses to men. Thursday's subject was "The new theology." For the last thirty years, said :qr. MATTHEWS, what had been called the theory of evolution had dominated the whole field of scientific inquiry. Side by side with that there had been a steady growth of scientific criticism in its relation to literature and not least in relation to the literatire which we called the Bible. They were, therefore, not surprised to waken up one morning and find that 30 yearti permeation of the theory of evolutinn on thle one hand and of scientific criticism on the other had changed the outlook of men of to-day as compared with that of twenty-five or thirty years ago. The new theology so called, was alleged to he a kind of combivation of Unitarianism on the one hand and of pantheism on the other. He wished that in attempting to criticise anything the average man would not suck in but would use his brains alike upon the critic and upon the criticised, and he ventured to remark , that if that were done it would not require half an eye ' to see that the re-statement of modern belief was very far from being pantheistic at all. Pantheism was an oriental importation and meant that God and the universe were interchangeable terms, but one of the fundamental principles of that so-called new theology was its fixed belief in the transcendence of the Deity', While it believed in the immanence of the Divine,it believed that God did not stand outside His universe but that Ho was inherent in its very life— part and parcel of it. It believed that God was supreme and that the universe was but one expression of the Infinite Mind. No man who believed in the transcendence of God as well as in His imman- ence could be charged with pantheism.He was not there to state what his position was in regard to the Virgin birth but he frankly and freely acknowledged that in any discussion of the Divinity of Christ the doctrine of the Virgin birth was abolntely irrelavent. If they were going to make the Divinity of Christ rest upon a Virgin birth they were going to make it rest upon a very shallow and a very pregnable basis and if he could not believe in Deity on a firmer and finer basis then for him the Deity of Christ would have to go. And He very much objected to a man being told that he was no Christian because he could not accept the doctrine of the Virgin birth. A most respected clergyman in Warrington went so far as ask " How could any man whether parson or layman call himself a Christian and dissociate himself from the great truth that Jesus Christ was born of the blessed virgin." He did not think that should be said as a great many men who could not believe in the virgin birth were as profoundly Christian as the Rev. William Bracecamp himself. (Applause). At the same time it was not impossible to conceive and realize that if in the fulfillment of the Divine purpose it became abso- lutely necessary that God should come closer and closer to man it was not impossible for Him to gather up something of Himself, and concentrate it in some human form and present it .in the history of man as a living ideal of what man him- self ought to be. They had been told that sin was more the fault and error of men who were slowly sloughing through an animal condition and reaching towards the spiritual and intellectual, and that there was therefore no need for the atonement. What did men mean, when they brrought the charge against the new theology that, it did away with the atonement? Was atonement to be identified with one or two or more theories? Atonement surely meantrecip- ciliation. If the theory of evolution and the scientific criticism had shaken some of the old forms until they thought that those forms created more difficulties than they removed the sooner those old forms were done away with and they spoke the language which they could understand, so much the better for their intellects, their beliefs and the Christain faith. The Infinite God was expressing himself in the finest and the holiest form in Jesus Christ and by the presenta- tion in human form the Infinite Love was striving to woo and win men that they might leave the old ways and rise on stepping stones of their dead selves to higher things. His own feeling was ta great deal which had been said about the new theology was premature and not until they had finally passed through the present state of transcision and until the sciences were far inure matured than they were, would they be able to construct a philosopy which would be as thorough-going and as searching as would satisfy the capacity of the human intelleat and the yearnings of the human heart.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Rev. W. Braceeamp's Views.</head>
        <p>The Rev. W. Braceeamp's Views.</p>
        <p>Preaching in St. Paul's Church, on Sunday evening, the Rev. W. Bracecamp, vicar, alluded to the new theolgy introduced by the Rev. R. J. Campbell, of the City Temple. He said that when they examined it they found there was nothing in it that was new. Any boy or girl who had begun to learn Church history, or any history, would find that the thoughts Mr. Campbell had put before the public were thoughts which were expressed years and years ago, and were treated as heresies. How could any man, whether parsson or lay- man, call himself a Christian and dissociate himself from the great truth that Jesus was born of the Blessed Virgin. He grew up to be a man, was treated as a man and died for the human race. These were thoughts that had comforted their forefathers and had made them happy and in the past cheerful. It was a faith also that had been a comfort and a support to the Church in past days, a faith that would comfort it now. Yet a great leader of men, a man-of education, in charge of the most important Congregational Church in London, did not believe in the atonement of Christ ! What would be the result? Some men would say "Now I shall be able to do as I like. lam not going to bother about religion." The New Theology, he felt convinced, would not trouble them in that congregation, and they would still believe in the grand old truth "The nearer to Cross the nearer to Christ."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>'NNW- – 2, .</head>
        <p>'NNW- - 2, .</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 26, 1907.</p>
        <p>and it is a puzzle to me where the justification for selecting Hockenhall on the wing comes in. He played a shocking game last S.,turday, and was never suited to the position. His selection seems to me about on a par with the playing of Lloyd a year ago. Jordan or Cartwright would have been infinitely better,- and it appears to me that the committee is merely inviting disaster at Oldham. The return of Dickenson is very gratifying • and if he can reproduce the brilliance of his play a few weeks ago there will be good hope of success. Boardman is injured and Preston fills the vacancy. Tilley is on neither team. Does he cost to much for the "A " team, I wonder ? The crowd would like to have seen him at full back at Wilderspool today. The selected teams are as follow :</p>
        <p>Ist team : Berry, fall back; Hockenhall, Isher- wood, Dickenson, and Brooks, threequarters ; Lees and Jenkins, half backs ; Preston, Thimas, Heath, Harmer, Naylor, and Shugars, forwards. " " team : Knight, fall bAck ; Jordan, Cart- wright, Jones, and Newall, threequarters ; Davies and Kay, half-backs ; Belton, Swift, Jolley, Forster, Holbrook, and Hewitt, forwards.</p>
        <p>The South-west Lancashire League now stands as follows : Pts.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. For Agst. Pts. W'gton.. 8 ... G 1 ... 1 ... 121 65 ... 13 Wigan... 7 ... 5 ... 2 ... 0 ... 150 ... 62 ... 10 Runcorn 6 ... 4 2 ... 0 ... 82 52 8 Widnes.. 8 ... 2 ... 0 ... 63 ... 194 ... 4 S.Helens 7 ... 1 ... 6 ... 0 ... 48 ... 103 ... 2</p>
        <p>The conference of Yorkshire clubs convened by the Leeds Club for the purpose of discussing the Northern Union rules, was held at the Green Dragon Hotel, Leeds, on Weduesday night, Colonel Foster, of Halifax, presiding. All thei Yorkshire clubs in the League and several of the district unions were repre- sented.</p>
        <p>There was a long discussion, on the question being put to the vote the meeting voted as f,Alows : One for and fifteen against the knock-on rule. One for and nine against the present mode of selecting referees. Three for and nine against the present formation of the Northern Rugby League. Ten for and five against the play the ball rul s. Thirteen for and two against 13-aside game. Four for and seven against dates of county matches as at present fixed. Eight for and three against the present system of dealing with reported players. Twelve for and two against the annual registra- tion of players. Six for and ten against a reversion to the punt- oat from touch. One for and thirteen against a reversion to Rugby Union rules. Six for and five against a wages limit for players. Nine for and six against an independent sub-com- mittee to deal with players' transfers and fess and appeals by either club or player interested.</p>
        <p>Judging by the voting the dissatisfaction in York- shire with the existing rules is not so strong as has been reported. Bradford are evidently the only club with any idea of going back to the Rugby 'Union. The knock-on rule and the formation of the League are the things most objected to, and I certainly think both might be improved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>– . , • hoitr..•,, – : :</head>
        <p>- . , • hoitr..•,, - : :</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NIECES AND NEPHEWS,— A few weeks ago I said I would give A, nice present to the member sending in the best description of how they spent Christmas-day. Well, I have got the prize, but I am no " for- rarder," as the •man says, in presenting it, be- cause, sad to rte, I have received such a small number of contributions. I should like at least 25 members to send me in accounts of what they did upon that eventful day. I don't mind how short the letters are; in fact, the shorter the better, as that will enable me to get a larger selection into the column, and give everyone a chance. If you have not yet sent in yours, then do so within the next fortnight, as I would like to settle this prize business. I have received a large number of contributions this week, but, as you will notice in_the Replies to Contributors, several stories are much too long for the small amount of space at my dis- posal. These long stories •have, in the past, compelled me to lay aside lots of things which I should like to have printed, and, not having done so, it may make my little friends (who send me jokes and do not see them in the column), think I am an awfully nasty old Uncle. So, in the future, please, dear nieces and nephews, will you refrain from sending me anything which will take up more than twenty lines?</p>
        <p>To change the subject, I must thank all those who in the past have sent me in parcels of clothing, and those who have just written to say that they are going to. Next week I am starting a new competition after this style. I am going to give a prize to the member who sends me in the prettiest picture postcard, con- taining one verse of poetry on some comic sub- ject. The prize I shall give will take the place of the usual weekly one offered for the best story, jokes, etc. I shall also be glad to receive any other tit-bit you boys and girls can send to • UNCLIC NED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Quite well</head>
        <p>Quite well</p>
        <p>ISS JENNY KNOW. 10, Gordon Terrace, Hoo, near Rochester, Kent.</p>
        <p>I am writing to tell you how much good Iron-Ox Tablets have done me. I was nervous, depressed, and upset by trifles. I‘ly appetite had left me. Sometimes when going upstairs I would have to stop because my heart was palpitating so much that I felt as if I would choke. The colour had disappeared from my cheeks and lips and I had dark rings round my eyes. I did not sleep well and I used to wake v..p in the morning feel- ing miserable and exhausted. Having read of a case similar to mine which had been cured by Iron-Ox Tablets I bought a box and after taking them for a little while I obtained great relief.l have taken four boxes in all and feel better in every way, in fact lam quite well now. I may also mention that three of my brothers are taking Iron-Ox Tablets and are deriving great benefit from them. I cannot praise Iron-Ox Tablets too much." Signed (Miss) JENNY KNOW.</p>
        <p>The severe indigestion which was the real cause of all Miss Know's ailments has been completely removed by Iron-Ox Tablets. They strengthened Miss Know's digestive organs, stimulated the flow of the gastric juices and quickly banished the trouble. As a conse- quence Miss Know is free from nervousness and depres- sion, the palpitation has left her, she sleeps well now, her appetite is good, and her colour has returned. Iron-Ox Tablets can do as much for you.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>MR. SHACKLETON, M.P. AT PENKETH.</head>
        <p>MR. SHACKLETON, M.P. AT PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Temperance Questions.</p>
        <p>A largely attended meeting was held under the auspices of the United Kingdom Alliance in the Wesleyan Chapel, Penketh, on Monday last. The chair was occupied by Mr. Charles E. Parker and the principal speaker was Mr. D. J. Shackleton,M.P., Labour Member for Clitheroe. Mr. Shackleton gave facts and figures, punctuated his speech by homely phrase and ready wit, and retained the close attention of the audience to the end. He naturally approached the question from the workingman's point of view. He told of instances in the past where Insurance Companies declined to insure the life of a teetotaler unless he paid an extra premium of 10 per cent,of how the position had been reversed and insurance companies were glad to accept teetotalers on a much reduced premium as compared with the moderate drinkers, and gave figures showing what had justified this course. He referred in a convincing manner to the scientific and medical testimony relating to the injurious effect of alcohol as a beverage and as a medicine. Special reference was made to the desirability of dissociating the meetings of clubs and trades unions from the public-house and he appealed to Churches and Municipalities to provide suitable accommodation at reasonable rates for these gatherings. The custom of allowing the use of public. houses free and relying for payment upon the honour of the members drinking for the good of the house was characterised as altogether evil. The following resolution was proposed by Mr. Shackleton, seconded by Mr. Boreland of the United Kingdom Alliance, supported by Mrs. Garnett, and unanimously passed :—" Tlyzt this meeting, while thanking the Prime Minister for his promise to intro- duce in the next session of Parliament an efficient and i comprehensive measure of temperance reform, earnestly hopes that the Government Bill will include :- 1. A short time limit to the Compensation Clauses of the Licensing Act of 1904. 2. A restoration and extension of the discre- tionary powers of the local licensing authority making every kind of licence subject to magisterial control. 3. A free and unfettered direct veto of power to be conferred on the people in local areas to enable , them to prevent, if they desire, the issue and I renewal of all licences for the sale of intoxicating</p>
        <p>liquors within their district. A voto of thanks was accorded the speakers, on the motion of Mr. James Seed, seconded by Mr. W. Walters.</p>
      </div>
