      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>WELCOME TO</head>
        <p>WELCOME TO</p>
        <p>E. Astle, Fred Howorth.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,---4We have removed from Craw:shawbooth to Scotland, but we still take in the local paper. I thought it would please other children if I wrote and told you how we spent Christmas over here. They do not have parties, and the people go to work on Christmas-day; but they have proper •holidays at New Year. "Santa Claus" comes over here, and the shops are beautiful in Glasgow at Christmas, with toys. I must now conclude, from,</p>
        <p>JANIE COMPSTON.</p>
        <p>I am sending you a piece of poetry which is called</p>
        <p>THE WISHES OF FOUR ,I.ITTLE GIRLS</p>
        <p>Four little happy, smiling girls, I chanced one day to see; They talked of-the coming year and told What they would like to be. Fair Lucy, with the curly hair, Would like to teach a school, And learn the children how to read, And mind their teacher's rule. Miss Ellen, only eight years old, Put on a woman's look, And said that when a woman grown, She'd like to write a book. And then the others had to laugh Till tears were in their eyes, To think about her writing books, And see her look so wise. Matilda, with a stately !lead, Would be a rich man's wife, And dress in silks and satins fine, And lead a merry life. She'd ride in costly carriages To party, church, and ball, And servants sliould her bidding do, And come at every call. The last to speak was .Mary, Who had listened to the rest, And wondered what she'd better do,. And what would gait her best. But she at 1- st. confluded that, As mam.na trA no other, She'd better alwa33 stay at home, And be a help ta mother.</p>
        <p>-4-- MAKING SAKES, I really love to be about On mother's bakim day, And coax for just a piece of dough, At making cakes to play. Some currants and some candied peel, To make my cake so nice; And just to see that they are right, _ . _</p>
        <p>I taste them c;nce or twice. I cut the peel in tiny bit; I stick the currants in • And then I bang the matins hard With Mother's rolling pin. But somehow it surprises me, When both the cakes are done; My mother's is as light as air, And mine a heavy one! ANNIE MATTILEWMAN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <p>.</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>REPLIES TO CONTRIBUTORS. .GGIE.—It is very kind of you to give me the offer of a serial story, but I am not in a position to accept it, on account of the small space at my disposal. Unfortunately, trough the same cause, I am unable to</p>
        <p>publiih more than a quarter of the weekly</p>
        <p>contributions. DORIS B.—l am sorry about the matter which you write about, and expect it has either got lost or mislaid in the post. Anyway, I am despatching another to you,to-day. LUCY T.—See reply to "Doris B." DOROTHY S.—The story you send in is interest- ing,"but I regret to say, Dorothy, it is too long. Perhaps you can find a shorter one, and let me have it. I only wish I had double the space at my disposal—but newspaper</p>
        <p>apace costs money, my dear. _ _ EDWARD G.—Yes, I expect you were themember I referred to. I shall look forward to your letters, Edward. DOROTHY M.—See reply to' " Dorothy S." FREDA W.—Unfortunately, I have not room enough for all your contributions, but I have found space for the charades. MY LITTLE DAUGHTERS.</p>
        <p>I have a little daughter, she is as good as gold; So loving to her mother; always does as she is told; I love to hear kt.r lzughter as she runs about the hous•Dj But wh(p m 1.0.er has a headache, she is quiet</p>
        <p>t Mouse. ittive a little daughter, as cross as she can be; ti-ech a worry to her mother, that anyone can</p>
        <p>see; So tiresome and.noisy as she runs in and out; A very naughty little girl) of that there is no</p>
        <p>doubt. - My best of little daughters, I hope you'll with</p>
        <p>me stay, For, when you do, the other one is sure to " keep away; She's really such a torment, I do not want her</p>
        <p>near; I'd rather be without her, so please stay with me, dear. About these little daughters—they both have but one name; The bad one and the good one are really just</p>
        <p>the same; _ _ I've only got one daughter,, as I dare say you have guessed, But now you know for certain, because I have confessed.</p>
        <p>DORIS MATTREWMAII/4T. GEOGRAPHICAL CHARADE.</p>
        <p>My first is easily blown, my second protects the King, and my whole is a town in Lincoln- shire.</p>
        <p>My second is the property of my first, and my whole is one of our Colonies.</p>
        <p>___4__</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l was very pleased to sec my story in print. Could you spare room in your " Corner " for a long story, and I will send two chapters every week, as I think my cousins will like it? Here is a short story, entitled— THE OWL AND HIS SCHOOL. • An Indian Fable.</p>
        <p>An owl named Old Wisdom once kept a school. Every beast used to go to him to take lessons. After some time, he wished to know what pro- gress they had made in their studies, io he gave them a number of questions to answer. The first was, "Why dOeS the moon shine in the sky?"</p>
        <p>The nightingale said, "That I may sing all night in his pleasant light to my bride, .the rose,"</p>
        <p>The hare said, "That there may be enough dew in the morning for me to 'lap." The dog said, "That I may find out thieves prowling round my master's house." The glow-worm said, "That he may throw me into the shade, for he envies my light.'• The fox said, "That I may see my way to the poultry yard." "Enough," said Old Wisdom, " ea.Ch of you is selfish, and thinks only of himself. There is but one moon, that shines in the sky for-the good of us all." Moral.—Remember that the good things of this earth are not meant only for yourself. Here is a puzzle:— A man in a high position mentioned in the Second Book of Kings, who was cured of a dreadful disease by means which he at first despised. My first is in mountain, but not in hill; My second's in streamlet, but not in rill; My third is in nature, and also in art; My fourth is in mercy, but not in hea'rt; My fifth is in eagle, but not in dove ;. My sixth's in affection, but not in love. I will send the answer next week.</p>
        <p>I remain, your affectionate Niece,</p>
        <p>MAGGIE EASTWOOD.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l have sent a short piece of poetry, which you will find on the other side, and I would be very pleased to see it in the "Children's Corner." Dear Uncle Ned, I hope pill have enjoyed a good Christmas, and likewise I hope you will have a prosperous New Year. From your loving Niece, BEATRICE MANTLE. "WHEN EVENING BRINGS US HOME." When twilight shadows softly fall, Across the fading light, And vesper bells in music call, The heralds of the night,- 0, hour that breathes of peace and rest, To those who sadly roam, Hour that is dearest, sweetest, best, When evening brings us home.</p>
      </div>
