        <p>matter havinr, come before the Council as a whole? I with contempt. It is making your Board like I am— mean if an individual member purchases-- a wooden Board. (Laughter.) The TOWN CLERK : There has never been an instance The Inspector : 1 quite agree with you. where an individual member has purchased. The Town Conncillor PEMBEitTON contended that there had been ' Council in their wisdom have recently passed a new a waste of money as a2O feet passage was sufficient. It standing order which requires all property to be bought seemed to have seen simply for the benefit of one man hereafter to be publicly discutsed before the purchase is and whatever influence he might have had in the Council made. I submit with all respect that the question appNtred to have been very well used for him. raised by Councillor Wilding is entirely beside the •ra..o INSPECTOR 'rho : You don't want to penalise the question for consideration this morning and does not ratepayers by paying the money out of the rates? form part of the subject matter of the application. The Councillor PENIBERTON : I don't. My sympathy is question before us is whether or not the money we with the ratepayers, but it seems to me that something have expended in making the street shall be paid for ought to be done by the Board to express dis- out of the rates or borrowed under the powers the approval. Local Government Board propose to give us. The • rhe INSPECTOR said it was only a rule of the Board , question of carryinc, the street through to Sankey- that a Corporation should not commit themselves to street will, I take it: now be abandoned by the Board expenditure before sanction had been obtained. If the after the strong opposition you have heard. The ques- Corporation chose to defy that rule and deliberately tion is whether the cost incurred in making and paving commit themselves to capital expenditure, the Board the street should be paid for out of the rates or felt free to refuse the loan and leave the Corporation to borrowed. get out of the dilficulty as best they could. Mr. PENDLEBURY thought the inquiry might be Councillor PEMBERTON : If that is only a rule, it shortened if the inspector accepted the Town Clerk's ought to be made law at once. suggestion and withdrew the third clause. Apart from . that he objected to anyone having his street paved by I OTHER PURCHASES WITHOUT SANCTION. the Corporation when he (Mr. Pendlebury) had to pay I Mr. GOUGH asked whether £13,000 worth of property for his own. had been purchased during the last three or four years _ _ . __ .. . . .. . . __ _ ~</p>
        <p>The INSPECTOR: As regards paragraph 3, I see there , and the douncil had never applied to the Board for is a great deal of opposition. You do not consider the sanction. traffic justifies you carrying the street through and i The TOWN CLERK : That is incorrect. The matter would not for a number of years, and it would be an is before the Local Government Board and has been expensive thing to do. You also feel that you would , for some time. rather the Council had not power to carry the street Mr. GOUGH : Will you tell us how much has been through, although it would not imply that the power borrowed? must be used, There is nothing more to be said on The TOWN CLERK : That is not-the subject of the that question. inqni ry and I refuse to answer such questions. Mr. PENDLEBURY : If you accept the withdrawal, Mr. GoUGff : These are traps which have been made there is an end of it, for us to fall into, and we have had no power to recover The INSPECTOR : I have no power. The Board put ourAelves. that in. T, e Tows CLERK : I don't say the Corporation did PART OF THE SCHEME DROPPED. not purchase properties to that amount. I simply said Councillor PEMBERTON said there was no doubt the the matter was before the Local Government Board. I public of Warrington and the majority of the Council denied Mr. Gough's assertion that it was not before the were against the scheme until they were compelled to Board and that no application had been made. go into it. The Town Clerk wrote stating that if the The INSPECTOR : The question rather was : have the Council did not obtain the order and did not acquire Council during the last two or throe years purchased the premises belonging to the executors of the late properties of the value of £15,000 without first getting Thomas Hewitt, they would leave themselves liable to either Parliamentary or Local Government Board an action for specific performance at the hands of Mr. sanction to borrow the money. R. E. Garnett. He (Councillor Pemberton) voted at The Tows CLERK : Oh yes I believe that is so. the Council meeting, and others did so, in the honest Mr. GOUGH then stated his objections at length and belief that they were under an agreement with Mr. contended that the object of the scheme was to benefit Garnett and could not get out of it. If he had known the adjoining owner, Mr. R. E. Garnett. The fact that as much then as he did now, not even the part bf the the Corporation had purchased £45,000 worth of property street would have been made. He felt strongly that during the last two or three years without sanction clearly if they had had an inquiry at the proper time, the proved that they meant to ignore the law, ride rough. scheme would have been knocked on the head. The shod over the will of the ratepayers, and take no notice Board should insist upon an inquiry before the money of orders from the Local Government Board. was spent. The INSPECTOR : Do you prefer that the money The INSPECTOR : We endeavour to do so. should come out of the rates? Councillor PEMBERTON : But this has been done Mr. GOUGH : Yes. I think Mr. Garnett ought to three years. It is the want of information that I corn- pay for it. (Laughter.) plain of. When a committee is appointed to buy The INSPECTOR: The only alternative to borrowing property, the negotiations should sometimes be kept the money is to pay it out of the rates. private, but they should obtain the option of purchase. THE QUEsnoN OF STREET MAKING. The INSPECTOR: That is the proper way. Councillor PEMBER,TON: But they don't do that. Mr. H. THORNTON said he strongly objected to Mr. They buy the property. Garnett being freed from the making of the street. He The INSPECTOR : It does sometimes happen that you (Mr. Thornton) had to pay for his streets and other can't get the option. people had to pay for their's, so ho thought it was only Councillor PF,MBERTON : I prefer to run the risk, fair that Mr. Garnett should do the same. If the street There should be no jobbery whatever, was continued, he thought Mr.Glarnett ought to pay for The INSPaCTOR : I think you can take it that pro- it, in all fairness to the ratepayers. bably the Order will not give power to carry the street The INSPECTOR: Is Mr. Garnett a member of the through to Sankey-street. Council ? Councillor ‘VILKINSON said he thought that for the Mr. THORNTON : No. last half hour they had been flogging a dead horse. The Mr. F. PENDLEBURY said he objected on the same Corporation had never sought powers to go through to ground. A private street ought to be paid for by the Sankey-street. The Local Government Board in its adjoining owners and he did not see why one owner wisdom or otherwise.— should be treated differently from other ratepayers. _ _ . . . . .</p>
        <p>The INSPECTOR: You will not add anything by talking in this way. Councillor WILKINSON: The Board inserted the paragraph that we could apply for tiowers to carry the street through to Sankey-street. We have never sought powers. The INSPECTOR : That has already been stated. We are fully aware of that. Councillor WILKINsON : The Town Clerk has offered to withdraw clause three and I consider that the whole of the arguments since then have been a waste of time. The INSPECTOrt : Of course, I am the judge of how to conduct this inquiry. Mr. S. KENYO.4. said that if they could be assured that the Corporation would not be granted power to carry the street through, the objectors would go away satisfied. (Cries of No.") He felt certain the majority of ratepayers would consider it wrong for their money to be spent in carrying out such a scheme and he trusted the Board would not allow the Corpora- sion to do that.</p>
        <p>Had it not been for the casting vote of the MayOr; the 20 feet passage would have remained. Mr. TitortxToN sai='. he would like to have shown the Inspector from the minutes what Mr. Garnett was making from the improvement, and what he would have made if it had baen carried through to Sankey-street. He thought the Inspoctor would have seen from the minutes how the arrangement had been made. THE TOWN CLERK'S EXPLANATION. The ToWN CLERK said he wanted to remove one or two false and misleading statements which had been made. Councillor Pemberton called attention to a resolution of the Council passed on October 6th, 1903, and emphasised the fact that it was carried by the cast- ing vote of the Mayor. He, however, failed to call attention to a similar resolution brought forward in the SAMe month by his friend Councillor Lord which was defeated by a majority of five. That proposition was to the effect that the previous resolution be rescinded And the vote showed thht the Council was in favour of</p>
        <p>The INSPECTOR: I think we will proceed on the assumption that that paragraph will be dropped. STRONG OPPOSITION. Mr. BARRTTT said that in the first place his clients objected because, the Corporation had exceeded their powers. There was no doubt that the new street had not been the idea of the Corporation but of the adjoin- ing owner. The only person to benefit in this particular way would be Mr. R. E. Garnett. The INSPECTOR: It is one of those cases where the thing has been done. Mr. Baatarrr said he thought the Corporation should be made to find the money in some other way without getting the loan. The street was serving no useful purpose and was not worth £O5O. It had been the making of a private improvement at the public expense, and it was giving to individuals what was wrong both in principle or anything else—that they should obtain a monopoly at the expense of the rate- payers. That was what the owner would obtain. There was no necessity for the new street. When the Bridge- street improvement was carried out all the traffic would be well provided for. The proximity to Bridge-street was altogether too close,and to make a street in that posi- tion would be in serious opposition to the shopkeepers in Bridge-street. If Benson's shop was pulled down the safety of Trinity Church would be imperilled, awl the taking away of the support of the tower would probably mean that the Church would have to be pulled down. Property built facing the back entrances of the Bridge- street shops would be of little value. His clients objected to the Corporation having general powers to proceed with various improvements. The present scheme seemed to have gone on without any definite plan and the Corporation apparently had no idea where it was going to end. The street was not wanted. It had been forced upon the ratepayers and would have to be paid for, but his clients thought the Board should teach the Corporation a lesson that before they entered upon a scheme it should be fully con- sidered and mapped out. Mr. Garnett certainly gave 20 f3et of land but he 'sold the Corporation another 20 feet for which he got £3 a yard. The whole thing was exploiting a private improvement at the expense of the public and it should not be allowed. The IsspEcTort : Do you think it has cost the Cor- poration more than if they had only made it 20 feet ? Mr. Mama. : There is no doubt about it. The original idea was that it should be a back passage for the new buildings in Bridge-street. There was, he con- tinued, no doubt that a through street would be required later on, but it should be made a greater distance from Bridge-street. He hoped the inspector would put his clients' views strongly before the Board , and that the Corporation would learn a lesson for the future and, before rushing into bargains, take the Council into confidence and study the interests of th 3 ratepayers before committing them to any cost. (Applause.) The Towx CLERK said he took exception to the last observation. COUNCILLOR PEAIBERTON'S POSITION. "LIKE A WOODEN MAN." Councillor PEMBERTON said he objected to the pro- posal on the ground thit to carry the street tlwough would cost the town at least £12,000. The INSPECTOR: But we are not considering that. I think you can take it that the power will not be gr mted. Councillor PENIBERTON Objected to the other part of the application on the ground that the true facts of the case had not been properly placed the Board. The resolution authorising the purchase was only supported by eleven members (a third of the Council) and catried by the Mayor's casting vote. Even those eleven were under the impression that they were legally bound to carry it out. It was only when an inquiry was held that it was found that the agreement was only a provisional one. The street would never have been made if they had known that. The INSPECTOR : You would rather this £l,OOO was found out of the rates as a way of punishing the Corporation. Councillor PEMBERTON : It is like beating the horse to punish the man who rides it. (Laughter.) Continu- ing, he said the Local Government Board gave the ratepayers the opportunity of objecting to the loan, but if the purchase went on before an inquiry was held the Board said : "If you object you must pay for it." That was unfair. The INSPECTOR: A lot of what you object to is in the hands of the people of 'Warrington. If they choose to send representatives to the Council they must be largely responsible for what the Council does. The Council has now passed a resolution that all purchases shall come before the Council so you have got what you want. Councillor PEMBERTON said be contended that he as a Councillor ought to have known the conditions under which the purchases were made. He understood that no bargain could be made by a committee without the confirmation of the Council and that purchases must have the seal of the Corporation. Those conditions had been ignored; the law was of no use in Warrington. There was a very strong feeling on the matter in the town. He himself felt at times that he was simply like a wooden man in the Council. The INSPECTOR said many Corporations negotiated in regard to purchases of land privately, with a view to saving the ratepayers' money. He was sure in the great majority of cases that was the only motive and it fre- quently happened that large sums were saved. At the same time he felt that Councillor Pemberton had a grievance when a purchase which be objected to came before the Council and he was told that the Corporation's hands were tied because they had practically agreed to buy it and as honourable men they were bonsai to carry it through. As Councillor Pemberton said, he was like a wooden dummy in the Council. Councillor PEMBERTON : It is treating yourßoard</p>
        <p>the scheme. Councillor Pemberton referred to the agreement made with Mr. Garnett. That agreement was not arrived at until the matter had been discussed five or six timea in the Council, nor was it made until the final resolution of the Council approving the scheme. With regard to the price paid to Mr. Garnett for the 1 land, Councillor Pemberton said he received £3 per yard. That was incorrect. When the Corporation 1 obtained powers to acquire from Mr. Garnett land and buildings, they called in three eminent valuers to advise the Corporation as to the price to be offered to him. The valuations were £779, £6BO, and £679 respectively to be paid for certain land. The Corporation acquired from Mr. Garnett all that land as well as an additional plot of 20 feet for the net sum of £651. At the same time they gave Mr. Garnett a piece of land comprising 531 square yards. In answer to the inspector, the TOWN CLERK said it was not considered necessary to submit the final arrange- ment to the valuers. Continuing he said that all the transactions with Mr. Garnett were fully and openly discussed in the Council and were reported in the news- loners, and nothing had been done without the full sanction of the Council. The matter was before the Council six or seven times and was fully discussed, all the terms belly,b set out. Mr. Barritt said the land was not sufficient to give a frontage to the new street. As ' a matter of fact there was a shop belonging to Messrs. Hodgkinson facing the street and the Grand Clothing Hall had reserved a space t erect a shop, if it were necessary, to face the street. He believed other owners would do the same, Mr. BARR= : That would mean back to back property which the byelaws would prohibit. There is only 110 feet depth from Bridge-street to the new street and that is not sufficient. Councillor PEMBERTON said he did not state that there was an agreement. He said the Council was told there was an agreement. The TowN CLERK: Nothing of the sort. Mr. GOUGH challenged the Town Clerk to produce a newspaper in which the transaction was reported. AtDEIZMAN BENNETT'S INDIGNATION. Alderman BENNETT, as chairman of the Street Improvement Committee, then addressed the inspector. He said that from the tone of some of the speeches, the inspector might have bean led to believe the committee had been engaged in a deep-laid plot to ride rough-shod over the ratepayers. Such a thought was never in the minds of the members. He and others believed that in this matter they had taken the public-spirited view rather than the parsimonious view. Whatever might have been said for or against the street in the early stages of the scheme, it seemed that when the street was half made it was utter folly to leave it in its present condi- tion. The Local Government Board had taken a much longer-sighted view of the question and he could not understand the opposition to the powers which the Board proposed to confer but which could hot have have been exercised without the consent of the Council. There was considerable ratable value in the property and the public would have gained by the improvement. He was absolutely certain that the day would come when the Council would ask for powers to carry the street through. The progress of the town could not be dominated in perpetuity by the small- minded opposition of the moment. He repudiated with scorn and contempt the charge that the Committee intended by some occalt method to give an advantage to Ntr. Garnett. They might have made a mistake, but they had been animated by a sincere deai re to promote the best interests of the public. NO ASPERSIONS. Mr. BA roan' said the opposition did not wish to cast aspersions. They were there in the public interest as much as Alderman Bennett was, and they wanted to guard against mistakes in the future. They did not say that anyone had benefftted pecuniarily in any way. The iNsrEcrort : I did not gather that from the opposition. , Mr. Ba.Rittrr : There is no imputation at all. Mr. PENDLEBUR.Y associated himself with these , remarks. If Alderman Bennett knew what the oaposi- , tion represented, he would probably be of a different opinion as to small-inindedness. There was no small- mindedness about it. Their interests were the town's interests, and the street was not needed. Mr. THORNTON said it was easy for Alderman Bennett to talk when he was not a property owner in the town. , Councillor Pemberton and Councillor Wilding, as , members of the Street improvement Committee,objected to Alderman Bennett's remarks. , Mr. J. E. RICHARDSON associated himself with the statement that there was no reflection on the committee. The opponents were tradesmen and large ratepayers in the town and he (the speaker) was a resident in the borough, which was more than Alderman Bennett could say. They could afford to treat his sneer at the pirsimonious ratepayers at its true value. The inquiry then closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Constable's Instinct.</head>
        <p>The Constable's Instinct.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Tednesdly Charles John Daniels, !h&amp;clough's-avenue, pleaded guilty to a charge of stealing 561 b. of lead, the property of George Howard, metal broker. It was stated that the previous night prisoner was seen by Police-constable Edwards carrying % a bag containing something heavy. The officer, not being satisfied with the explanation as to the contents, took Daniels into custody, and on ingu;ries being made it was ascertained that the lead had been stolen from Mr. Howard, by whom prisoner was formerly employed.—The magistrates imposed a fine Of 255. with the alternative of a month's imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Drinking Habit.</head>
        <p>The Drinking Habit.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Perhaps some of our young temperance friends will be interested in the following "punning points." That ale causes many ailings and beer brings many of the bier is well known. It may also be said that brandy brands the nose and rum creates many a rumpus. That wine makes people take a winding way home and cham- pagne causes real pain, is equally true. May we not also say that whiskey is the key which opens the door to many evils. Let us now examine the word "habit." If you take away the first lettet it leaves " abit." It you take away the second letter it still leaves a" bit," If you take away the third letter" it "remains. There- fore it is useless trying to leave off a bad habit in bits you must leave it off altogether.—Yours, &amp;c., NV. C.</p>
        <p>Temperance Legislation League. Sir,—The Temperance Legislation League is an embodiment in organised form of a phase of temperance opinion which has been too long inarticulate. Until very recent years, temperance effort has been largely confined, not to temperance people, but to total abstainers. Everyone should be ready and willing to pay tribute to the earnestness, devotion, and per- sistency shown in their efforts, to convince the nation that alcoholic beverages are an evil in every form and quantity. The position which the temperance question occupies to-day is largely due to the sacrifice and zeal of these workers. Such has been the zeal of total abstainers they have created the impression wittingly or unwittingly, amongst temperate men generally, that there is no room for them in temperance work,unless they are prepared to adopt total abstinence principles. Any temperance effort of such has been viewed by a large number of abstainers as insincere, or their action has been deemed to be inconsistent ; in either case it has been looked upon as ineffective. The total suppression of the liquor trade has been the goal of The Alliance, Good Templar, and other like associations, their stand- point is that the evil is altogether, or almost altogether, in the beverage itself, and that contributory causes such as bad housing, poverty,absence of amusement, the interest of the vendor in pushing the sale of drink, are of minor importances and their removal would have little or no effect as long as there are left facilities for drinking. In regard to poverty for example, this has been considered as almost entirely due to drunkenness, and little consideration given to finding how far drunkenness has been due. to poverty. On the other hand the thought represented by the Legislation League is that drunkenness, while immediately caused by drinking to excess, such excess is largely due to contributory causes. Even if we grant, for the sake of argument that drink is the primary cause of all the evil, and that total abstinence is the only remedy, it must be evident in-so- much as total abstainers only form about one-tenth of the electors, legislation from the total abstinance stand- point is out ofttlae question. If anything practical is to be achieved by law, such reform must be introduced as will carry the consent of fully half the electorate. The moderate proposals of the League are calculated to do this. They not only appeal to the one tenth who are abstaimrs, but they also appeal to the moderate 7--en who view with sorrow and concern the havoc which intemperance is making to the well-being of the people. And such an appeal is far more likely to achieve its purpose than a policy of local veto which introduces a curtailment of individual liberty which it is difficult for many of us to approve, and can only be considered at all in view of the great burdens placed on the corn- munity by the result of excessive drinking. It is probable however, that a very large number of the advocates of the League policy approve of Local Veto and would hail its adoption wherever it received the approval of a considerable majority of the inhabitants. But it is surely evident that it would only be adopted in very few towns in this country, it would certainly be inoperative in Warrington. And are we to be prevented from the aid which legislation could give to limiting the excesses due to drunkenness and removing unnecessary facilities until some far distant period, when through the slow action of the persuasion of total abstainers, consent might be secured for the suppression of the liquor trade ? Is it not far wiser to put before the electors a policy which will appeal to all who deals() to eradicate excess whether they are in favour of total abstinence or not? By holding up a policy of total suppression as the main object of temperance effort we compel 3 great number of the friends of teraperance, who are not prepared to approve of total suppression, to join the camp of the opponents to all reform, or we oblige them to assume a neutral position. If a wore moderate policy were adopted we would secure their moral support and active co-operation. It is along the line of least resistance and most assistance that we can hope to effect practical results. This is the line adopted in the policy of the Temperance Legislation League. It faces facts and deals with things as they are and does not persist in acting as though they were as we think they ought to be. There naay be objection taken to some of the pro- posals of the League from.a practical standpoint, but I claim that the main principles are the only ones which are likely to receive the assent of the majority of the lectors. How best to embody these principles in practice and legislation is a matter open to friendly discussion and ought not to engender bitter- ness between those who have the 331110 object in view. The League advocates that a municipality should have a large power in dealing with the sale of intoxi- cants within its area, and one method suggested for consideration of the municipality is that of handing over to a disinterested board the management of certain licensed houses should there be suitable parties willing to incur the responsibility. Suppose total abstainers were able to convince members of the League that such managemegt was likely to increase drunkenness. This would not prove that the principle of the League was unsound, but simply that this particular form of practical embodiment of the principle was at fault. I trust that the forthcoming meeting of the League, which is to be addressed by the Bishop of Liverpool and Mr. Henry Vivian, M.P., will be supported by all (whatever their opinions and habits) who long to see drunkenness and its terrible consequences removed from the land.</p>
        <p>CHARLES E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>Penketh, January 24th, 1907.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>DON'T DELAY IF YOU FEEL "OUT OF SORTS</head>
        <p>DON'T DELAY IF YOU FEEL "OUT OF SORTS</p>
        <p>Or if you have a friend whose constitution has been weakened, whether by overwork of brain or bo ly by exposure to the weather, by working long hour in, ill-ventilated rooms, by recent sickness, or by any cause tending to weaken the bodily powers ; in a word, if he is one who suffers from any form of complaint which may be the result of any of the above or similar causes; if, for instance, you can see that he is troubled with Indigestion, the bane of our Country and of modern civilization; or, if you think he has Low Spirits, that sure sign of some Nervous Disorder, bringing in its train a host of other complaints such as Melancholy, Lassitude Stc., or, perhaps, Neuralgia, and frequently, Sleeplessness, which of itself is sufficient to weaken the trongest constitution, and to make life a burden; or, if being free from these, he is regarded by his friends or himself as being of a delicate constitution, subject to Bronchitis, Coughs, Colds, a tendency to Consumption, or other Chest Affections, and if he, like thousands of others, failed to get relief from other remedies,tell him he Reed not be disheartened, that there is one Remedy whose virtues are so great, that it has succeeded where others have failed, and that there is hope he may be cured,as thousands of others have been cured before him by making a sensible and timely use of Gwilym Evans Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. If your friend then gives it a fair trial, he will be fully convinced, as others have been, that this is the Best Remedy of The Age. You or your friend may ask "What is Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters ?" It is a Preparation now extensively taken throughout the country by patients suffering from Debility, Nervousness, and General- Exhaustion, and if any value be attached to human testimony, the efficacy of this medicine has been suc- cessfully established. Its claims have been tested and proved by the medical proJtession and others, and corro- borated by the written testimonials of eminent men. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters contain not only as suitable quantity of Quinine in each dose, but the active principles of the following well-known herbs— Sarsaparilla, Saffron, Gentian, Lavender, Burdock, and Dandelion. The use of Quinine is well kown but it has never been satisfactorily combined with these preparations, until after overcoming considerable difficulties, the proprietor was able to secure a perfectly 'uniform preparation, corn hi fling Ili the essential properties of the above plants in therg greatest purity and concentration. It is now estab- lished as a family rnedicine,and is increasing in popular favour the more it is known and tested. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is a tonic " Pick-me-up " scientifically mixed in happy proportions. Sold everywhere in bottles, 2s. 9d. and 4s. Gd. each. Should any difficulty be experienced in procuring Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, it will be forwarded carriage free for the above prices by the Sole Pro- prietors: The Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. We particularly caution our readers against imitators, and impress upon hem the importance of always seeing that the name Gwilym Evans "is on the Label, Stamp, and Bottle without which none is genuine.</p>
        <p>A Montenegrin prieat who was shipwrecked in a small boat off the Cattano coast was refused shelter by the inhabitants because it was reported that he had com- mitted the sacrilege of photographing his daughter and her husband in church after the wedding ceremony. When a juror was sworn at an inquest at Kingston he Insisted that his name was George William and not George. " Do you object to George ?" the coroner asked. No sir," was the reply. " I ought not to. I kave beau christened three times."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>LIVELY SCENES.</head>
        <p>LIVELY SCENES.</p>
        <p>BANK MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>The Union Bank of Manchester Limited.</p>
        <p>The half-yearly meeting of the shareholders of the Union Bank of Manchester Limited was held at the Head Office of the bank York-street, Manchester, on Wednesday. Mr.G. C. Haworth, chairman of the bank, presided and he was supported by Messrs. Charles Behrens, M. Cababd, H. M. Addey, J. R. Oliver, Colonel C. H. France-Hayhurst, D.L., A. H. Bright, J.P., A. J. Fletcher, and P. Forrester, the general manager. Sir Anthony Marshall, the deputy- chairman, was unable to be present owing to iil health. Amongst other shareholders the following were present: —Messrs. T. Stuttard, D. Fogg, George Clare, G. Smart, C. B. Crossley, H. H. Humphreys, ' (3. Pfenninger, Thomas Mylchreest, Thomas Dutton C. T. Needham, E. Holt and G. I. Blake. It was moved by the Chairman that the report of the Directors for the half year ended the 31st December, 1906, be received and adoped, and entered in the book of proceedings. In doing so he said: The six months which have elapsed since we last met, on July 26th, have not been without interest. First and foremost there is the Bank Rate. All present would hail with satisfaction its reduction from 6 per cent. to 5 per cent, which took place last week. The following figures show the average rate during the last half-year, compared with the two previous corresponding half- years : December half, 1906, £4 12s. 7d. ; December half, 1905, £3 Os. fid. ; December half, 1901, £3. It is no less interesting to note that within a fortnight the Bank Rate was raised from 4 per cent. to 6 per cent., thus causing a great depression in the price of railway and other first-class securities. This rate has been characterised by one of the financial papers as a "war measure in times of peace." ' Previously, when this higb rate has ruled, some great financial crisis has been the cause, and the re-action upon trade has been somewhat serious. Unlike those times the 6 per cent, bank rate in 1906 was caused by the great increase in trade all over the world, and the enormous demands made upon us by the United States, South America, and Egypt. It was necessary to raise the bank rate in order to avoid a further drain upon our reserves. It is true that the trade of the country lhas been handicapped by the charge of so heavy a bank ' rate ; yet it is pleasing to observe that the activity of business did not greatly suffer, owing to the momentum in trade and the brightness of its prospects. With regard to the question of the gold reserves held by the Bank of England, it is understood that the Chan- cellor of the Exchequer has this matter under considera- tion, and the feeling seems to be that if any legislation is proposed, it will have a tendency to throw upon the joint stock banks a further responsibility. Touching upon the accounts for the half-year, I wish to call your attention to the profit and loss account. The aross 1 amount of profit is £80,395, being £8,691 in excess of the corresponding period of last year. The total profit, after adding the amount brought forkard from lasthalf- year, is £86,341. The expenses, viz., £34,777, are certainly in excess of the corresponding period of last year. This, however, may be attributed to the new branches which have been opened, which new branches successfully act as feeders to the main trunk line. You will find that our liabilities on current, deposit,and other accounts have almost reached five millions of money, which is unprecedented in the history of this bank, showing as it does an increase of nearly £600,000 over the corresponding period of last year. On the credit side of the balance sheet is cash in hand £716,034, which is well above the average figure of ptevious half- years. It will perhaps be a source of satisfiaction to all present to know that,for the first time in the history of the bank, the amount of bills on hand has touched a million of money. In conclusion, I may say that although the profits shown are substantial, the share- holders have also our assurance that liberal appropria- tions have been made for increasing the inside funds of the bank, and strengthening in various ways the bank's business.</p>
        <p>Mr. Fletcher said that he had very grelt pleasure in seconding the resolution which the Chairman had pro- posed. One item that afforded him distinct satisfaction was the increase in the deposits. While these had increased to such a large extent, it was satisfactory to note that the amount of advances to customers amounted to only half of the amount of the deposits. He had.looked at many banks' reports during the past few weeks, and this was one of the items he always examined, and he regarded it as an indication of strength that there was, in regard to the Union Bank, such a wide margin between the deposits by customers and the advances to customers.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Stuttard asked, in regard to the gold reserves, if the proposed interference by the Cban,-;ellor of the Exchequer would entail any extra expense to bankers. It appeared to him that the Bank of England itself ought to be the custodian of the visible gold reserves. The Chairman, replying, said that this appeared to be rather a matter of controversy at the present time, and</p>
        <p>it was not known what the Chancellor of the Exchequer's proposals were on the subject. The usual votes of thanks to the Chaim-in, nnd to the Ueneral Manager, Mr. Forrester, the various Branch Managers, and other oMcials of the Bank, for their services during the past half-year concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Parr's Bank, Limited. Our Gold Reserves.</head>
        <p>Parr's Bank, Limited. Our Gold Reserves.</p>
        <p>Mr. Cecil F. Parr, the chairman, presided over the forty-second annual meeting of the shareholders of Parr's Bank, Limited, held at Cannon-street Hotel, London, on Thursday. The attendance included Mr. Brodrick.</p>
        <p>The Chairman,lafter an expression of sympathy with the colony of Jamaica, said the bank had had a very good half-year, though bankers would have felt more comfortable had business been under more normal con- ditions. A 6 per cent. rate WAS not so wholly advan- tageous to banks as the ma ain the street was apt to imagine, for, although at such times they received a larger amount of interest in the aggregate, they also paid their depositors a relatively larger amount. As a matter of fact, they paid out in interest during the past half-year £50,000 more than they paid in the pre- ceding six months. It was a satisfactory feature that the present universal demand for money was almost entirely a perfectly legitimate one, for the enormous expansion of trade both explained and justified it. When a plentiful production coincided with high prices there was a double strain thrown on the world's supply of gold. Every additional bale of wool, every addi- tional ton of steel rails, required more money to pro- duce it, more meney to move it, and more money to finance it. Naturally, this relative scarcity of gold had again brought prominently forward the vexed question of gold reserve. He hoped that the discussion that had been raised might lead to the embodiment of some practical proposal, and to this end he felt sure that, if the president of the Bankers' Institute would propose the formation of a committee of exports to be wisely and widely chosen, such a proposal would meet with a general and willing acceptance from bankers. It was generally admitted now that the gold reserve of this country ought to be strengthened. Let the joint stock banks show their readiness to do their share. But they must act, and act promptly, ctherwise they would have only themselves to thank if the Government took the solution of the question out of their hands. More- over, they would then be in a better position to call upon both the Gevernment and the Bank of England to do their share also in the matter. Though every other country in the last ton years had materially increased its gold reserve,the Bank of England had added hardly a sovereign to its reserve, and this although both the production of gold in the world and the commercial commitments of the world trad greatly increased during that period. The position of the Government was still more astonishing. Not only did they decline to keep any metallic reserve against their two hunlrecl millions of deposits in the savings banks, but they had no reserve at all to make good their losses, excePt the long-suffering British tax- payer. It might surprise some to know that, according to their own statement, the Government actually made a loss last year of .C 90,000. Surely this was a "Case in which they were entitled to say, " Physician, heal thy- self" ; but direct legislation in this matter was, in his opinion, greatly to be deprecated. Not only did the the expert knowledge necessary to the proper solution of this problem reside chiefly in the bankers themselves but bankers had fully earned the right to have a preponderating voice in the settlement of the question that concerned , them so nearly. For the first time in our history the combined total of exports and imports had exceeded £1,000,000,000. The exports and imports of the precious metal had also beaten all previous records. No less than .C 43,000,400 of it had come—C:36,ooo,ooo of it from our own colonies ; but all except £3,400,000 had gone out again to other countries. The hopes held out twelve months ago of a profitable year bad been abundantly verified, and it would seem to be his privi- lege to foreshadow equal, if not greater, prosperity for 1807' He moved the adoption of the report, and the declaration of a dividend of 19 per cent.,together with a bonus of 1 per cent. Mr. E. W. Nix, deputy-chairman, seconded the motion, which was agreed to without discussion. Sir C. W. Freemantle, Sir D tvid Gamble, Bart., Mr. .1. Dun, Mr. E. W. Nix, Mr. E. Banbury, and Mr. R. Neill were re-elected directors, and thanks were voted to the directors and officers for their services.</p>
      </div>
