        <p>WARRINGTON CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY. Annual Meeting. The annual meeting of the Warrington Equitable Industrial Co-operative Society was held in the Co-operative Hall,Sankey-street,on Monday evening, Mr. Thomas Critchley presiding over a crowded attendance. DEATH OF AN OLD CO-OPERATOR. The CHAIRMAN said that since the last quarterly meeting, they had lost one of their oldest members, a thorough co-operator who had been connected with the Society for forty years—Mr. Samuel Dunbobbin. The Committee had passed a resolution of sympathy with the family. The members signified their approval of this course by standing. ILLNESS OF THE SECRETARY. The CHAIRMAN said he was sorry that Mr. Rathbone was unable to be present through illness. He suggested that the meeting should forward an expression of sympathy, with the hope that he would soon be restored to health. This was agreed to. THE PROGRESS OF THE SOCIETY. . The CHAIRMAN said the committee had great pleasure in presenting a very satisfactory balance sheet which they believed would be acceptable to the members. Their total sales for 1905 amounted to £151,031 18s. id., whereas last year they realized £157,677 Is.—an increase of £6,645 2s. 11d. There had been a corresponding increase in the amount of dividend paid which was £23,364 as against £22,402 in 1905. If they added to this the dividend paid on capital it gave a total of about £26,1100—a very substantial amount to be divided among 8,000 or 9,000 members. This for Warrington was a satisfactory state of things as far as the progress of Co-operation was concerned,but compared with some societies it was very insignificant. The Preston society had incressed its sales by £114,000 in three years. If the Warrington Society increased in the same ratio they would in a few years command the whole of the pur- chasing power of Warrington. BUSINESS OF THE QUARTER. TRANSFER OF THE BAKERY. One of the most important alterations agreed upon during the quarter by the committee was the transfer of the bakery from Sankey-street to Winwick-road—a work which is now in progress. A suggestion was made that an assistant secretary should be engaged but the feeling of the committee was that the management should remain as at present, but if extra clerical help was required Mr. Rathbone was empowered to engage it. Donations of £3 3s. to the Railway Men's Widows' and Orpha,ps' Association ; £1 Is. to the N.S.P.C.C. and £3 3s.to the Royal Eye Hospital,Manchester were agreed to. It was decided that application be made to the Co-operative Wholesale Society for a donation to the Infirmary Extension Fund. Mr.BorcHrErt supported the committee's proposal to transfer the bakery and said that for some time it had been felt that the congestion at the Sankey-street branch was £0 great that an alteration ought to be made. The minutes were passed. It was decided that in future the attendances of com- mittee men should be recorded in the report. THE BALANCE SHEET. The committee reported that the sales for the quarter amounted to £41,033 16s. Bd.; these were the highest recorded,and showed an increase of £1,171 ss. 4d. over the corresponding quarter of last year, and £4,052 12s. 6d. over last quarter. The profits after pro- viding for All liabilities, interest on capital, deprecia- tion, would allow a dividend of 3s. in the £ on members' purchases, with a balance of £64 4s. 10d. to be carried to next quarter. The balance sheet was adopted. THE VALUATION OF PROPERTIES. Mr. DON,NELLAN moved the appointment of a com- mittee to value the ploperties of the Society and report. To judge from the figures appearing in the balance sheet one would conclude that instead of being an acquisition some of the properties were like a mill- stone round their necks. He contended that nothing live the proper value was allowed, too much being deducted for depreciation. Mr.BoucHinn seconded and pointed out that the Sankey- street property which occupied such a central position was valued at only £950. The members of the Society were robbing themselves for the sake of those who world come after them. If they could increase the dividend it would be more satisfactory. He had also heard people say that they could not get things as cheaply at the stores as at other shops, and that ought not to be. Mr. Mow said the Society's goods were not dearer if they took the quality iato consideration. Mr. CROWTHER. asked if the committee had any intention of using the Bewsey-road property. The CHAIRMAN said they had a good deal on their hands at present and could not pretend to open a branch ' in that district. It would be used when the opportunity presented itself and in the meantime they were getting good interest on the investment. The following were elected a committee (with power to call in an expert, to value the properties :—Messrs E. England, F. W. Timperley, J. W. Boni:tiller, Rigby and Bowers. ELECTIONS. Mr. T. CRITCHLEY was unanimously re-elected president of the Society and Mr. J. G. Chappell was re-elected auditor. Messrs. H. Maybury and N. Cook retired from the committee and the vacancy created by the death of Mr. Dunbobbin had also to be filled. Twelve names were submitted and the voting resulted in the election of Messrs. Donnellan, Cook and T. Bowers.</p>
