        <p>LAND REFORM. Address to the Junior Liberals. There was a good attendance at the meeting of the Warrington Junior Liberal Association on Monday when the question of land reform was under considera- tion. The subject was introduced by Mr.Hyder,general secretary of the Land Nationalization Society. Mr. , H. W. Guest presided. Mr. HYDER said he took it that they were all agreed that some land reform was necessary and that the question with which they bad more particularly to deal was as to how that reform should be brolight about. (Hear, hear.) They would never be able to get rid of the monopoly on lani but as a. land nationalizer he said that monopoly, and in fact every monopoly should be public and not private, and the time -could come, he ' thought, when they would wonder that a private property in a thing so indispensable as land was per- mitted in the 9.oth centary. (Hear, hear.) They were all agreed that it was not right to buy and sell the bodies of men, but as a matter of fact, though there was no buying and selling, there was as much actual slavery to day as ever there was and so long as they permitted a private property in what was essential to man's existence so long would they have a form of property in man himself. .(Hear„ hear.) It was an established fact that the receipts of labour were less than the earnings of labour, and that was chiefly due to the owners of the land who gave nothing in return for the tribute which they exacted from labour. What did landlords give in return for land rents and mining royalties? Nothing. The payments were made by man simply for the right to live and to work-the minerals. That resulted in the improverishment of labour and, to a great extent, in the overcrowding of the labour market, and to prevent the very keen com- petition in the labour market and to raise wages they must stop the deductions from the earnings of labour to pay tribute to the idler.Hovv were landlords to be got rid of? Henry George advocated confiscation. That idea was not popular and would never be popular and when he advanced it Henry George was wrong, and therefore they, must compensate and not confiscate. It would perhaps be Rlged "If the land is ours by right why compensate ?" A great deal of money had been invested in land and to take the land away suddenly without compensation would be to take that money to which they had no right. Mr.Hyder proceeded to explain that he would not how- ever pay what the landlords asked. He would ask them to place a value upon their land and if that value was too hightwould tax them at that figuie, but if the price was reasonable he would purchase outright. (Hear, hear.) Mr. OCKEL argued that the purchase of the land by bond would not be easy as in a few years a great amount of money would be annually required to pay the intcrest on the enormous amount of capital required tor, the purchase of the land, and it would end in labour having to pay as much as it paid as the pnrent time. Mr. bHERBURN askea if the price to be paid for land would be the value of the land notwithstanding the fact that that valve had not been created by the land- lord but by the presence of an industrial community ? , Mr. T.BARNES thought land nationalization altogether outside the range of practical politics, and thought they should work to attain the end of peasant proprietor- ship. Mr. Morton, Mr. Gray and Mr. Guest also took part in the discussion. In replying, Mr. HYDER urged that if it paid aprivate individual to buy land it would pay the State to do the same, although in the purchase of land they would have to pay the present value. The interest on the bonds would not be a perpetual one as at intervals these would be purchased by the State and would then become only so much waste paper. (Hear, hear). A vote of thanks to the speaker concluded the proceedings.</p>
